\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 California measure brings rideshare drivers one step closer to unionizing\b0\par\par\uc2 Coral Murphy Marcos and agencies \par \uc2 820 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 October 2025\par 03:19 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  signs measure opening path to more than 800,000 drivers to bargain for wages and benefits\par \par \uc2 More than 800,000 ride-hailing drivers in California will soon be able to join a union and negotiate for higher wages and better benefits under a measure signed Friday by the governor, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/gavin-newsom"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gavin Newsom}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Supporters said the new law will open a path for the largest expansion of private-sector collective bargaining rights in the state\u8217\'20\'19s history. The legislation is a significant compromise in the years-long battle between labor unions and tech companies.\par \par \uc2 California is the second state where \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers can unionize as independent contractors; Massachusetts voters passed a ballot referendum in November allowing unionization, while drivers in Illinois and Minnesota are pushing for similar rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDonald Trump is holding the government hostage and stripping away worker protections,\u8221\'20\'1D Newsom said in a statement, referring to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbo.gov/system/files/2025-09/61773-Government-Shutdown.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated}}}\uc2  750,000 federal employees who are furloughed as a result of the first federal government {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/oct/01/us-government-shuts-down"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shutdown}}}\uc2  since 2018, with the administration planning to implement another sweeping wave of cuts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn California, we\u8217\'20\'19re doing the opposite: proving government can deliver \u8211\'20\'13 giving drivers the power to unionize while we continue our work to lower costs for families. That\u8217\'20\'19s the difference between chaos and competence,\u8221\'20\'1D he added.\par \par \uc2 The new law is part of an agreement made in September among Newsom, state lawmakers and the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2 , along with rideshare companies \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2 . In exchange, Newsom is expected to sign a measure supported by \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  to significantly cut the companies\u8217\'20\'19 insurance requirements for accidents caused by underinsured drivers.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  CEO David Risher said in September that the new insurance rates are expected to save the company $200m and could help reduce fares.\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  fares in California are consistently higher than in other parts of the US because of insurance requirements, the companies say. \uc2 Uber\uc2  has said that nearly one-third of every ride fare in the state goes toward paying for state-mandated insurance.\par \par \uc2 Labor unions and tech companies have fought for years over drivers\u8217\'20\'19 rights. In July of last year, the California supreme court ruled that app-based ride-hailing and delivery services like \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  can continue treating their drivers as independent contractors not entitled to benefits like overtime pay, paid sick leave and unemployment insurance.\par \par \uc2 A 2019 law mandated that \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  provide drivers with benefits, but voters reversed it at the ballot box in 2020 with a measure known as Proposition 22. \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  spent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/nov/04/california-election-voters-prop-22-uber-lyft"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than $200m}}}\uc2  in their efforts to bar app-based workers from being classified as traditional employees. Drivers and labor groups opposed Prop 22, saying it would allow companies to sidestep their obligations to provide benefits and standard minimum wages to their workers even as they make billions.\par \par \uc2 The collective bargaining measure now allows rideshare workers in California to join a union while still being classified as independent contractors and requires gig companies to bargain in good faith over issues such as driver deactivations, paid leave and earnings. The new law doesn\u8217\'20\'19t apply to drivers for delivery apps like \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTrump is gutting workers\u8217\'20\'19 fundamental right to come together and demand fair pay and treatment,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tia Orr, executive director of SEIU California. \u8220\'20\'1CBut here in California, we are sending a different message: when workers are empowered and valued, everyone wins. Shared prosperity starts with unions for all workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The insurance measure will reduce the coverage requirement for accidents caused by uninsured or underinsured drivers from $1m to $60,000 per individual and $300,000 per accident.\par \par \uc2 The two measures \u8220\'20\'1Ctogether represent a compromise that lowers costs for riders while creating stronger voices for drivers \u8211\'20\'13 demonstrating how industry, labor, and lawmakers can work together to deliver real solutions,\u8221\'20\'1D Ramona Prieto, head of public policy for California at \uc2 Uber\uc2 , said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The new law arrives as \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  continue negotiating a settlement with California and the cities of San Francisco, Los Angeles and San Diego, which sued the companies for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/26/uber-lyft-drivers-wage-theft-settlement-california"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 allegedly withholding wages}}}\uc2  from thousands of drivers before Proposition 22 took effect.\par \par \uc2 Rideshare Drivers United, a Los Angeles-based advocacy group of 20,000 drivers, said the collective bargaining law isn\u8217\'20\'19t strong enough to give workers a fair contract. The group wanted to require the companies to report their data on pay to the state.\par \par \uc2 New York City drivers\u8217\'20\'19 pay increased after the city started requiring the companies to report how much an average driver earns, the group said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDrivers really need the backing of the state to ensure that not only is a wage proposal actually going to help drivers, but that there is progress in drivers\u8217\'20\'19 pay over the years,\u8221\'20\'1D said Nicole Moore, president of Rideshare Drivers United.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251005ela50005n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
