\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Travel, national parks and housing: what does the \uc2 US government\uc2  shutdown mean for everyday people?\b0\par\par\uc2 Marina Dunbar \par \uc2 1,099 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2025\par 18:51 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Several thousand federal employees will be furloughed or must do unpaid work. Regular people will feel it differently\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 US government\uc2  shut down Wednesday at midnight, tens of thousands of federal workers have been furloughed (told not to work), while others must work without pay until Congress passes a budget. The shutdown will have a wide range of effects on government services and programs as well as the US economy.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what it means for everyday people:\par \par \uc2 Paychecks and federal workers\par \par \uc2 Who keeps working?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CExcepted\u8221\'20\'1D government workers, employees whose work is deemed essential to protecting life and property, such as military personnel, border agents, TSA screeners and \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, will remain at work without pay for as long as the shutdown continues. Some federal workers will continue to work with pay, particularly employees with the \uc2 Veterans Health Administration\uc2  and mail carriers, because the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://about.usps.com/newsroom/statements/092525-postal-service-not-affected-by-a-government-shutdown.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US Postal Service}}}\uc2  is an independent entity that generally relies on revenue from its products and services, not tax dollars.\par \par \uc2 Large portions of several agencies are furloughed, meaning they are not working or being payed because their jobs are not considered \u8220\'20\'1Cessential\u8221\'20\'1D to immediate government operations.\par \par \uc2 All federal employees who are furloughed or required to work without pay during a government shutdown will receive back pay, under a law passed in 2019. Government contractors, however, are not guaranteed back pay for time lost during a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 How many people are affected?\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of thousands of workers are impacted by the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 According to official government data, the impact of furloughs varies widely across agencies. The \uc2 EPA\uc2  was expected to be hit hardest, with 89% of its staff furloughed, although there has been confusion among the agency and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/01/us/politics/government-shutdown-epa-working.html?partner=slack&smid=sl-share"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 employees say}}}\uc2  they are still reporting to work without pay. The Department of Education follows closely behind at 87% and the Department of Commerce at 81%. The Department of Labor also sees a large share, with 76% of its employees sidelined, while the \uc2 Department of Housing and Urban Development\uc2  (HUD) has 71% furloughed.\par \par \uc2 At the state department, 62% of the workforce is out, while the Department of the Interior stands at 53%. Roughly half of agriculture employees (49%) are affected, along with 45% of civilian defense department staff and 41% of health and human services workers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  is only 5% furloughed, but most staff remains unpaid until funding returns. The VA estimates that 97% of its employees would continue to work during a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Travel and transportation\par \par \uc2 Will my flight still take off?\par \par \uc2 Yes. Air traffic controllers and TSA officers are still working, but without pay. Expect possible long lines or delays if staffing becomes strained.\par \par \uc2 Can I get a passport or visa?\par \par \uc2 Yes, passport and visa services continue, but delays are possible if the shutdown drags on.\par \par \uc2 Benefits and health\par \par \uc2 Will I still get\par \par \uc2 social\par \par \uc2 security, Medicare\par \par \uc2 or Medicaid?\par \par \uc2 Yes. Benefits continue, but customer service (like benefit verification or replacement cards) may be delayed.\par \par \uc2 Will I still get S\par \par \uc2 nap (food stamps) or WIC (special supplemental nutrition program for\par \par \uc2 women,\par \par \uc2 infants\par \par \uc2 and\par \par \uc2 children)?\par \par \uc2 Yes, these nutrition programs continue.\par \par \uc2 What about healthcare and research?\par \par \uc2 The National Institute of Health (\uc2 NIH\uc2 ) hospital stays open for current patients, but will not admit new ones unless medically necessary. \uc2 The US Food and Drug Administration (FDA)\uc2  will continue some inspections, but new drug/device approvals are expected to slow down. New research grants are frozen.\par \par \uc2 National parks\par \par \uc2 Can I visit a national park?\par \par \uc2 It depends. According to the interior department, \u8220\'20\'1Cpark roads, lookouts, trails, and open-air memorials will generally remain accessible to visitors\u8221\'20\'1D, but visitor services are limited due to reduced staffing.\par \par \uc2 Some campgrounds and remote sites may shut down entirely. If conditions worsen due to lack of workers, more parks could be closed. Tours of sites such as the US Capitol and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  will likely be suspended indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 Smithsonian museums\par \par \uc2 Are the Smithsonian museums open?\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Smithsonian Institution\uc2 , which includes museums, research centers, and the National zoo in Washington DC will stay open through at least Monday, 6 October using \u8220\'20\'1Cprior-year funds\u8221\'20\'1D, the institution shared in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.si.edu/newsdesk/releases/smithsonian-update"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 press release}}}\uc2 . It remains unclear whether they will remain open past that day should the shutdown continue, but updates will be posted on the website.\par \par \uc2 What about the Kennedy Center?\par \par \uc2 In a statement to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2025/09/30/nx-s1-5558189/government-shutdown-smithsonian-zoo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NPR}}}\uc2 , a spokesperson said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe Center\u8217\'20\'19s programming will not be impacted by a shutdown. Federal employees like plumbers will not be impacted either,\u8221\'20\'1D The spokesperson credited Trump\u8217\'20\'19s One Big Beautiful Bill Act, which included a $257m budget for repairs and restoration for the center, as the reason workers will not be affected.\par \par \uc2 Housing\par \par \uc2 How is the housing market affected?\par \par \uc2 The housing crisis is likely to intensify as the \uc2 Department of Housing and Urban Development\uc2  (HUD) ceases much of its work in housing fairness, according to Sharon Cornelissen, director of Housing at the \uc2 Consumer Federation of America\uc2  (CFA).\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis government shutdown will further paralyze HUD and cease the little fair housing work it was still doing,\u8221\'20\'1D Cornelissen said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://consumerfed.org/press_release/government-shutdown-cfas-impacts-on-consumers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThousands of home closings will grind to a halt \u8211\'20\'13 consumers in flood zones will be unable to buy or sell homes \u8211\'20\'13 as funding for the National Flood Insurance Program runs out.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She added: \u8220\'20\'1CBroad, affordable, and fair access to housing is impossible without a well-functioning government, but this administration has done nothing but undermine the essential housing programs and protections that people across the US rely on every day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Consumer protections\par \par \uc2 What about other implications for consumers?\par \par \uc2 Since the start of the Trump administration, watchdog agencies and regulators have faced major staffing cuts and financial constraints. Experts warn the shutdown could compound the government\u8217\'20\'19s ability to protect consumers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis shutdown is not just political theater, it\u8217\'20\'19s a continuation of the relentless assault on consumer protection,\u8221\'20\'1D said Erin Witte, director of consumer protection for the \uc2 Consumer Federation of America\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CBy halting the very agencies that safeguard families from predatory lenders, unfair fees and corporate abuse, the administration is abandoning Americans at the height of a widespread affordability crisis.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In summary\par \par \uc2 * You\u8217\'20\'19ll still get your social security and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 * Flights will keep going, but with unpaid and possibly limited staff.\par \par \uc2 * Most parks will remain open, but will probably be under-maintained.\par \par \uc2 * Smithsonian museums and the National zoo are open through at least 6 October.\par \par \uc2 * Federal workers are the hardest hit, withmany being unpaid or furloughed.\par \par \uc2 * Consumer protections, which have already been hit hard by cuts, are at risk of incapacity.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251002ela2002h1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
