\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US company hiring plans weakest since 2009; \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  creditors propose new rescue plan \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Julia Kollewe \par \uc2 3,167 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2025\par 13:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 UK companies\u8217\'20\'19 hiring plans also weaken; European shares hit record highThames Water creditors ask for up to 15 years\u8217\'20\'19 leniency from river pollution rules\par \par \uc2 2.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 Time to wrap up.\par \par \uc2 Stocks are rising on both sides of the Atlantic, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/oct/02/thames-water-creditors-rescue-plan-tesco-profit-markets-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-68de7f978f08f91f2eb5e461#block-68de7f978f08f91f2eb5e461"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hitting fresh record highs}}}\uc2 , despite the \uc2 US government\uc2  shutdown. The S&P 500 and the Nasdaq rose to new peaks at the open.\par \par \uc2 The pan-European Stoxx 600 rose by 0.7% to an all-time high, led by gains in Germany and France, with the Dax up 1.5% and the CAC 1.4% ahead. The FTSE 100 index in London is unchanged, after closing at record high yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  may not fully comply with rules on pollution of England\u8217\'20\'19s waterways for as long as 15 years, according to a new plan by creditors who are scrambling to avoid the utility being forced into government administration.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/tesco"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tesco}}}\uc2  has warned of an intensifying price battle in the run-up to Christmas\par \par \uc2 amid \u8220\'20\'1Cpressure on household budgets\u8221\'20\'1D but upped its full-year profit expectations by \u163\'00\'A3100m after it grabbed market share and drew in more shoppers by keeping a lid on inflation this summer.\par \par \uc2 Our other stories:Thank you for reading. We\u8217\'20\'19ll be back tomorrow. Take care! \u8211\'20\'13 JK\par \par \uc2 2.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 Returning to our earlier story:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/tesco"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tesco}}}\uc2  has warned of an intensifying price battle in the run-up to Christmas amid \u8220\'20\'1Cpressure on household budgets\u8221\'20\'1D but upped its full-year profit expectations by \u163\'00\'A3100m after it grabbed market share and drew in more shoppers by keeping a lid on inflation this summer.\par \par \uc2 The UK\u8217\'20\'19s biggest retailer said it expected to make annual profits of up to \u163\'00\'A33.1bn, up from a previous maximum of \u163\'00\'A33bn, as it said efforts to cut the price of 6,500 items by an average 9% had \u8220\'20\'1Cworked better\u8221\'20\'1D than it predicted. The company said its rate of inflation was \u8220\'20\'1Cwell behind\u8221\'20\'1D the latest headline rate for groceries of 4.9% set out by the analysts Worldpanel, formerly known as Kantar.\par \par \uc2 Referring to comments by \uc2 Asda\uc2  in March that it would invest {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/mar/14/asda-cut-prices-battle-drop-sales-market-share"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Ca pretty significant war chest\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  in cutting prices, \uc2 Ken Murphy\uc2 , the chief executive of \uc2 Tesco\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 2.41pm BST\par \par \uc2 Wall Street indices hit record highs at the open\par \par \uc2 Shares are up on Wall Street, taking the benchmark S&P 500 and the tech-heavy Nasdaq to fresh all-time highs.\par \par \uc2 Investors shrugged off the \uc2 US government\uc2  shutdown, and took heart from weaker jobs data that have cemented expectations of two more interest cuts this year.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 rose by 20 points or 0.3% to 6,731 while the Nasdaq Composite climbed by 130 points, or 0.6%, to 22,885.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, shares are also rallying, boosted by chipmakers and healthcare stocks, with the pan-European Stoxx 600 hitting a new record.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 2.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  beats forecasts for Q3 deliveries\par \par \uc2 Just in: \uc2 Tesla\uc2  has smashed Wall Street expectations for car deliveries in the last quarter.\par \par \uc2 The electric carmaker has reported it delivered 497,099 vehicles in the third quarter, up 7.4% from 462,890 a year earlier. Analysts had expected about 443,919 vehicles for the July\u8211\'20\'13September period.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  reported:\par \par \uc2 Those record deliveries comes despite persistently weaker sales in the \uc2 European Union\uc2  this year, where some buyers appear to have been put off by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/jun/05/tesla-share-drop-trump-musk-feud"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elon Musk\u8217\'20\'19s support for Donald Trump, before the two men fell out earlier this year.}}}\par \par \uc2 Analysts, however, are expecting a sales slump in the October-December period following the unusual sales boost in the latest quarter, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 1.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  may not fully comply with rules on pollution of England\u8217\'20\'19s waterways for as long as 15 years, according to a new plan by creditors who are scrambling to avoid the utility being forced into government administration.\par \par \uc2 The creditors who in effect own \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  have said they will commit to paying {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/aug/27/thames-water-agrees-payment-plan-for-sewage-and-dividend-fines"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fines for pollution}}}\uc2 , as well as writing off more of their loans and investing more in the company, in new proposals published on Thursday ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/oct/02/thames-water-creditors-rescue-plan-tesco-profit-markets-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-68de13688f08694a84a03a3a#block-68de13688f08694a84a03a3a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 see opening blogpost for details}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 However, the creditors also said that \u8220\'20\'1Ca full return to legal, regulatory and environmental compliance\u8221\'20\'1D under their plan would not be completed until at least the 2035-2040 period, raising the prospect of sewage levels above legal limits in some places for at least a decade.\par \par \uc2 They will argue for further leniency on fines from the regulator, \uc2 Ofwat\uc2 , during that period, and that it will be impossible for the company to make upgrades across London and south-east England more quickly because of the scale of the work needed after years of neglect.\par \par \uc2 More here:\par \par \uc2 12.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 US company hiring plans weakest since 2009\par \par \uc2 Newflash: US companies have been cutting back on hiring plans too, with workers facing a recruitment slowdown on both sides of the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 US companies\u8217\'20\'19 hiring plans have deteriorated to their lowest level since the aftermath of the financial crisis, new data from recruitment and coaching firm \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2  shows.\par \par \uc2 They have found that so far this year, US employers have announced plans to create almost 205,000 jobs, which is 58% less than the 483,590 announced hiring plans in the first nine months of 2024.\par \par \uc2 Most of this drop is due to a low number of seasonal hiring announcements, indicating that US companies are reluctant to take on more staff in the current economic environment.\par \par \uc2 Andy\par \par \uc2 Challenger, senior vice president and labor expert for \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2 , says:\par \par \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2  also report that US companies have announced 946,426 job cuts, the highest year-to-date figure since 2020 when 2,082,262 were announced.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s very likely job cut plans are going to surpass a million for the first time since 2020 and for the ninth time in our series,\u8221\'20\'1D AndyChallenger warns.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 12.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 Another day, another rising gold price.\par \par \uc2 The spot price of gold is up 0.5% today at $3,883 per ounce, close to the record high of $3,895 set yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Many of these factors may well remain relevant in Q4, keeping the gold outlook positive.\par \par \uc2 Demand for safe-haven assets, geopolitical tensions, worries about the US economy and a weakening dollar have all pushed up gold \u8211\'20\'13 up over 45% so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Fawad Razaqzada\uc2 , market analyst at City Index and FOREX.com, reckons it has further to rise, putting the $4,000/oz mark in sight:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 11.45am BST\par \par \uc2 On the pharma front, Reuters has this.\par \par \uc2 Pharmaceutical executives are getting near-daily calls from staff at the White House \u8211\'20\'13 including chief of staff Susie Wiles, and senior figures at agencies like health & human services and the US commerce department, two sources told the news agency.\par \par \uc2 The drugmaker \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2  was asked to produce more insulin; \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  was asked to produce more of its top-selling cancer drug Ibrance and its cholesterol drug Lipitor; and London-based \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  to consider a new headquarters in the US, according to two sources.\par \par \uc2 11.29am BST\par \par \uc2 Eurozone unemployment rate ticks up to 6.3%\par \par \uc2 In the eurozone, the unemployment rate ticked up last month.\par \par \uc2 The jobless rate increased from 6.2% in July to 6.3% in August, according to data from \uc2 Eurostat\uc2 , the EU\u8217\'20\'19s statistical office.\par \par \uc2 While the number of unemployed people was 11,000 higher than in July, it was 15,000 lower than in August last year, and 136,000 lower than in June. That puts this uptick into perspective.\par \par \uc2 Bert Colijn\uc2 , chief economist, Netherlands at ING, said:\par \par \uc2 10.47am BST\par \par \uc2 UK firms' hiring plans weakest since 2020, BOE survey says\par \par \uc2 UK companies\u8217\'20\'19 hiring plans are the weakest since 2020, according to a \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  survey.\par \par \uc2 The survey showed that in the three months to September, businesses expected to keep employment steady over the next 12 months. It is the first time since the three months to November 2020 that firms did not intend to take on more staff.\par \par \uc2 Policymakers at the central bank hope that a weaker jobs market will bring down wage growth and help lower inflation, which it expects to hit 4% in September, double its target and up from 3.8% in August. Food prices in particular have remained high.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 10.40am BST\par \par \uc2 An auction of \u163\'00\'A34.5bn of UK 10-year government bonds this morning attracted the weakest demand since May \u8211\'20\'13 despite offering the highest 10-year yield since the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 Investors submitted bids worth 2.78 times the amount on offer of the 4.75% October 2035 gilt, the lowest ratio since an auction on 21 May.\par \par \uc2 The bond sold at an average yield of 4.769%, the highest for a 10-year gilt since an auction in January.\par \par \uc2 In financial markets, gilt prices were little changed, leaving the yield on the 10-year benchmark gilt flat at 4.71%.\par \par \uc2 10.35am BST\par \par \uc2 Joshua Mahony, chief markets analyst at Scope Markets, has looked at the financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The dollar continues to fall, down by 0.15% against a basket of major currencies, taking it close to a one-week low. Poor jobs data yesterday (the ADP national employment report) boosted expectations that the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  will cut interest rates twice more this year.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.54am BST\par \par \uc2 10.27am BST\par \par \uc2 Perky Maxwell House viral ad takes on housing crisis as \u8216\'20\'18Maxwell Apartment\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Housing in the US has become so unaffordable that a coffee company has based a viral marketing campaign on the idea that almost nobody can afford to buy a house.\par \par \uc2 Maxwell House coffee, a 133-year-old brand, recently launched a marketing campaign rebranding themselves as \u8220\'20\'1CMaxwell Apartment Coffee\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMaxwell House? In this economy?\u8221\'20\'1D a narrator asks in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.amazon.com/stores/MaxwellHouse/page/7F25DEA2-A0EF-4167-8439-CD5AD0B61BFC?is_byline_deeplink=true&deeplink=92F2B2D1-1CDD-4411-B775-C3C6678AF11F&redirect_store_id=7F25DEA2-A0EF-4167-8439-CD5AD0B61BFC&lp_asin=B0CB1R8MHC&ref_=ast_bln"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 video ad}}}\uc2 , promising that Maxwell Apartment is \u8220\'20\'1Cthe same affordable coffee you love, now with an even more affordable name\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The company is also offering a year\u8217\'20\'19s supply of its coffee for \u8220\'20\'1Cunder $40\u8221\'20\'1D on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , though supplies of this \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.kraftheinzcompany.com/press-releases-details/2025/For-the-First-Time-in-133-Years-Maxwell-House-Rebrands-as-Maxwell-Apartment/default.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 12-month lease}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D of pre-ground coffee are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.amazon.com/stores/page/230AD4C4-241D-435D-9AF4-CB8886AE93E1?ingress=2&lp_context_asin=B0CB1R8MHC&visitId=74f5fb0e-44b6-4a60-9b60-51a0536622e4&ref_=ast_bln"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 apparently limited}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 10.26am BST\par \par \uc2 Europe\u8217\'20\'19s air safety at risk amid cost-cutting and staff pressures, study warns\par \par \uc2 Pilots and cabin crew at European airlines feel increasingly under pressure to work long hours and hide signs of tiredness at the expense of safety, according to a major study.\par \par \uc2 Cost cutting and profit chasing at airlines has \u8220\'20\'1Csystemically weakened\u8221\'20\'1D safety, and many exhausted employees feel too intimidated to challenge management decisions, the research by Ghent University in Belgium found.\par \par \uc2 The study, which involved 6,900 workers, also found concerns among cabin crew who reported feeling pushed into making onboard sales of perfumes and alcohol, presenting a conflict with their role in keeping passengers safe and well.\par \par \uc2 The report found that the Covid pandemic had accelerated a decline in working conditions.\par \par \uc2 Researchers said a generation of senior pilots had left the industry, replaced by younger, cheaper and more flexible workers who were more likely to accept precarious contracts that weaken their ability to uphold standards.\par \par \uc2 10.17am BST\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s our full story on \uc2 Thames Water\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 The creditors in control of \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  have said they will commit to paying {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/aug/27/thames-water-agrees-payment-plan-for-sewage-and-dividend-fines"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fines for pollution}}}\uc2 , as well as writing off more of their loans and investing more in the company, in new proposals to try to avoid the utility being forced into government administration.\par \par \uc2 The controlling group of financial institutions, under the new London & Valley Water holding company, has been locked in talks for months with the regulator \uc2 Ofwat\uc2  since May over acceptable terms for the hugely complex restructuring of Britain\u8217\'20\'19s biggest water company.\par \par \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  has been crippled by huge debts built up over two decades by owners who have been criticised for paying out dividends without investing enough in its leaking pipes and malfunctioning treatment works.\par \par \uc2 The new plan, which is not yet legally binding, is aimed at finding a way forward that is acceptable to \uc2 Ofwat\uc2  and to the Labour government, including the newly installed environment secretary, Emma Reynolds.\par \par \uc2 The alternative to a creditor-led turnaround plan {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jun/30/what-would-happen-if-thames-water-is-temporarily-renationalised"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 is a special administration regime (SAR)}}}\uc2 , under which the water company would come under temporary government control to impose debt write-offs and find a buyer. The government has been keen to avoid SAR, claiming that it would cost too much \u8211\'20\'13 although any costs would be recouped in the eventual sale \u8211\'20\'13 and fearing calls for permanent nationalisation from MPs.\par \par \uc2 The creditors are also desperate to avoid SAR, as it would probably result in steeper debt write-offs. London & Valley Water said on Thursday that the investors would write off about \u163\'00\'A34bn of their loans, compared with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jun/10/thames-water-creditors-offer-up-5bn-as-part-of-emergency-turnaround-plan"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about \u163\'00\'A33.2bn offered in May}}}\uc2 . Senior sources at \uc2 Ofwat\uc2  had previously expressed concerns that the level of debt write-off proposed by the creditors was not sufficient. Junior creditors will have their entire \u163\'00\'A31bn debt written off, as before.\par \par \uc2 10.00am BST\par \par \uc2 European shares hit record high, lifted by chipmakers, healthcare stocks\par \par \uc2 Despite {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/oct/01/jd-vance-government-shutdown-layoffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the US government shutdown}}}\uc2 , stocks have been hitting new all-time highs on the back of a rally in chip-related companies and healthcare shares.\par \par \uc2 The pan-European Stoxx 600 index rose by 0.7% to a new intra-day high of 568.5 points, with Germany and France leading gains, with the Dax and the CAC both more than 1% ahead.\par \par \uc2 Technology stocks rose by nearly 2.4%, tracking gains on Wall Street, and after South Korea\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://openai.com/index/samsung-and-sk-join-stargate/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Samsung Electronics}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://openai.com/index/samsung-and-sk-join-stargate/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  and }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://openai.com/index/samsung-and-sk-join-stargate/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 SK Hynix}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://openai.com/index/samsung-and-sk-join-stargate/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  signed letters of intent}}}\uc2  to supply memory ships for \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s data centres.\par \par \uc2 Dutch chipmakers ASML and \uc2 ASM International\uc2  both jumped by more than 4%.\par \par \uc2 Healthcare stocks rose by as much as 1%, extending yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s rally, after \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s deal with the \uc2 US government\uc2  raised hopes that it could serve as a model for other drugs companies. The New York-based firm agreed to cut its medicine prices by up to 85% and to sell directly to the American public, and was given a three-year grace period from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s threatened 100% tariffs on branded drugs.\par \par \uc2 Shares in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/oct/01/uk-house-prices-return-to-growth-us-dollar-shutdown-manufacturing-growth-business-live-news"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AstraZeneca}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/oct/01/uk-house-prices-return-to-growth-us-dollar-shutdown-manufacturing-growth-business-live-news"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 , Britain\u8217\'20\'19s biggest drugmaker, surged by more than 11%}}}\uc2  yesterday, lifting its market value to over \u163\'00\'A3192bn. That meant it overtook \uc2 HSBC\uc2  again, and reclaimed the crown as the most valuable company listed in London. Today, \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  shares are up by 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 index in London is up slightly at 9,451. It closed at a new peak of 9,446 points yesterday, extending its gains for this year to around 15%.\par \par \uc2 Matt Britzman, senior equity analyst at \uc2 Hargreaves Lansdown\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.31am BST\par \par \uc2 8.56am BST\par \par \uc2 Another water company, \uc2 Pennon Group\uc2 , has appointed \uc2 Affinity Water\uc2  boss \uc2 Keith Haslett\uc2  as its new chief executive, bringing in an industry veteran to steer the company through its transformation.\par \par \uc2 Haslett will replace \uc2 Susan Davy\uc2 , who has led \uc2 Pennon\uc2  since 2020.\par \par \uc2 Haslett, a chartered civil engineer, has run \uc2 Affinity\uc2  since January 2023 and also held senior executive roles at Northumbrian Water Group and United Utilities, where he led large-scale water and wastewater operations.\par \par \uc2 Pennon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s South West Water{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jul/10/south-west-water-allowed-to-invest-24m-rather-than-pay-19m-fine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 received a \u163\'00\'A324m penalty from Ofwat in July}}}\uc2  for illegal sewage discharges into the environment from its wastewater treatment works. The industry as a whole is struggling with high debts and pollution, and is under pressure to invest more in infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 9.01am BST\par \par \uc2 8.21am BST\par \par \uc2 The consortium of investors, including Aberdeen Investments, the US hedge funds Elliott and Silverpoint Capital and California-based \uc2 PIMCO\uc2 , has submitted its proposal to the water regulator \uc2 Ofwat\uc2  today.\par \par \uc2 Ofwat\uc2  will now assess the proposal, the group said,\par \par \uc2 For a year and a half, Thames has been at the heart of a scandal in Britain\u8217\'20\'19s water industry, with the company fined more than \u163\'00\'A3100m for sewage spills while its debts of more than \u163\'00\'A320bn have left it teetering on the brink of financial collapse.\par \par \uc2 7.57am BST\par \par \uc2 Introduction: \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  creditors propose new rescue plan; \uc2 Tesco\uc2  ups profit outlook despite pressure on household budgets\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 Thames Water\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s creditors have put forward a new rescue plan with \u163\'00\'A35.4bn funding for the beleaguered water company, under which outstanding fines for sewage spills will be paid and no dividends will be paid to shareholders.\par \par \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  is labouring under huge debts built up over two decades by owners who have been criticised for paying out dividends without investing enough in its leaking pipes and malfunctioning treatment works.\par \par \uc2 A consortium of investors who hold much of the company\u8217\'20\'19s debts, called London & Valley Water, have been locked in negotiations with the regulator \uc2 Ofwat\uc2  for months. The group said \u163\'00\'A312.5bn of debt will be written off, in what is expected to be the largest-ever loss on a UK infrastructure investment.\par \par \uc2 Within this, class A investors will write off \u163\'00\'A34bn of their loans \u8211\'20\'13 a quarter of all class A debt \u8211\'20\'13 compared with \u163\'00\'A33.2bn offered in May.\par \par \uc2 The creditors also said they would invest about \u163\'00\'A3150m more in new equity capital than previously suggested, taking the total to \u163\'00\'A33.15bn.\par \par \uc2 Existing equity, previously valued at \u163\'00\'A35bn, will be cancelled. No dividends will be paid during the turnaround period. The plan, which is not yet legally binding, includes \u163\'00\'A35.4bn in committed capital, which will support \u163\'00\'A320.5bn of strategically targeted investment and operational expenditure over the next five years.\par \par \uc2 The group said the new plan is \u8220\'20\'1Cthe fastest and most reliable route\u8221\'20\'1D to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/sep/03/creditors-set-out-20bn-plan-to-save-thames-water"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 turn Thames around}}}\uc2 , to avoid putting the UK\u8217\'20\'19s largest water company, which serves 16 million consumers, into a form of temporary nationalisation.\par \par \uc2 Mike McTighe\uc2 , the proposed future chair of Thames under the terms of the plan, said:\par \par \uc2 Tesco\uc2 , Britain\u8217\'20\'19s biggest retailer, has raised its profit outlook after sales were boosted by an unusually hot summer, despite pressure on household budgets.\par \par \uc2 Tesco\uc2  said it had won market share from rivals, but highlighted rising competition and \u8220\'20\'1Ccontinued pressure on household budgets\u8221\'20\'1D. It said it had reduced prices on 6,500 products, with an average reduction of 9%.\par \par \uc2 The supermarket chain\u8217\'20\'19s UK like-for-like sales rose by 4.9% in the first half of the year, while across the group like-for-like sales (at outlets open at least a year) climbed by 4.3%. Overall revenues rose by 5.1% to \u163\'00\'A333.1bn. However, profit before tax fell by 6.3% to \u163\'00\'A31.3bn.\par \par \uc2 Tesco\uc2  now expects to make an adjusted operating profit of between \u163\'00\'A32.9bn and \u163\'00\'A33.1bn this year, up from between \u163\'00\'A32.7bn and \u163\'00\'A33bn previously.\par \par \uc2 Ken Murphy\uc2 , the chief executive, said:\par \par \uc2 The Agenda\par