\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Government shutdown leaves federal workers \u8216\'20\'18on edge\u8217\'20\'19 as Trump eyes more job cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,004 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2025\par 01:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employees wonder if the shutdown will leave them out of work temporarily or set stage for permanent termination\par \par \uc2 An {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbo.gov/system/files/2025-09/61773-Government-Shutdown.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated}}}\uc2  750,000 federal employees are furloughed as a result of the first federal government {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/oct/01/us-government-shuts-down"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shutdown}}}\uc2  since 2018.\par \par \uc2 Many have been \u8220\'20\'1Con edge for months\u8221\'20\'1D, according to James Kirwan, who works as a labor attorney inside a government agency. Now the Trump administration is threatening to implement another sweeping wave of cuts to their ranks.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-federal-government-shutdown-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Shutdowns }}}\uc2 cause widespread disruption \u8211\'20\'13 both to government services and the lives and livelihoods of the staff that typically work to deliver them.\par \par \uc2 Claire Babineaux-Fontenot, CEO of the \uc2 Feeding America\uc2  network of food banks, warned \u8220\'20\'1Cmany people\u8221\'20\'1D in the US are \u8220\'20\'1Ca single missed paycheck\u8221\'20\'1D away from needing to turn to a food bank for support. \u8220\'20\'1CA prolonged shutdown will deepen the strain, and more families will seek help at a time when food banks are already stretched due to sustained high need,\u8221\'20\'1D she said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The shutdown \u8220\'20\'1Churts federal workers in many ways\u8221\'20\'1D, Kirwan, who organizes with the Federal Unionists Network, said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe want to be working. We want to be serving the public.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 During a shutdown, many federal workers, especially those earning less than $100,000 annually, are forced to dip into savings, rack up credit-card debt, or take out loans to survive until they receive back pay when the government reopens, Kirwan said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t even go into all the emotional and psychological harm too. We are federal workers because we care deeply about the federal government, and we care deeply about serving our constituents and the public,\u8221\'20\'1D he added. \u8220\'20\'1CTo be kept from our ability to serve the American public, it hurts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd then\u8221\'20\'1D, Kirwan continued, \u8220\'20\'1Cyou add in the fear that we might be fired\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration has faced criticism for ordering federal agencies to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/25/trump-administration-government-shutdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 draw up plans for mass-scale firings}}}\uc2  in the event of a government shutdown. The US president {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/oct/01/trump-administration-news-updates-today-latest"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suggested}}}\uc2  he would use the pause to \u8220\'20\'1Cget rid of a lot of things we didn\u8217\'20\'19t want\u8221\'20\'1D earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 Trump has already overseen {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/28/us-mass-resignation-federal-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the largest mass resignation in US history}}}\uc2  this week, with more than 100,000 federal workers set to formally quit as part of the latest wave of his administration\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ourpublicservice.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Deferred-Resignation-Program-Guide-FedSupport-Partnership-for-Public-Service.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deferred resignation program}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now workers are left wondering if this shutdown will leave them out of work temporarily, or set the stage for their permanent termination.\par \par \uc2 In a memo seen by the Guardian, the \uc2 US Patent and Trademark Office (USPTO)\uc2  announced a \u8220\'20\'1Creduction in force\u8221\'20\'1D (RIF) program of layoffs at the agency on Wednesday, the first reported agency to do so.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CApproximately one percent of the USPTO workforce will be affected by this RIF,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote Valencia Martin Wallace, acting commissioner for the USPTO, wrote to employees at the agency. The USPTO did not respond to a request for comment\par \par \uc2 At the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  (\uc2 EPA\uc2 ), only about 1,700 out of 15,000 workers are set to keep working during the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe workers who do the majority of the frontline work will be furloughed and potentially RIF\u8217\'20\'19ed,\u8221\'20\'1D said Justin Chen, an environmental engineer and president of the \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees (AFGE)\uc2  Council 238, which represents thousands of \uc2 EPA\uc2  workers. \u8220\'20\'1CWe may be recalled for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epa.gov/emergency-response"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 emergencies}}}\uc2 , but the workers who do the work which prevents those emergencies won\u8217\'20\'19t be done.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even workers who are still working, for the time being, are bracing for possible layoffs amid the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe still are yet again finding, as federal employees, a level of concern as our livelihoods being used as a pawn, being used in this showdown between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the oligarchs against the working class,\u8221\'20\'1D said Colin Smalley, who works for the US army corps of engineers in Chicago, and represents 300 workers as president of International Federation of Professional and Technical Engineers Local 777. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are trying to extract these big changes on the backs of the public services federal workers provide and our communities run on.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration estimates it will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2025/08/22/us/politics/trump-federal-workers.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cut}}}\uc2  the federal workforce by about 300,000 workers by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Much of these cuts have yet to be reflected in monthly jobs report data, which will not be released due to the shutdown. As of August, federal employment is down {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 97,000 jobs}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The last shutdown started in December 2018 and lasted 35 days, until January 2019, costing the US economy an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://carbajal.house.gov/shutdown/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated $11bn}}}\uc2 , according to the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kirwan suggested the federal government, since Trump took office, has already effectively been operating under a partial shutdown, with about one-eighth of the federal workforce removed, several agencies dismantled and programs eliminated.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe do not want a shutdown, but we know that the most important thing is standing up against further harms through our programs,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CMore and more harm has been happening every single month to programs that are critical and that tens of millions of Americans rely upon.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI hope that the budget that is passed will come with assurances that will protect against further harms to critical programs, because we serve the public. Our government serves the public, and we need to make sure that there are no further harms to those programs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Labor unions {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://democracyforward.org/updates/trump-administrations-plan-for-mass-firing-of-federal-workers-during-government-shutdown-violates-law-unions-say/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed }}}\uc2 a lawsuit on Tuesday over the firing threats, alleging the move is unlawful and an effort to punish workers and pressure Congress.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnnouncing plans to fire potentially tens of thousands of federal employees simply because Congress and the administration are at odds on funding the government past the end of the fiscal year is not only illegal \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s immoral and unconscionable,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Everett Kelley\uc2 , president of the \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2 , in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CFederal employees dedicate their careers to public service \u8211\'20\'13 more than a third are military veterans \u8211\'20\'13 and the contempt being shown them by this administration is appalling.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251001ela1005k1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
