\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Jobs to go at Lincolnshire oil refinery; UK economy grew faster than thought under Labour \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Julia Kollewe (now) and Graeme Wearden (earlier) \par \uc2 4,318 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 September 2025\par 14:27 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Insolvency firm running Lindsey oil refinery announces job losses after collapse of parent company Prax Group\par \par \uc2 3.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, the benchmark S&P 500 has slipped by 0.17%, but is on track for its best third-quarter performance since 2020, while the tech-heavy Nasdaq fell by 0.27% and the Dow Jones industrial average lost 0.6%.\par \par \uc2 The UK\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE 100 index is 0.4% higher while other major European markets have also moved cautiously higher, with the exception of the French bourse.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will announce a deal with the New York-based drugmaker \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  to voluntarily sell the company\u8217\'20\'19s medications through Medicaid at lower prices, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reported, citing sources.\par \par \uc2 Almost a third of workers at the Prax Lindsey oil refinery in north Lincolnshire, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jun/30/lindsey-oil-refinery-prax-group-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 collapsed into administration this summer}}}\uc2 , will lose their jobs at the end of October.\par \par \uc2 The Insolvency Service said the decision to make 125 roles redundant, with 255 people remaining at the site, \u8220\'20\'1Cwas not taken lightly\u8221\'20\'1D and follows a thorough review of \u8220\'20\'1Call aspects of the business, following its insolvency\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson said:\par \par \uc2 The job losses emerged weeks after a court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/sep/04/uk-court-freezes-150m-of-assets-of-collapsed-prax-lindsey-oil-refinery-owner"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 froze \u163\'00\'A3150m of assets}}}\uc2  belonging to the owner of the refinery, \uc2 Winston Soosaipillai\uc2  , whose whereabouts have been a unknown since the plant\u8217\'20\'19s sudden financial collapse.\par \par \uc2 Soosaipillai, who is known by his middle names \uc2 Sanjeev Kumar\uc2  , is being sued by administrators for breach of his duties as a director, and the government has called for an investigation into the conduct of bosses at the refinery.\par \par \uc2 Our other main stories:Thank you for reading. We\u8217\'20\'19ll be back tomorrow. Bye! \u8211\'20\'13 JK\par \par \uc2 3.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 Spotify\uc2  boss steps down after 19 years\par \par \uc2 The boss of \uc2 Spotify\uc2  , \uc2 Daniel Ek\uc2  , is stepping down after 19 years to become executive chair, in a shake-up at the music streaming company that will split his position between two top executives.\par \par \uc2 The company said that \uc2 Alex Norstr\u246\'00\'F6m\uc2  and \uc2 Gustav S\u246\'00\'F6derstr\u246\'00\'F6m\uc2  will take over as co-chief executives at the start of next year.\par \par \uc2 Ek, who co-founded \uc2 Spotify\uc2  in 2006, said that he had already \u8220\'20\'1Cturned over a large part of the day-to-day management and strategic direction of \uc2 Spotify\uc2  to Alex and Gustav\u8221\'20\'1D, adding that their promotion \u8220\'20\'1Csimply matches titles to how we already operate\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 3.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  takeover of \uc2 Deliveroo\uc2  approved by high court judge\par \par \uc2 Deliveroo\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s \u163\'00\'A32.9bn takeover by American firm \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  has been approved by a judge at the High Court.\par \par \uc2 The London-based food delivery company in May agreed a{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/apr/28/deliveroo-shares-surge-doordash-takeover-move"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  \u163\'00\'A32.9bn takeover by its US rival DoorDash}}}\uc2  that will result in a near-\u163\'00\'A366m payday for its staff. The San Francisco-based company will pay 180p a share in cash for the London-listed \uc2 Deliveroo\uc2  .The takeover will create a combined firm with a presence across 40 countries and handling about $90bn of orders each year.\par \par \uc2 At a hearing today, Sir Alastair Norris said the takeover was \u8220\'20\'1Centirely straightforward\u8221\'20\'1D and that he was \u8220\'20\'1Chappy to sanction\u8221\'20\'1D the move.\par \par \uc2 Deliveroo\uc2  , which was founded in 2013 by \uc2 Will Shu\uc2  and Greg Orlowski, made sales of around \u163\'00\'A32bn last year.\par \par \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  was also set up in 2013, co-founded by chief executive \uc2 Tony Xu\uc2  , who has led the company ever since. It operates in more than 30 countries and delivers over 2.5bn orders a year, resulting in revenues of $10.7bn in 2024. It does not operate in the same countries as \uc2 Deliveroo\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Xu said in May that he \u8220\'20\'1Ccould not be more excited\u8221\'20\'1D about the merger, while Shu said that the move marked a \u8220\'20\'1Ctransformative new chapter\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In written submissions for the hearing on Tuesday, Andrew Thornton KC, for \uc2 Deliveroo\uc2  , said the takeover \u8220\'20\'1Cwill not have any adverse impact\u8221\'20\'1D on the company\u8217\'20\'19s creditors and was unanimously recommended by its independent directors. He added that the scheme was also approved by \uc2 Deliveroo\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s shareholders at a meeting in June.\par \par \uc2 In court, Thornton said that two shareholders had been identified as being sanctioned by the government over their ties to Russia. The barrister said that the transfer of their shares would be \u8220\'20\'1Cdelayed\u8221\'20\'1D until \u8220\'20\'1Cthey cease to be sanctioned\u8221\'20\'1D, while the judge stated that their shares would be \u8220\'20\'1Cexcluded from the scheme\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  is now expected to start a review of the merged group, with a potential reduction of around 1% to 3% of the combined workforce, largely affecting general administrative and business support roles.\par \par \uc2 It said in May that some of \uc2 Deliveroo\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s offices and support functions would also no longer be needed, because it will not be listed after the deal, which would result in a \u8220\'20\'1Csmall number\u8221\'20\'1D of job cuts.\par \par \uc2 But it also confirmed it would honour the agreement between \uc2 Deliveroo\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s riders and the \uc2 GMB trade union\uc2  , while \uc2 Deliveroo\uc2  said that \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  does not plan to make major changes to its London headquarters.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, Shu announced that he would leave his role as \uc2 Deliveroo\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s chief executive, claiming he wanted to \u8220\'20\'1Ccontemplate his next challenge\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Deliveroo\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s non-executive directors, who include Cafe Rouge founder Dame \uc2 Karen Jones\uc2  and \uc2 Flutter\uc2  boss Peter Jackson, are also due to step down following the approval of the takeover.\par \par \uc2 2.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  sees lower risks of US recession as economy holds up\par \par \uc2 Strategists at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  are fairly relaxed about the US econmy, saying signals that is sliding into recession have yet to emerge as tariff effects peak.\par \par \uc2 Despite a sharp slowdown in the labour market, strategists led by Kamakshya Trivedi said the US economy is holding up.\par \par \uc2 They noted that the overall data picture does suggest an imminent risk of recession, with signs of improvement in better-than-expected consumer data and an uptick in the housing sector. On \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s tariffs, they believe that the \u8220\'20\'1Cworst of the impacts are likely to be felt over the next 3-4 months, and the path beyond this period looks better.\par \par \uc2 2.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  has acknowledged that the government asked a lot from businesses at the last budget, and said their contribution had allowed the UK to take a huge step forward on the path of renewal.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister said in his keynote speech at the annual Labour party conference in Liverpool:\par \par \uc2 Many companies have complained about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/mar/30/a-bitter-blow-british-pubs-restaurants-and-hospitality-firms-brace-for-rise-in-nics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the \u163\'00\'A325bn increase in employers\u8217\'20\'19 national insurance contributions}}}\uc2  (NICs) that came into force in April. Some publicans say last year\u8217\'20\'19s{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jul/17/worse-for-hospitality-than-covid-bosses-blame-reeves-budget-for-uk-downturn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  budget was \u8220\'20\'1Cworse for hospitality than Covid\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You can follow the latest on our politics live blog here:\par \par \uc2 2.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 Apparently, going shoeless could boost your productivity at work.\par \par \uc2 Slippers, socks and the occasional novelty toe warmer are fast becoming part of the office dress code for startups hoping to ease stress and boost creativity.\par \par \uc2 It might sound like a gimmick conjured up during a yoga retreat, but asking staff to leave their shoes at the door taps into something more serious: how to make the workplace feel a little less like work.\par \par \uc2 With record levels of work-related stress in the UK, rising burnout and a labour force increasingly focused on wellbeing over salary, even small changes can have a surprising impact.\par \par \uc2 Inspired in part by Silicon Valley startups where footwear-free floors are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fortune.com/2025/09/24/the-hottest-workplace-policy-at-startups-right-now-no-shoes/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly}}}\uc2  spreading faster than kombucha fridges, some British companies are trialling no-shoes policies as a way to improve focus, comfort and even staff morale.\par \par \uc2 Natalie James, the founder of the skincare startup, helloSKIN, introduced a sock-only policy at her UK office last year. \u8220\'20\'1COffices are, by their very nature, stressful environments,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf a little thing like taking off your shoes makes you feel more comfortable \u8211\'20\'13 and thus be more creative \u8211\'20\'13 then that\u8217\'20\'19s a no-brainer.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 2.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 Wall Street\u8217\'20\'19s main indices are on track to open lower on the final day of the third quarter, with investors worried about likely delays in the release of key economic figures because of a potential \uc2 US government\uc2  shutdown, which could impact the outlook for further interest rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US government\uc2  is hurtling towards its first shutdown in six years, with no signs {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-congress"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 congressional}}}\uc2  leaders are near agreement on legislation to continue funding beyond the Tuesday night deadline to prevent workers from being furloughed and agencies from shutting their doors.\par \par \uc2 Congress\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republican}}}\uc2  majority is pushing legislation to fund the government through 21 November, but Democrats have refused to vote for it unless it includes a series of concessions centered on healthcare.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\par \par \uc2 convened a meeting of the two party\u8217\'20\'19s congressional leaders on Monday evening, but there was no sign of a breakthrough, with JD Vance, the vice-president, declaring: \u8220\'20\'1CI think we\u8217\'20\'19re headed into a shutdown because the Democrats won\u8217\'20\'19t do the right thing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 However, US stocks have done well through the third quarter, and the benchmark S&P 500 index is on course for its best third-quarter performance since 2020.\par \par \uc2 Over here, the FTSE 100 index in London has risen by 31 points to 9,331, a 0.3% gain. Germany\u8217\'20\'19s Dax has gained 0.2% while Italy\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE MiB is slightly higher and France\u8217\'20\'19s CAC has slipped by 0.17%.\par \par \uc2 2.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 Retailers say tax rises could further fuel inflation as shop prices jump\par \par \uc2 Retailers have told the government that tax rises could further fuel inflation as the pace of shop price rises stepped up in September with increases on home improvement and gardening goods offsetting stabilising food prices.\par \par \uc2 Annual shop price inflation rose to 1.4% in September, up from 0.9% in August, according to the latest monthly report from the \uc2 British Retail Consortium (BRC)\uc2  and analysts NIQ.\par \par \uc2 A year and a half of deflation on non-food goods appears set to come to an end, according to the trade body, with prices just 0.1% lower year on year in September compared with an annual drop of 0.8% in August.\par \par \uc2 Falling prices on back-to-school items such as laptops dampened inflation on other household items including DIY goods. Annual food price inflation, which has been steadily accelerating all year, levelled out at 4.2% in September \u8211\'20\'13 the same rate as in August.\par \par \uc2 12.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  to lay off 2,000 workers globally\par \par \uc2 The American oil and gas giant \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  will lay off 2,000 workers around the world as part of a long-term restructuring plan, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-09-30/exxon-to-cut-2-000-workers-as-oil-sector-job-losses-mount?embedded-checkout=true"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bloomberg News reported}}}\uc2 , citing a memo.\par \par \uc2 The job cuts amount to 3% to 4% of \uc2 Exxon\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s global workforce, as the Texas-based company is reorganising smaller offices into regional hubs.\par \par \uc2 Exxon\uc2  chief executive \uc2 Darren Woods\uc2  said he is making \u8220\'20\'1Ctough decisions\u8221\'20\'1D to make the company more competitive in a memo to employees, according to Bloomberg:\par \par \uc2 Woods is seeking to simplify the company\u8217\'20\'19s sprawling global footprint that resulted from \uc2 Exxon\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s merger with Mobil two decades ago.\par \par \uc2 The regional hubs will focus on \uc2 Exxon\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s growth initiatives such as oil in Guyana, liquefied natural gas along the Gulf Coast and trading globally. The company recently laid out plans to move staff from Brussels and Leatherhead in the UK, to central London, where many of its traders are based.\par \par \uc2 The news comes a day after Calgary-based \uc2 Imperial Oil\uc2  , which is nearly 70% owned by \uc2 Exxon\uc2  , announced it is cutting a fifth of its workforce.\par \par \uc2 Other big energy companies, including \uc2 Chevron\uc2  , ConocoPhilllips and the UK\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 BP\uc2  , have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jan/16/bp-to-cut-4700-jobs-3000-contractor-roles-costs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced thousands of job cuts}}}\uc2  this year, as the industry struggles with weaker crude oil prices.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 11.54am BST\par \par \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  owner to rebrand thousands of Collect+ stores as it buys stake\par \par \uc2 Thousands of convenience stores will be branded Royal MailShop, after the delivery company\u8217\'20\'19s new owner acquired a stake in the parcel network Collect+, in the first big deal since it was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/apr/03/royal-mail-takeover-deal-czech-billionaire-finalised-daniel-kretinsky"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 taken over by a Czech billionaire}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  owner, \uc2 International Distribution Services\uc2  (IDS), which was taken over by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/apr/03/royal-mail-takeover-deal-czech-billionaire-finalised-daniel-kretinsky"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Daniel K\u345\'01\'59et\u237\'00\'EDnsk\u253\'00\'FD}}}\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s EP Group in April in a \u163\'00\'A33.6bn deal, said it had bought a 49% stake in Collect+ for \u163\'00\'A343.9m, which offers access to almost 8,000 pickup points \u8211\'20\'13 newsagents, convenience stores, supermarkets and petrol stations.\par \par \uc2 The launch of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/royal-mail"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Royal Mail}}}\uc2  Shop puts its brand directly on the high street, with customers able to collect, send and return parcels. Significantly, the shops will be open during evenings and weekends, unlike many Post Office branches.\par \par \uc2 Shops will sell \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  postage, initially just Tracked 24 and Tracked 48, with more products to follow. This means customers can pay in person rather than online.\par \par \uc2 The move comes two years after \uc2 Royal Mail{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/07/royal-mail-loses-360-year-monopoly-on-delivering-parcels-from-post-office-sites"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lost a 360-year-old monopoly}}}\uc2  on delivering parcels from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/post-office"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Post Office}}}\uc2  branches, after \uc2 Evri\uc2  and DPD signed deals.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  service is available in 500 shops and will be rolled out more widely in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 11.56am BST\par \par \uc2 11.51am BST\par \par \uc2 Tata\uc2  bought slabs from rival \uc2 British Steel\uc2  as it sought to beat Trump tariff plans\par \par \uc2 British Steel\uc2  has made the unusual move to sell slabs of metal to its rival \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  , as the latter sought ways around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s proposed tariff rules.\par \par \uc2 The Scunthorpe {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/apr/07/it-will-cripple-the-town-scunthorpe-ponders-life-after-steel-as-2700-jobs-at-risk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 steelworks in north Lincolnshire}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 now controlled by the \uc2 UK government\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 provided slabs made in its blast furnaces to \uc2 Tata\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s operations in south Wales in recent months, according to steel sources.\par \par \uc2 Indian-owned {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/tata"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tata}}}\uc2  was seeking ways to avoid threatened US tariffs on steel not sourced from the UK while its Welsh operations were reliant on buying slabs from elsewhere. However, the proposed rules were later dropped after the UK and US failed to reach agreement.\par \par \uc2 The UK {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/apr/11/parliament-recalled-to-discuss-british-steel-nationalisation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 government took control of British Steel}}}\uc2  in April amid fears that its Chinese owner, Jingye Steel, was preparing to walk away and leave irreparable damage.\par \par \uc2 Since taking control, the government has directed \uc2 British Steel\uc2  to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/may/07/british-steel-to-hire-more-staff-government-rescue-scunthorpe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increase output and hire more workers}}}\uc2 . Jingye claimed the blast furnaces at Scunthorpe \u8211\'20\'13 the last in Britain able to make steel from iron ore \u8211\'20\'13 had been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/apr/12/why-does-british-steel-need-to-be-rescued-by-the-government-and-what-happens-next"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 losing \u163\'00\'A3700,000 a day}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ramping up production was seen by some people as a way to improve profitability and ease the financial burden on the government. Earlier this month, the government said that it had injected \u163\'00\'A3180m to \uc2 British Steel\uc2  to pay for raw materials, salaries and unpaid bills.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 11.58am BST\par \par \uc2 10.58am BST\par \par \uc2 German unemployment rises more than expected in September\par \par \uc2 In Germany, Europe\u8217\'20\'19s biggest economy, unemployment rose more than expected this month, as the labour market struggles to pick up in a weak economy.\par \par \uc2 The number of people out of work increased by 14,000 to 2.98 million, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.arbeitsagentur.de/news/arbeitsmarkt#accordion1228431759213399"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 seasonally adjusted figures from the federal labour office Bundesagentur f\u252\'00\'FCr Arbeit}}}\uc2 , higher than the 8,000 increase forecast by economists. The jobless rate stayed at 6.3%.\par \par \uc2 The number of vacancies also declined, by 66,000 from a year earlier to 630,000 job openings. The head of the labour office, \uc2 Andrea Nahles\uc2  , said:\par \par \uc2 The German economy shrank by 0.3% between April and June compared with the first three months of the year. Leading German economic institutes nudged up their growth forecast for this year to 0.2% from 0.1%, rising to 1.3% in 2026.\par \par \uc2 Chancellor \uc2 Friedrich Merz\uc2  has pledged to get Germany out of its downturn with a big increase in infrastructure and defence spending, but the effects have yet to be felt on the ground.\par \par \uc2 10.43am BST\par \par \uc2 UK consumer borrowing rises most since October 2024\par \par \uc2 Consumer borrowing in the UK rose at the fastest annual pace since last October in August, according to \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  figures \u8211\'20\'13 a sign that household demand remained solid ahead of potential tax hikes in the budget on 26 November.\par \par \uc2 Consumer borrowing rose by 7.1% in August from the year before, up from an annual increase of 7% in July. In the last three months, borrowing increased at the fastest pace since March 2024.\par \par \uc2 Ruth Gregory\uc2  , deputy chief UK economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  , said:\par \par \uc2 Mortgage approvals totalled 64,680, down slightly from 65,161 in July. Mortgage lending fell to \u163\'00\'A34.3bn from \u163\'00\'A34.5bn.\par \par \uc2 10.21am BST\par \par \uc2 Smith+Nephew\uc2  CFO moves to US\par \par \uc2 The finance chief of artificial hips and knees maker \uc2 Smith+Nephew\uc2  will relocate to the US from the UK, citing operational efficiency, with more than half the company\u8217\'20\'19s revenues coming from the US.\par \par \uc2 The UK company, which makes orthopaedic implants, wound dressings and surgical aids for sports medicine, said its chief financial officer \uc2 John Rogers\uc2  will move to the US this week.\par \par \uc2 Rogers will be employed under a local US employment contract.\par \par \uc2 The move comes after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  urged global pharmaceutical firms to invest more in the US, prompting US drugmakers along with the UK\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  and \uc2 GSK\uc2  , and Swiss firms \uc2 Novartis\uc2  and Roche to announce billions of dollars of investment.\par \par \uc2 10.09am BST\par \par \uc2 125 jobs to go at Lindsey oil refinery\par \par \uc2 About 125 jobs are to go at the Lindsey oil refinery in north Lincolnshire, following the collapse of its parent company, the energy conglomerate Prax Group .\par \par \uc2 However, there are at least two bids to buy and operate the site as a going concern with a full workforce, according to the \uc2 Unite union\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 The government-run Insolvency Service said:\par \par \uc2 Unite said the government is responsible for the redundancies going ahead as it could provide support to ensure the refinery is kept operational. There are a further 500 contract jobs and potentially thousands in the supply chain depend on the refinery, it said.\par \par \uc2 The union believes that the insolvency firm \uc2 FTI\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s preferred bidder wishes to mothball the site and use it as a storage terminal for oil tankers. It would be the easiest way to satisfy creditors but would \u8220\'20\'1Cgut jobs, harm the regional economy and compromise the UK\u8217\'20\'19s ability to produce its own fuel\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Unite general secretary Sharon Graham said:\par \par \uc2 The refinery\u8217\'20\'19s biggest creditor is HMRC, alongside oil company \uc2 Glencore\uc2  . Both can wait for the more complicated but far less damaging process of maintaining the site as an oil refinery, with government support, Unite said.\par \par \uc2 Without the refinery, which supplied 25% of the UK diesel market, the country is more reliant on imported fuel. This impacts the UK\u8217\'20\'19s energy security and leaves consumers more exposed to price rises at the pump. A substantial amount of diesel is imported from Turkey and India and made from Russian crude.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 11.50am BST\par \par \uc2 9.33am BST\par \par \uc2 Asos shares slump as it warns on outlook amid 'soft consumer backdrop'\par \par \uc2 Shares in Asos fell more than 11% to a near-six month low, after the British online fashion retailer said full-year revenues will miss forecasts and fall more steeply amid weak consumer spending.\par \par \uc2 The shares fell to 260p in early trading, the lowest since 9 April, making them the biggest loser on the FTSE Smallcap Index, and are now trading 9% down at 267p.\par \par \uc2 The company said revenues for the year to 26 September will come in slightly below market expectations, as it focuses on higher quality sales against a \u8220\'20\'1Csoft consumer backdrop\u8221\'20\'1D. Analysts had pencilled in an 8.4% decline in revenues.\par \par \uc2 Asos now sees full-year profit at the lower end of the \u163\'00\'A3130m to \u163\'00\'A3150m forecast range, but said gross profit margins had improved, and is expecting a return to revenue growth in the coming years.\par \par \uc2 It has slashed costs by mothballing its Atlanta warehouse and reducing its stockpile of clothes by more than 60% since the end of 2022, from \u163\'00\'A31.1bn to \u163\'00\'A3400m, in an attempt to become more agile.\par \par \uc2 Over the summer Asos started the final phase of its transformation programme, launching new customer experiences such as its adidas x ASOS collaboration, the ASOS.WORLD loyalty programme in the UK, and expanding Topshop and Topman through new channels. It said there were \u8220\'20\'1Cpositive early signs\u8221\'20\'1D from customers.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 9.35am BST\par \par \uc2 8.35am BST\par \par \uc2 UK growth revisions: what the experts say\par \par \uc2 Although the upward revisions to UK GDP this morning are welcome, they probably won\u8217\'20\'19t save chancellor Reeves from a productivity downgrade from the UK\u8217\'20\'19s fiscal watchdog, says \uc2 Paul Dales\uc2  , chief UK economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Matt Swannell, chief economic advisor to the EY ITEM Club, points out that growth has been \u8220\'20\'1Cvery sluggish\u8221\'20\'1D since Covid-19:\par \par \uc2 Richard Flax, chief investment officer at{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.moneyfarm.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Moneyfarm}}}\uc2 , suggests the economy is \u8220\'20\'1Cnavigating headwinds\u8221\'20\'1D better than thought:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.58am BST\par \par \uc2 8.17am BST\par \par \uc2 Gambling company shares drop as Reeves hints at higher taxes\par \par \uc2 Shares in gambling companies are dipping at the start of trading, after chancellor \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  hinted that they could face higher taxes.\par \par \uc2 Evoke (which owns 888poker and William Hill) has dropped by 1.5%, with RankGroup down 1%.\par \par \uc2 Entain\uc2  (the firm behind \uc2 Ladbrokes\uc2  , Coral and \uc2 BetMGM\uc2  ) fell 1.7% at the open in London, before recovering.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, Reeves signaled she could be open to raising taxes on the UK\u8217\'20\'19s gambling industry, which could raise funds to fund services or benefits.\par \par \uc2 She told ITV News:\par \par \uc2 8.06am BST\par \par \uc2 UK GDP per capita stats\par \par \uc2 You get a better picture of a country\u8217\'20\'19s living standards if you look at GDP per capita (the calculation that divides GDP by population).\par \par \uc2 And again, we can see that the picture was less grim than first thought in the first six months of the Labour government (and in the last quarter of the Conservatives, before last July\u8217\'20\'19s election).\par \par \uc2 GDP per capita in July-September 2024 rose by 0.1%, rather than shrinking by 0.1% as first thought.\par \par \uc2 In October-December, GDP per capita also rose by 0.1%, having previously been estimated as 0%.\par \par \uc2 However, GDP per capita in January-March this year has been revised down to 0.5% from 0.6%, even though the estimte for GDP is unchanged.\par \par \uc2 7.57am BST\par \par \uc2 Estimates for UK business investment in the last quarter have also been revised higher.\par \par \uc2 Business investment is estimated to have fallen by 1.1% in Quarter 2 2025, revised up from the first estimate fall of 4.0%. This follows a 4.1% increase in January-March.\par \par \uc2 7.55am BST\par \par \uc2 Annual growth faster than estimated too\par \par \uc2 This morning\u8217\'20\'19s revisions mean UK growth over the last year was faster than estimated.\par \par \uc2 GDP on a year-on-year basis was 1.4% higher than a year ago in Q2 2025, up from a previous estimate of 1.2%\par \par \uc2 7.53am BST\par \par \uc2 Household savings rate rises as consumers turn cautious\par \par \uc2 UK households saved more money in the second quarter of this year \u8211\'20\'13 possibly a sign of caution about the economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 The households\u8217\'20\'19 saving ratio is estimated to have increased to 10.7% in Quarter 2 2025, up from 10.5% in the first quarter of this year, due to a rise in non-pension saving.\par \par \uc2 Thomas Pugh, chief economist at leading audit, tax and consulting firm RSM UK says:\par \par \uc2 7.47am BST\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s some disappointing news in today\u8217\'20\'19s GDP report, though \u8211\'20\'13 the production sector suffered a sharper downturn in the last quarter, and the building sector grew more slowly than estimated.\par \par \uc2 The production sector is estimated to have fallen by 0.8% in Quarter 2 2025 (previously estimated as a 0.3% fall), the ONS reports. This downturn was driven by lower power generation and gas production.\par \par \uc2 Services output increased by an unrevised 0.4% in Quarter 2 2025,\par \par \uc2 And construction output is estimated to have grown by 1.0% in Quarter 2 2025 (previously a 1.2% increase).\par \par \uc2 7.34am BST\par \par \uc2 Introduction: UK growth in early months of Labour government revised up\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s economy has grown faster than first estimated in the first six months of the Labour government.\par \par \uc2 Updated economic data released this morning shows that the economy did not stagnate in the third quarter of last year, as \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  , \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  and colleagues took the helm with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/article/2024/sep/01/doom-and-gloom-starmer-hardline-strategy-pm-rose-garden-voters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gloomy warnings about the challenge ahead}}}\uc2 .\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/dec/23/uk-gdp-economy-flatlined-activity-falling-public-confidence-nosedive-recession-fears-business-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Having previously said GDP was flat in July-September 2024}}}\uc2 , the \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  has recrunched the numbers and concluded the economy actually grew by 0.2% \u8211\'20\'13 modest growth, but better than none at all.\par \par \uc2 Growth in October-December 2024 has also been revised up, from 0.1% to 0.2%.\par \par \uc2 The performance of the economy in the last six months of Conservative rule has also been revised. Growth in Q1 2024 has been cut from 0.9% to 0.8%, while growth in April-June 2024 has been revised up from 0.5% to 0.6%.\par \par \uc2 Although these changes are minor, and historic, they do undermine the narrative that fears over Reeves\u8217\'20\'19s budget plans chilled the economy to a standstill last year.\par \par \uc2 Something for Labour to ponder at their party conference this week, where the prime minister is expected to tell members that his economic strategy can be the \u8220\'20\'1Cantidote to division\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 GDP figures for 2025 have not been revised though \u8211\'20\'13 the economy still grew by 0.7% in January-March, slowing to 0.3% in April-June.\par \par \uc2 And the big picture is that the level of GDP in Quarter 2 2025 compared with Quarter 4 (Oct to Dec) 2023 is now estimated to be 2.9% higher, revised up from the first estimate of 2.6%, the ONS says.\par \par \uc2 ONS director of economic statistics \uc2 Liz McKeown\uc2  explains:\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 * 7am BST: GDP quarterly national accounts, UK: April to June 2025\par \par \uc2 * 10.30am BST: \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2  president Lagarde speaks at the \uc2 Bank of Finland\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s monetary policy conference\par \par \uc2 * 3pm BST: US JOLTS vacancies data\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.44am BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250930el9u001ry\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
