\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 YouTube\uc2  agrees to pay Trump $24.5m to settle lawsuit over account suspension\b0\par\par\uc2 Dara Kerr \par \uc2 657 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 September 2025\par 00:09 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Platform suspended the US president\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel in 2021 after the January 6 Capitol riot\par \par \uc2 YouTube\uc2  has agreed to pay $24.5m to settle a suit brought by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2021 that alleged the platform wrongly suspended his channel after the January 6 attack on the US Capitol. The \uc2 Google\uc2  subsidiary is the latest in a long string of tech companies to make a multimillion-dollar payout to the president over past decisions about his accounts.\par \par \uc2 Trump had filed the suit against \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  CEO \uc2 Sundar Pichai\uc2 , alleging that the platform had \u8220\'20\'1Caccumulated an unprecedented concentration of power, market share, and ability to dictate our nation\u8217\'20\'19s public discourse\u8221\'20\'1D. \uc2 YouTube\uc2  said it suspended Trump\u8217\'20\'19s channel because it had violated the website\u8217\'20\'19s policies against inciting violence. Because of the settlement, the case is now dismissed. \uc2 Google\uc2  did not immediately return a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 The news comes just a week after \uc2 YouTube\uc2  announced that it would allow creators who were once banned for spreading misinformation about Covid-19 and the 2020 US presidential election to be reinstated. In its announcement, \uc2 YouTube\uc2  said it celebrated conservative voices on its site and blamed the account suspensions on pressure from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2 -parent company \uc2 Meta\uc2  settled a similar lawsuit with Trump in Januaryfor $25m, and the social media platform X, previously \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , settled another for $10m in February. Most of the payout from the \uc2 Meta\uc2  suit will go to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s presidential library fund. For the \uc2 YouTube\uc2  settlement, Trump has directed $22m of the payment to go to restoring and preserving the National Mall and supporting construction of the White House ballroom, according to documents filed in the US district court for the northern district of California. The lavish ballroom is expected to cost around $200m.\par \par \uc2 The three cases were first brought by Trump lawyer and ally, John Coale, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/youtube-to-pay-24-5-million-to-settle-lawsuit-brought-by-trump-808f6823?mod=hp_lead_pos1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wall Street Journal}}}\uc2 , which first reported the news. Coale told the Journal that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s return to the White House was instrumental in reaching the slew of settlements with tech companies, saying: \u8220\'20\'1CIf he had not been re-elected, we would have been in court for 1,000 years.\u8221\'20\'1D Coale is now Trump\u8217\'20\'19s deputy special envoy to Ukraine and Belarus.\par \par \uc2 In an email to the Guardian, Coale said Trump was an \u8220\'20\'1Cideal client\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGlad it and the others I filled [sic] for DJT in July or [sic] 2021 ended to the tune of 60mil,\u8221\'20\'1D Coale added. \u8220\'20\'1CWe got $$$ and changed tech behavior I believe.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The case against \uc2 YouTube\uc2  had been closed in 2023, but Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers filed to reopen the case after he won the presidency. Before his victory, all three of the lawsuits faced uphill court battles. A federal judge dismissed the case against \uc2 Twitter\uc2  in 2022, and the suits against \uc2 Meta\uc2  and \uc2 YouTube\uc2  were stayed, then the latter was administratively close. Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers, however, revived the cases with appeals to overturn each ruling.\par \par \uc2 YouTube\uc2  first suspended Trump\u8217\'20\'19s channel for seven days on 12 January 2021, after he posted a video saying the speech he made to his supporters on January 6 before the Capitol riot was \u8220\'20\'1Ctotally appropriate\u8221\'20\'1D. \uc2 YouTube\uc2  said it suspended the channel over \u8220\'20\'1Cconcerns about the ongoing potential for violence\u8221\'20\'1D. The company then extended the ban without an end date.\par \par \uc2 It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t until March 2023, after Trump announced his bid for his second presidency, that \uc2 YouTube\uc2  reinstated Trump\u8217\'20\'19s channel, saying it \u8220\'20\'1Ccarefully evaluated the continued risk of real-world violence, balancing that with the importance of preserving the opportunity for voters to hear equally from major national candidates in the run up to an election\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Within hours of getting his channel back, Trump posted: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19M BACK!\u8221\'20\'1D accompanied by an 11-second video of him talking at a rally saying: \u8220\'20\'1CSorry to keep you waiting. Complicated business. Complicated.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250929el9t006k5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
