\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US set for largest mass resignation in history as Trump continues deep cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,070 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 September 2025\par 12:55 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Federal workers say they have little choice but to depart, with 100,000 leaving under deferred resignation program\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration is set to oversee the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7375898216262946816/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 largest mass resignation }}}\uc2 in US history on Tuesday, with more than 100,000 federal workers set to formally quit as part of the latest wave of its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ourpublicservice.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Deferred-Resignation-Program-Guide-FedSupport-Partnership-for-Public-Service.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deferred resignation program}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With Congress facing a deadline of Tuesday to authorize more funding or spark a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/27/government-shutdown-explainer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 government shutdown}}}\uc2 , the White House has also ordered federal agencies to draw up plans for large-scale firings of workers if the partisan fight fails to yield a deal.\par \par \uc2 Workers preparing to leave government as part of the resignation program \u8211\'20\'13 one of several pillars of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s sweeping cuts to the federal workforce \u8211\'20\'13 have described how months of \u8220\'20\'1Cfear and intimidation\u8221\'20\'1D left them feeling like they had no choice but to depart.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFederal workers stay for the mission. When that mission is taken away, when they\u8217\'20\'19re scapegoated, when their job security is uncertain, and when their tiny semblance of work-life balance is stripped away, they leave,\u8221\'20\'1D a longtime employee at the \uc2 Federal Emergency Management Agency\uc2  (Fema) told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s why I left.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The total resignation program is set to cost {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hsgac.senate.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025-07-31-Minority-Staff-Report-The-21.7-Billion-Blunder.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $14.8bn}}}\uc2 , with 200,000 workers paid their full salary and benefits while on administrative leave for up to eight months, according to a Senate Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 report in July.\par \par \uc2 Trump officials argue this outlay is worth it. The Office of Personnel Management claimed the one-time costs lower longer-term spending by the federal government. It also criticized job protections of federal civil servants, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.opm.gov/news/secrets-of-opm/what-they-got-wrong-about-the-deferred-resignation-program-1/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claiming}}}\uc2  the government should have a \u8220\'20\'1Cmodern, at-will employment framework like most employers\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the White House claimed there was \u8220\'20\'1Cno additional cost to the government\u8221\'20\'1D as employees would have received their salaries regardless of the program. \u8220\'20\'1CIn fact, this is the largest and most effective workforce reduction plan in history and will save the government $28bn annually,\u8221\'20\'1D they added.\par \par \uc2 The total number of expected departures through the delayed resignation and voluntary separation programs, attrition, and early retirement programs is about 275,000 employees, the spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Several thousands of additional federal workers have been fired as part of reduction in force mandates ordered by the administration. The mass exodus is the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://web.archive.org/web/20250826161242/https://www.nytimes.com/2025/08/22/us/politics/trump-federal-workers.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 largest }}}\uc2 single-year decline in civilian federal employment since the second world war.\par \par \uc2 Federal employees who took the deferred resignation offer requested to speak anonymously in hopes of returning to the federal government in the future and to protect future job prospects.\par \par \uc2 They are entering a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/sep/05/us-jobs-report-august-tariffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lagging job market }}}\uc2 as the unemployment rate in August 2025 ticked up to 4.3%, the highest since 2021, and only 22,000 jobs were added amid disruptions and uncertainty caused by Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariffs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a huge grieving process,\u8221\'20\'1D said a Department of Veterans Affairs (VA) employee who took the deferred resignation offer. \u8220\'20\'1CMyself and many others that I know really hoped that we would finish our careers with the government. We felt very tied, especially in the VA, to the mission.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMany of us thought we could do better for our clients, for our veterans outside of the VA, and a lot of us were so burnt out from the six months before the deferred resignation that it was actually a mental health decision for many as well.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Communicating why they left has been challenging while searching for a new job, the VA employee said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe job market stinks right now,\u8221\'20\'1D they said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s great to not be working 60 to 70 hours a week any more, but you also lose your support system from those that stayed, and from those that may judge you for leaving.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 An archaeologist at the \uc2 US Department of Agriculture (USDA)\uc2 , and military veteran, said they \u8220\'20\'1Cloved\u8221\'20\'1D their job, but took the deferred resignation out of fear and pressure from the administration. \u8220\'20\'1CI was forced to accept the resignation plan. \u8220\'20\'1CNot physically, or through any legal means, but through fear and intimidation,\u8221\'20\'1D they said.\par \par \uc2 They cited {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/10/who-is-russell-vought-trump-office-of-management-and-budget"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 comments }}}\uc2 from Russell Vought, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s head of the Office of Management and Budget, who said of federal workers last October: \u8220\'20\'1CWhen they wake up in the morning, we want them to not want to go to work, because they are increasingly viewed as the villains. We want their funding to be shut down \u8230\'20\'26 We want to put them in trauma.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is exactly what happened,\u8221\'20\'1D the \uc2 USDA\uc2  worker said. \u8220\'20\'1CI was scared to go to work. Scared that the next day would be the day I would get fired, or barred from future service, scared I would wait too long to leave and not find a job, and just living everyday like a raw nerve.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The public vilification of federal workers has pushed many to take the resignation program, the archaeologist suggested, as they were constantly inundated with threats of cuts and firings.\par \par \uc2 Another employee at the \uc2 US Department of Agriculture\uc2  was fired in February as a probationary employee, reinstated in April only to take the deferred resignation offer.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt that point, I felt they could terminate me at any time,\u8221\'20\'1D they told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s hard to focus on your work when they can just send you an email and you can be gone, and they completely changed the terms of my work. I was hoping things would stabilize and there would be an opportunity to go back, but now it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t look like there will be an opportunity.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2  and other labor unions representing federal workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.mad.280398/gov.uscourts.mad.280398.77.0.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed a lawsuit}}}\uc2  that is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.courtlistener.com/docket/69610323/american-federation-of-government-employees-afl-cio-v-ezell/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 still ongoing}}}\uc2  over the deferred resignation program earlier this year, alleging the buyout circumvents congressional authority, undermines statutorily required functions of government agencies in losing employees en masse, and was enacted with the threat of firings.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPurging the federal government of dedicated career federal employees will have vast, unintended consequences that will cause chaos for the Americans who depend on a functioning federal government,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 AFGE\uc2  president \uc2 Everett Kelley\uc2  in February. \u8220\'20\'1CThis offer should not be viewed as voluntary.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBetween the flurry of anti-worker executive orders and policies, it is clear that the Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s goal is to turn the federal government into a toxic environment where workers cannot stay even if they want to.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250928el9s001s6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
