\par\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to cut 900 jobs and close dozens of North American stores as sales struggle\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 473 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2025\par 07:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Restructuring comes after six quarters of falling sales and growing pressure from unions over working conditions\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/starbucks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Starbucks}}}\uc2  will lay off around 900 staff and pull down the shutters on some 100 cafes across North America as part of a $1bn restructuring plan to revive the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest coffee chain.\par \par \uc2 The business, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/feb/24/starbucks-corporate-employee-lay-offs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliminated}}}\uc2  1,100 corporate positions earlier this year, will also close \u8220\'20\'1Cmany\u8221\'20\'1D open or vacant positions as it notifies affected employees affected by cuts on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The 900 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  staff hit by the restructuring work in \u8220\'20\'1Cnon-retail\u8221\'20\'1D roles, the company said. It will also close 1% of the coffee houses it operates in North America.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is scrambling to reverse its fortunes. The firm\u8217\'20\'19s US sales have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jan/28/starbucks-earnings-sales-wall-street"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dropped}}}\uc2  for six straight quarters as consumers, grappling with years of higher inflation, think twice before ordering its pricey coffees.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/07/starbucks-brian-niccol-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A year under CEO Niccol: Starbucks workers\u8217\'20\'19 long fight for a union contract}}}\par \par \uc2 The latest cuts are designed to \u8220\'20\'1Creinforce what we see is working and prioritize our resources against them\u8221\'20\'1D, the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  CEO, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/07/starbucks-brian-niccol-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brian Niccol}}}\uc2 , wrote in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/829224/000082922425000067/exhibit991.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 letter}}}\uc2  to employees on Thursday. \u8220\'20\'1CI believe these steps are necessary to build a better, stronger, and more resilient \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  that deepens its impact on the world and creates more opportunities for our partners, suppliers, and the communities we serve.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jan/14/starbucks-union-fight"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Starbucks Workers United}}}\uc2 , a union representing workers at hundreds of its stores, said the move \u8220\'20\'1Cmakes it clear things are only going backwards\u8221\'20\'1D at the chain. \u8220\'20\'1CFixing what\u8217\'20\'19s broken at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  isn\u8217\'20\'19t possible without centering the people who engage with the company\u8217\'20\'19s customers day in and day out,\u8221\'20\'1D it said.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  attributed about 90% of the restructuring cost to its North American business, with $150m expected in employee separation costs and $850m in costs related to store closures.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  also plans to \u8220\'20\'1Cuplift\u8221\'20\'1D more than 1,000 store locations in the next 12 months, Niccol added, \u8220\'20\'1Cto introduce greater texture, warmth and layered design\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In his letter, Niccol did not mention the union organizing efforts at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 . More than 650 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores in the US have unionized, with workers pushing for a first union contract.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations between the union and \uc2 Starbucks{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/07/starbucks-brian-niccol-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 faltered }}}\uc2 months after Niccol assumed the role of CEO last September, as the union launched its largest strike ever at the company in December.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers with the union have criticized the \u8220\'20\'1CBack to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D changes under Niccol, and have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/SBWorkersUnited/status/1970536446881829265"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 threatened}}}\uc2  further strikes to push the company to reach a first contract with the union. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://one.starbucks.com/negotiations/our-ongoing-commitment-to-fair-bargaining/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claimed}}}\uc2  it is committed to fair bargaining.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI know these decisions impact our partners and their families, and we did not make them lightly,\u8221\'20\'1D Niccol said on Thursday. Shares in \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  dropped 1%.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250925el9p003h1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
