\par\par \b \uc2 America\u8217\'20\'19s leadership class is failing us. It\u8217\'20\'19s up to you and me to defend democracy\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Reich \par \uc2 754 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2025\par 18:41 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The disappearance of America\u8217\'20\'19s leadership class at a time like this means that the rest of us have to be leaders\par \par \uc2 As Trump and his goons strip Americans of our constitutional rights, the silence of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s leadership class is deafening.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m old enough to remember when, during the Vietnam war, university presidents utilized their bully pulpits to remind America of its moral center.\par \par \uc2 Today, university presidents are cowed. I recently talked to one young female college president who told me, point blank, that \u8220\'20\'1Cuniversity presidents have no business speaking out on public issues\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The chancellor of my own university, the \uc2 University of California at Berkeley\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the very place where the \u8220\'20\'1Cfree speech movement\u8221\'20\'1D began in 1965 \u8211\'20\'13 still hasn\u8217\'20\'19t explained why Berkeley last week handed over to the regime the names of 160 students, lecturers, and faculty members who took part in pro-Palestinian demonstrations. Some are here on visas and terribly vulnerable. Others lack tenure and are vulnerable in different ways.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not just university presidents. Whatever happened to America\u8217\'20\'19s religious leaders?\par \par \uc2 During prior crises of conscience, such as the struggle for civil rights, the voices of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s religious leaders were loud and confident. They brimmed with moral power. Today, we hear only the strident voices of the religious right.\par \par \uc2 What happened to America\u8217\'20\'19s business leaders? They\u8217\'20\'19ve never been especially reluctant to speak out on public issues.\par \par \uc2 For years \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , CEO of \uc2 JP Morgan Chase\uc2 , has acted as a self-appointed spokesperson for American business, sometimes reminding CEOs of their social responsibilities. This time? Utter silence.\par \par \uc2 Other CEOs have gone over to the dark side, competing to suck up to the tyrant-in-chief, eager to lavish him with praise, gush over his accomplishments, even hand him gifts of solid gold bullion.\par \par \uc2 The leaders of America\u8217\'20\'19s legal community? \u8220\'20\'1CI want to keep my head down,\u8221\'20\'1D the senior partner of a large firm told me. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have too much to lose.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The leaders of the media? They\u8217\'20\'19re busy consolidating their ownership over ever more of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s media and dare not upset Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 FCC\uc2  and its Trump toady \uc2 Brendan Carr\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What of their responsibility to protect free speech? They\u8217\'20\'19re far more interested in maximizing the value of their shares of stock.\par \par \uc2 And whatever happened to the nation\u8217\'20\'19s political leaders? Where are their voices in this time of democratic crisis?\par \par \uc2 Most Republicans are zombies and most Democrats wimps.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , the Senate minority leader, won\u8217\'20\'19t even endorse Zohran Mamdani for New York mayor \u8211\'20\'13 even though Mamdani is among the most popular young politicians with young voters.\par \par \uc2 We have to wait for Ted Cruz \u8211\'20\'13 Ted Cruz! \u8211\'20\'13 to sound the alarm about the \uc2 FCC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s attack on freedom of speech?\par \par \uc2 The sad fact is that, like so much else Trump\u8217\'20\'19s reign of terror has revealed, it is showing us a reality we should have known years ago but many of us didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to see: America\u8217\'20\'19s leadership class no longer leads. It hides.\par \par \uc2 Its obligations to the common good \u8211\'20\'13 to the freedom of speech, freedom to assemble, freedom from government arrest and imprisonment without due process, freedom to vote and participate in our democracy, freedom from arbitrary and capricious government decisions \u8211\'20\'13 have disappeared.\par \par \uc2 Instead, people in positions of significant responsibility have succumbed to greed, small-mindedness, insularity, and cowardice.\par \par \uc2 During a crisis like the one we\u8217\'20\'19re now in, these so-called leaders have abdicated their moral responsibility.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not all bad that America no longer has a leadership class.\par \par \uc2 True leadership doesn\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily require high office. It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t require a fancy title. Mahatma Gandhi, \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 , \uc2 Nelson Mandela\uc2 , and countless others who have moved the world held no formal positions of power. Their power came from their moral authority to tell the truth and mobilize others.\par \par \uc2 The disappearance of America\u8217\'20\'19s leadership class at a time like this means that the rest of us have to be leaders. You must be a leader.\par \par \uc2 We are the leaders we\u8217\'20\'19ve been waiting for.\par \par \uc2 * Robert Reich, a former US secretary of labor, is a professor of public policy emeritus at the \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 . He is a Guardian US columnist and his newsletter is at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://robertreich.substack.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 robertreich.substack.com}}}\uc2 . His new book, Coming Up Short: A Memoir of My America, is out now\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250922el9m0020c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
