\par\par \b \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s $100,000 H-1B visa fee could hurt US growth, economists warn\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Almeida \par \uc2 600 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2025\par 09:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Move is a blow to big tech companies, which rely heavily on visas to hire staff from overseas, particularly IndiaBusiness live \u8211\'20\'13 latest updates\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision to impose a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/19/trump-h1b-visa-100000-fee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $100,000 (\u163\'00\'A374,096) fee on H1-B visas for skilled foreign workers}}}\uc2  could hurt US economic growth, economists have warned.\par \par \uc2 Trump signed a proclamation on Friday introducing the application fee for the H-1B visa, 60 times the current cost, in a move designed to encourage companies to hire more American workers.\par \par \uc2 The increase represents a blow to big tech companies, which rely heavily on such visas to hire engineers, scientists and coders from overseas, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/sep/22/trump-india-h1-b-visa-fee-hike-response-afraid-of-talent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 particularly India}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Atakan Bakiskan, an economist at the investment bank Berenberg, said the move was an example of the Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Canti-growth policymaking\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBy making it very expensive for companies to attract foreign talent, and by forcing some international students to leave the country after graduation, the brain drain will weigh heavily on productivity,\u8221\'20\'1D he added.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CInvestments in artificial intelligence are unlikely to offset the damage caused by the loss of human capital under restrictive immigration policies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Berenberg recently lowered its estimate for US economic growth, from 2% at the start of the year to 1.5%. However, Bakiskan warned that, unless Trump relents, the 1.5% forecast \u8220\'20\'1Cmay soon look optimistic\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said: \u8220\'20\'1CTaken together, the erosion of trust in institutions, a loss of human capital, tariffs, chronic uncertainty, and unsustainable fiscal policies can raise the tail risk of a financial crisis in the US. In the long run, they may set a path for an even weaker dollar and higher long-term yields.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  market strategist, Jim Reid, said the new application fee \u8220\'20\'1Ccaused a huge amount of uncertainty over the weekend for those that rely on it\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s announcement on Friday triggered chaos in the tech industry, with some businesses in Silicon Valley urging staff not to travel outside the country. It prompted the White House to clarify on Saturday that the new higher fee applied only to new applicants and would be a one-off payment.\par \par \uc2 Kathleen Brooks, the research director at the broker XTB, said \uc2 Amazon\uc2  had the highest number of workers on H-1B visas, closely followed by \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  owner \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAlthough these companies have the money to afford the visas, other sectors who also rely on H-1B visas may struggle with future recruitment, for example the health care and education sectors,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 In the first half of 2025, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/amazon"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amazon}}}\uc2  had more than 10,000 H-1B visas approved, while {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/microsoft"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Microsoft}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/meta"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Meta Platforms}}}\uc2  had more than 5,000 approvals each.\par \par \uc2 The H-1B programme offers 65,000 visas annually to employers bringing in temporary foreign workers in specialised fields, with a further 20,000 visas for workers with advanced degrees.\par \par \uc2 India has been the biggest beneficiary of H-1B visas, accounting for 71% of approved visas last year. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/sep/22/trump-india-h1-b-visa-fee-hike-response-afraid-of-talent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Indian government hit back at the new fee for skilled workers}}}\uc2 , warning it would have \u8220\'20\'1Chumanitarian consequences\u8221\'20\'1D by way of \u8220\'20\'1Cdisruption caused for families\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, India\u8217\'20\'19s commerce minister, \uc2 Piyush Goyal\uc2 , said: \u8220\'20\'1CThey are also a little afraid of our talent. We have no objection to that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the share prices of the multibillion-dollar Indian tech groups \uc2 Infosys\uc2  and \uc2 Tata Consulting Services\uc2  fell by about 3% in response to the news. Both companies use the H1-B programme to deploy thousands of Indian workers to US clients.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250922el9m00209\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
