\par\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Afraid of our talent\u8217\'20\'19: India hits back against Trump\u8217\'20\'19s H-1B visa fee hike\b0\par\par\uc2 Hannah Ellis-Petersen in Delhi \par \uc2 839 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2025\par 09:44 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 India has been one of the largest beneficiaries of H-1B visas, accounting for 71% of approved visas in 2024\par \par \uc2 India has hit back at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision to impose a $100,000 fee on H-1B visas for skilled foreign workers in the US, sparking warnings by the Indian government that it would have \u8220\'20\'1Chumanitarian consequences\u8221\'20\'1D and one minister claiming they were \u8220\'20\'1Cafraid of our talent\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the US president announced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/19/trump-h1b-visa-100000-fee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new rules around the visas}}}\uc2 , which allows companies to hire foreign workers in skilled occupations like IT, healthcare and engineering, to work in the US for up to six years.\par \par \uc2 The H-1B visas are designed to allow US companies to hire skilled foreign workers in areas where there have been shortages in the domestic work market. India had become by far the largest beneficiary of H-1B visas, accounting for 71% of approved visas in 2024.\par \par \uc2 However, the White House said the visas were being \u8220\'20\'1Cabused\u8221\'20\'1D to undercut American wages and outsource IT jobs, claiming companies were laying off thousands of US workers, only to hire cheaper foreign labour from countries such as India through the scheme. Those on H-1B visas now account for 65% of the IT workforce in the US.\par \par \uc2 A statement from India\u8217\'20\'19s external affairs ministry over the weekend said the fee would have humanitarian consequences \u8220\'20\'1Cby way of the disruption caused for families\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Indian government said it \u8220\'20\'1Chopes that these disruptions can be addressed suitably by the US authorities and emphasised that the exchange of skilled workers has \u8220\'20\'1Ccontributed enormously\u8221\'20\'1D to both nations.\par \par \uc2 In comments on Sunday, India\u8217\'20\'19s commerce minister \uc2 Piyush Goyal\uc2  said: \u8220\'20\'1CThey are also a little afraid of our talent. We have no objection to that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The new $100,000 fee, which is 60 times the current cost, came into effect on Sunday. It immediately sent ripples across the IT and tech industries, which are the biggest beneficiaries of H-1B scheme.\par \par \uc2 IT firms with clients such as \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 , \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2  and \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Google\uc2  are among those reported to be impacted.\par \par \uc2 On Monday morning, the share prices of India\u8217\'20\'19s biggest IT companies such as \uc2 Infosys\uc2  and \uc2 Tata Consulting Services\uc2  slumped in response to the H-1B visa fee hike.\par \par \uc2 Nasscom\uc2 , India\u8217\'20\'19s IT trade body, said the move would \u8220\'20\'1Cpotentially have ripple effects on America\u8217\'20\'19s innovation ecosystem\u8221\'20\'1D and create \u8220\'20\'1Cconsiderable uncertainty for businesses, professionals, and students across the world\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The chief minister of the southern state of Telangana, \uc2 Revanth Reddy\uc2 , which sends a high number skilled IT workers to the US on H-1B visas urged Indian prime minister \uc2 Narendra Modi\uc2  to treat the issue on a \u8220\'20\'1Cwar footing\u8221\'20\'1D, warning that the suffering of Indian IT workers in America would be \u8220\'20\'1Cunimaginable\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Immigration lawyers in India reported receiving frantic calls over the weekend as confusion reigned over how the scheme would be implemented.\par \par \uc2 Before the White House clarified that the order applied only to new applicants and not holders of existing visas or those seeking renewals, companies including \uc2 Tata Consultancy Services\uc2 , \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  advised employees on H-1B visas to stay put or return to the US before 21 September, forcing many workers from India and China to abandon travel plans and rush back.\par \par \uc2 One Indian worker on a H-1B visa told Indian news agency PTI there was \u8220\'20\'1Cabsolute panic\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a travel ban,\u8221\'20\'1D they said, requesting anonymity for fear of losing their visa. \u8220\'20\'1CEven if a person has a valid H-1B visa stamped on their passport, if they are travelling, or are on holiday, you cannot enter the US unless they have proof of the $100,000 payment. No one knows what the process is, what the fine print is.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another India worker said the new policies by the Trump administration were making many already on H-1B visas reconsider staying in the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are really starting to question if they can continue to build their lives in the US because there\u8217\'20\'19s such a high level of uncertainty around everything now,\u8221\'20\'1D they said.\par \par \uc2 In China, which accounts for the 11.7% of all H-1B visas \u8211\'20\'13 the second highest after India \u8211\'20\'13 the Chinese government has not yet responded to the news, but online there was a sense of confusion and panic.\par \par \uc2 Some social media users accused the US of \u8220\'20\'1Cmaliciously crafting policies purely to torment H-1B holders\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven if Trump had outright banned H-1B entry with a single decree, it wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have been this insulting,\u8221\'20\'1D said one person on the video app, Xiahongshu.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have endured the hardships of studying abroad and struggling to find work in a foreign country,\u8221\'20\'1D said another. \u8220\'20\'1CNo matter how capable or hardworking we are, in an era of constantly changing immigration policies, we are just tiny specks of dust.\u8221\'20\'1DAdditional research by Lillian Yang and Jason Tzu Kuan Lu\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250922el9m000xd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
