\par\par \b \uc2 The Guardian view on Merck\u8217\'20\'19s exit: Britain\u8217\'20\'19s biopharma strategy stalls in the face of China\u8217\'20\'19s rise\b0\par\par\uc2 Editorial \par \uc2 635 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2025\par 17:51 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The industry\u8217\'20\'19s retreat from the UK reflects a deeper shift about how Beijing is rewriting the rules of innovation\par \par \uc2 When Merck {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/sep/10/us-drugmaker-merck-scraps-1bn-london-research-centre-and-cuts-125-science-jobs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 abruptly}}}\uc2  scrapped its billion-pound London research hub last week, critics blamed Britain\u8217\'20\'19s lacklustre support for life sciences and a Scrooge-like grip on \uc2 NHS\uc2  drug prices. But one important factor may have been missed. That Merck, which is also cutting jobs elsewhere \u8211\'20\'13 6,000 globally \u8211\'20\'13 is recalibrating not just in response to the UK or the US, but to China.\par \par \uc2 Merck\u8217\'20\'19s cash cow is pembrolizumab (brand name {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/360cb65b-a9ab-4fed-b8b3-7c34f2560938"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Keytruda}}}\uc2 ), an immunotherapy drug launched in 2014 that has successfully treated advanced melanoma, head and neck, lung, cervical and other cancers. It blocks an antibody called PD-1, teaching the immune system to fight the cancer. Because some patients are out of otheroptions, the results sometimes seem miraculous.\par \par \uc2 But Keytruda\u8217\'20\'19s patent runs out in 2028. And now there is another drug on the global market, called ivonescimab, made by \uc2 Akeso Biopharma\uc2 . In head-to-head trials, reported in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736%2824%2902722-3/fulltext"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lancet}}}\uc2  journal in March, the innovative Chinese drug, which hits an additional tumour target, outperformed Merck\u8217\'20\'19s \u8211\'20\'13 and it\u8217\'20\'19s cheaper. The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.who.int/news/item/05-09-2025-who-updates-list-of-essential-medicines-to-include-key-cancer--diabetes-treatments"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lists}}}\uc2  PD-1 drugs as essential medicines worldwide, with no special status for Keytruda. No wonder Merck is cutting costs.\par \par \uc2 Experts warn that this is no flash in the pan. Both treatments are products of a shift in the technology from pharma to biopharma. The former delivered mass-market drugs; the latter produces bespoke, high\u8209\'20\'11cost therapies. Richard Sullivan, professor of cancer and global health at King\u8217\'20\'19s College London, and Lewis Husain, a research fellow at \uc2 the Institute of Development Studies\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cgdev.org/blog/escaping-kindleberger-trap-what-role-china-reshaping-global-health-low-cooperation-world"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  earlier this year that China, once seen as a follower, is now emerging as a global leader in cutting-edge biopharmaceutical treatments, reshaping the competitive landscape.\par \par \uc2 The closure of Merck\u8217\'20\'19s UK research centre, with the loss of 125 scientist positions, is another setback for the government\u8217\'20\'19s biopharma ambitions. \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  has already scrapped vaccine research expansion in Liverpool. In an interview with the BBC, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/sep/11/big-pharma-will-halt-investments-in-uk-warns-eminent-scientist-sir-john-bell"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sir John Bell}}}\uc2 , Oxford\u8217\'20\'19s former regius professor of medicine, said industry bosses had told him that they see Britain as a poor bet. They demand more subsidies, higher drug-price caps and fast \uc2 NHS\uc2  approval for expensive new biopharma treatments. Yet these niche therapies benefit only a small number of patients, and higher spending on them inevitably leaves less for everyone else\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump is setting the mood music, demanding drug firms make their medicines in the US. Merck, known as \uc2 MSD\uc2  in Europe but headquartered in New Jersey, will be looking to oblige. The nominally Anglo-Swedish AstraZeneca, which followed Merck by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ainvest.com/news/astrazeneca-pauses-200m-cambridge-investment-industry-woes-2509/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announcing}}}\uc2 it was \u8220\'20\'1Cpausing\u8221\'20\'1D a \u163\'00\'A3200m research expansion in Cambridge, in July {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jul/22/astrazeneca-invest-us-trump-tariff-threats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pledged}}}\uc2  to spend \u163\'00\'A337bn on research and development and manufacturing inthe US by 2030.\par \par \uc2 But this is a global industry. \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 , \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , Roche and \uc2 GSK\uc2  are among the big players now doingdeals to develop new Chinese drugs. Countries will buy the best medicines they can afford, which may be Chinese. Britain can\u8217\'20\'19t just rely on price caps and vague {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/life-sciences-sector-plan-to-grow-economy-and-transform-nhs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 industrial strategy}}}\uc2  promises. It needs to examine how rivals structure their incentives, regulation and support \u8211\'20\'13 and then decide where it can compete. That doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean simply inflating the \uc2 NHS\uc2  drugs bill, but targeting strengths. With the US retreating from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://institute.global/insights/public-services/the-mrna-multiplier-making-the-uk-a-world-leader-in-a-vital-health"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mRNA}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13the platform behind the most widespread Covidvaccines \u8211\'20\'13 Britain has a rare opening.\par \par \uc2 * Do you have an opinion on the issues raised in this article? If you would like to submit a response of up to 300 words by email to be considered for publication in our{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tone/letters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  letters}}}\uc2  section, please {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:guardian.letters@theguardian.com?body=Please%20include%20your%20name,%20full%20postal%20address%20and%20phone%20number%20with%20your%20letter%20below.%20Letters%20are%20usually%20published%20with%20the%20author%27s%20name%20and%20city/town/village.%20The%20rest%20of%20the%20information%20is%20for%20verification%20only%20and%20to%20contact%20you%20where%20necessary."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 click here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250915el9f005po\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
