\par\par \b \uc2 The chilling effect of Title VI investigations: the professors accused of antisemitism\b0\par\par\uc2 Alice Speri \par \uc2 3,153 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2025\par 21:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Exclusive: Andrea Brower quits \uc2 Gonzaga University\uc2  after the Civil Rights Act is used to challenge her pro-Gaza activism\par \par \uc2 When Andrea Brower was hired two years ago as the lead instructor for \uc2 Gonzaga University\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s solidarity and social justice program, she thought she had landed a dream job. Brower was tasked with \u8220\'20\'1Cmentoring students in their social justice pursuits\u8221\'20\'1D at the private, liberal arts university in eastern Washington state. The school\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gonzaga.edu/about/our-mission-jesuit-values/mission-statement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jesuit mission}}}\uc2  highlighted a commitment to diversity, global engagement and \u8220\'20\'1Csolidarity with the poor and vulnerable\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a perfect fit for Brower, who had come to academia by way of grassroots, environmental activism in her native Hawaii.\par \par \uc2 Brower felt at first that her activist credentials were welcome, but when she started organizing around issues on campus \u8211\'20\'13 criticizing the treatment of adjuncts and rising administrator salaries and speaking out against the university\u8217\'20\'19s investments in fossil fuels \u8211\'20\'13 she began to run into opposition from university administrators.\par \par \uc2 But it was after she began to protest against Israel\u8217\'20\'19s war in Gaza and criticize Gonzaga\u8217\'20\'19s investments in weapons manufacturers that her troubles really started. Last year, a colleague Brower had never met filed a formal complaint accusing her of antisemitism. It took eight months for the university to conclude that the allegations were unsubstantiated.\par \par \uc2 The months-long ordeal ultimately led Brower to resign from her position rather than work for an institution increasingly willing to \u8220\'20\'1Crepress critical thought and dissent\u8221\'20\'1D, she wrote in her {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rangemedia.co/gonzaga-andrea-brower-resignation-letter"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 resignation letter}}}\uc2 , which she made public on Thursday. \u8220\'20\'1CI can bear the harassment, the bullying, the character slander, but what I will not tolerate is being constricted from doing honest, rigorous teaching,\u8221\'20\'1D she said in an exclusive interview before she resigned. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t know how I\u8217\'20\'19m going to teach in an environment where I know I am being so closely watched and surveilled by administrators.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for Gonzaga did not answer a detailed list of questions, saying the university \u8220\'20\'1Cdoes not discuss or disclose details about any person\u8217\'20\'19s employment\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Brower\u8217\'20\'19s case is hardly isolated. As the Trump administration has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/may/01/trump-administration-civil-rights-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 turned civil rights legislation}}}\uc2  into a cudgel to root out progressive politics on US campuses, with billions of dollars in federal funding on the line, pro-Palestinian professors have increasingly been caught in the crossfire. But while the government\u8217\'20\'19s crackdown on universities has drawn widespread condemnation, a parallel campaign targeting specific faculty members has received far less attention. Often initiated by actors seizing on the political climate and the vulnerability of universities, accusations against professors of antisemitism have come at a great professional and personal cost for many.\par \par \uc2 At the heart of the campaign against both universities and faculty are civil rights investigations over alleged discrimination. Often, they invoke Title VI, the provision of the landmark Civil Rights Act of 1964, which prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color and national origin in programs that receive federal funds, as most universities do. During his first term, Trump expanded Title VI protections to Jewish students \u8211\'20\'13 on the basis that while religion is not a protected category under the act, students\u8217\'20\'19 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://brandeiscenter.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/title06.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ethnic or ancestral background is}}}\uc2 . Trump also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/executive-order-combating-anti-semitism/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 encouraged institutions}}}\uc2  to refer to a controversial definition that conflates some criticism of Israel with antisemitism when applying the law.\par \par \uc2 Since the beginning of Israel\u8217\'20\'19s war in Gaza, the federal government has launched 99 antisemitism investigations into universities \u8211\'20\'13 with a steep increase following Trump\u8217\'20\'19s inauguration, according to the Middle East Studies Association (Mesa), which has been tracking them for a forthcoming study. Already, leading universities have reached settlements with the administration \u8211\'20\'13 including millions in payouts and a slew of measures ostensibly aimed at fighting antisemitism, even as a federal judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/03/trump-administration-harvard-funding-cut"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled last week}}}\uc2  in a suit brought by Harvard that the administration \u8220\'20\'1Cused antisemitism as a smokescreen for a targeted, ideologically-motivated assault on this country\u8217\'20\'19s premier universities\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Since October 2023, another 28 lawsuits have been filed against universities by students or outside groups accusing them of violating civil rights law by enabling antisemitism on campuses, and some have already resulted in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/29/ucla-lawsuit-settlement-jewish-students"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 multimillion-dollar settlements}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As they have come under growing pressure, some schools have responded, advocates say, by throwing faculty under the bus \u8211\'20\'13 including by launching lengthy investigations over allegations they might have dismissed in the past.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSometimes administrators are even saying that this is something we would normally be able to dismiss out of hand, but we are under this Title VI investigation and therefore we have to go through these steps,\u8221\'20\'1D said Isaac Kamola, who leads the \uc2 American Association of University Professors\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 Center for the Defense of Academic Freedom and has been supporting many of the targeted faculty. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd so what you have is this big political infrastructure and coordinated rightwing efforts to say, we don\u8217\'20\'19t like that individual \u8230\'20\'26 and the administrations are basically not able to defend them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s hard to know exactly how many professors have faced internal investigations over their pro-Palestinian speech, but in recent months, universities have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.insidehighered.com/news/students/diversity/2025/07/14/colleges-hire-title-vi-coordinators-amid-federal-scrutiny"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rushed to hire}}}\uc2  Title VI coordinators to handle the surge of antisemitism claims raised against faculty members. \u8220\'20\'1CDozens\u8221\'20\'1D of educators have been affected, said Anna Feder, an organizer with the Palestine Anti-Repression Network, a nationwide coalition of educators who have come under fire for their advocacy.\par \par \uc2 Feder is a curator who was laid off from her role at Emerson College\u8217\'20\'19s visual media arts department last year over, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://berkeleybeacon.com/85628-2/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she believes}}}\uc2 , a dispute surrounding her decision to screen the documentary {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2023/nov/12/israelism-documentary-american-jewish-israel-palestine-conflict"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Israelism}}}\uc2 , which follows young Jewish Americans becoming disillusioned with Zionism. (In April, Feder sued Emerson for wrongful termination, alleging that she was targeted for her \u8220\'20\'1Cpersonal political activism\u8221\'20\'1D. A spokesperson for the university declined to comment on personnel matters and pending litigation but said that the college \u8220\'20\'1Cunequivocally denies any allegation of wrongdoing or retaliation\u8221\'20\'1D.)\par \par \uc2 While some high-profile cases received significant attention \u8211\'20\'13 like those of Columbia professor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/13/pro-palestine-columbia-professor-katherine-franke"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Katherine Franke}}}\uc2  and Muhlenberg College professor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/oct/24/university-professors-discipline-palestine-support"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Maura Finkelstein}}}\uc2 , both tenured professors who were forced out and fired, respectively, over their pro-Palestinian speech \u8211\'20\'13 most professors dealing with antisemitism allegations are getting far less attention, Feder noted.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople don\u8217\'20\'19t understand how pervasive it is, and how most people are isolated when it happens,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Making this up as they go\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Brower regularly spoke out during the first year of Israel\u8217\'20\'19s war in Gaza as protests mounted on Gonzaga\u8217\'20\'19s campus and beyond. She joined protests calling for Palestinians\u8217\'20\'19 rights, called out Gonzaga\u8217\'20\'19s connections to defense contractors, and spoke at a campus walkout where she praised students\u8217\'20\'19 \u8220\'20\'1Cmoral clarity\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But tensions around her activism were escalating. Someone ripped off signs she had put outside her office reading \u8220\'20\'1Cpeace and liberation for all\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Clet Gaza live\u8221\'20\'1D. She caught campus security taking photos of her office, leading to a heated exchange. Colleagues she did not personally know accused her of \u8220\'20\'1CHolocaust denial\u8221\'20\'1D on faculty email lists. A sticker affixed to her office door called her an \u8220\'20\'1Cextremist\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Then in December 2024, while she was off for winter break, Brower received an email from the university\u8217\'20\'19s Office of Inclusive Excellence notifying her that a colleague had filed a complaint accusing her of violating institutional policy and that the university would conduct \u8220\'20\'1Ca prompt, thorough, and impartial investigation of these allegations\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 When Brower pressed the administration for details of the allegations raised against her, the administrator who contacted her denied her request. \u8220\'20\'1CI was completely in the dark,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 The interview took place nearly a month later, and to Brower, it felt hostile and deeply uncomfortable. \u8220\'20\'1CI was told at the very start, you are presumed innocent, basically until proven guilty,\u8221\'20\'1D she recalled. \u8220\'20\'1CBut the experience of going through the process felt as if they were presuming guilt from the get-go.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It was not until the investigation was closed in July that Brower was read the charges she had faced. It turned out the complainant had accused her of antisemitism for attending a student-run protest in which other speakers used the word \u8220\'20\'1Cgenocide\u8221\'20\'1D in reference to Israel\u8217\'20\'19s actions in Gaza. They complained that she had shared with a faculty mailing list a letter signed by hundreds of Gonzaga students accusing the university of having fallen into a \u8220\'20\'1Csinister trap of division, silence, and censorship\u8221\'20\'1D over the war \u8211\'20\'13 phrasing that the complainant claimed was antisemitic. And they pointed to another letter signed by 70 faculty members criticizing the university\u8217\'20\'19s response to student protests, which Brower forwarded to the same list at a time \u8220\'20\'1Ctoo close\u8221\'20\'1D to the anniversary of the 7 October \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacks, she was told.\par \par \uc2 The university should have quickly concluded that \u8220\'20\'1Cthis is not a claim worth investigating\u8221\'20\'1D, Brower said of the allegations. Instead, she said they tried to use the complaint \u8220\'20\'1Cas a sort of backdoor mechanism to shut me up\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 When she sat down for her interview, Brower quickly concluded that the investigator assigned to her case had \u8220\'20\'1Cvery little understanding of the conflict between Israel and Palestine or the ideological conflict about how we talk about these things in the US\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 According to Brower, notes by an attorney who joined her at the meeting and a summary of the interview provided by the university, the investigator asked whether Brower had spoken about the \u8220\'20\'1CJewish people as committing genocide\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 to which she replied that she had used the word \u8220\'20\'1Cgenocide\u8221\'20\'1D in reference to the Israeli government\u8217\'20\'19s actions and that she would never say \u8220\'20\'1Ca people\u8221\'20\'1D are committing genocide. \u8220\'20\'1CThey asked me questions that intentionally conflated Jewish people with Israel,\u8221\'20\'1D Brower said, suggesting the investigator did not have a grasp of the issues at the heart of the matter. \u8220\'20\'1CThey did this thing that made it very difficult to clearly answer questions \u8211\'20\'13 I think because the investigator themselves didn\u8217\'20\'19t really understand what they were investigating.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As she awaited resolution of her case, Brower wrote an email to university administrators asking what the university planned to do to protect her. The university\u8217\'20\'19s response came days later \u8211\'20\'13 offering little guidance but including phone numbers for campus security and a mental health line \u8220\'20\'1Cfor the anxious feelings and concerns about your personal safety\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was highly insulting, and kind of shows their ignorance as to what was happening,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CMost universities are kind of making this up as they go, and that tends not to fare well for those of us who are being accused.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Colleagues are taking note\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Jewish faculty have also faced antisemitism investigations. Judith Norman, a tenured professor of philosophy at Trinity University, a small, private liberal arts institution in San Antonio, Texas, had been involved in pro-Palestinian organizing for more than 25 years as a member of the local Jewish Voice for Peace chapter. Norman\u8217\'20\'19s advocacy was not always welcomed by colleagues and administrators but she faced no significant opposition to it until the current war in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Since then, the university has received half a dozen antisemitism complaints against her and launched two Title VI investigations in response, she said. While the university did not tell Norman who filed the complaints, at least one appears to have come through an anonymous whistleblower tip line. One stemmed from a social media post in which Norman shared a nationwide call to \u8220\'20\'1CDrop Hillel\u8221\'20\'1D, the pro-Israel organization active on many campuses \u8211\'20\'13 while others, she said, were false, including one accusation that she had been involved in an incident in which students wrote pro-Palestinian slogans in chalk on the sidewalk and another that she had invited speakers to campus \u8220\'20\'1Cwho denied Jewish indigeneity in Israel\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In Norman\u8217\'20\'19s case, none of the accusations stuck \u8211\'20\'13 even after the university hired an officer who spent dozens of hours on her case and produced a 60-page report ultimately clearing her of all charges. (Norman was allowed to read the report but not keep a copy of it.) Norman credited the investigator with being \u8220\'20\'1Cthorough and unbiased\u8221\'20\'1D and said her case was a \u8220\'20\'1Cbest-case scenario\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for Trinity did not answer questions about Norman\u8217\'20\'19s case but wrote in a statement that \u8220\'20\'1Cin accordance with our obligations under Title VI, we are committed to promptly and effectively addressing any harassment based on race, color, or national origin to help ensure a safe and inclusive environment for all\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think Title VI is a good thing in general. People who are discriminated against ought to have a formal process, for their protection, and I\u8217\'20\'19m glad that the university takes Title VI seriously,\u8221\'20\'1D Norman said.\par \par \uc2 But she warned that the specter of Title VI investigations was creating a chilling effect on faculty. \u8220\'20\'1CMy colleagues have taken note of what\u8217\'20\'19s happening to me,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, adding that many are young and untenured. \u8220\'20\'1CThe degree of intimidation that is caused by just the fact that I\u8217\'20\'19m being dragged through these processes is absolutely tangible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While the formal investigations left her nervous to speak in class, wondering who might be taking notes to use against her, she said she had no plans to cut back her advocacy. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve been to Gaza,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, \u8220\'20\'1Cand there is no way I\u8217\'20\'19m going to shut up about Gaza.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Litigating antisemitism\par \par \uc2 Other professors are being taken directly to courts, which are being asked to litigate questions around when anti-Zionism crosses into antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 Michel DeGraff, a linguistics professor at the \uc2 Massachusetts Institute of Technology\uc2 , has been embroiled in a nearly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.chronicle.com/article/a-curricular-clash-at-mit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two-year, contentious dispute}}}\uc2  with the university after he proposed teaching a class about language and decolonization in Palestine, Israel and Haiti. (DeGraff was born and raised in Haiti, and the country is his primary area of study.) The linguistics department rejected the class, citing DeGraff\u8217\'20\'19s lack of expertise on the subject; he ultimately taught the course as an unsanctioned {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.google.com/document/d/1qKDfAtQ9utIs-Z5jKdqYXScY6dYdXQ1GhvK4iQ5Nu6k/edit?tab=t.0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lecture series}}}\uc2 , but the conflict with both the department and the university led to him being temporarily pushed out of the linguistics department and denied a scheduled raise.\par \par \uc2 In June, the Brandeis Center, a legal group fighting alleged antisemitism on campuses, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://brandeiscenter.com/lawsuit-mit-professor-harassed-israeli-researcher-jewish-student-as-president-stood-by-masslive/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sued both MIT and DeGraff}}}\uc2 , accusing him of \u8220\'20\'1Charassing\u8221\'20\'1D an Israeli researcher and a Jewish student, and university leadership of turning MIT into a \u8220\'20\'1Cbreeding ground of anti-Semitic and anti-Israeli hate\u8221\'20\'1D. The suit seeks punitive and emotional damages.\par \par \uc2 DeGraff\u8217\'20\'19s dispute with the student began after the latter took to social media to denounce the \u8220\'20\'1Cextremely dangerous rhetoric\u8221\'20\'1D in the professor\u8217\'20\'19s lecture series. According to the complaint, DeGraff posted on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  about the Zionist \u8220\'20\'1Cmind infection\u8221\'20\'1D and accused well-known \u8220\'20\'1CJewish student life organizations\u8221\'20\'1D such as Hillel and Chabad of \u8220\'20\'1Cfund[ing]\u8221\'20\'1D this \u8220\'20\'1Cmind infection\u8221\'20\'1D. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://brandeiscenter.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/06.25.2025-MIT-Complaint.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 complaint also alleges that}}}\uc2  DeGraff posted the researcher\u8217\'20\'19s name and image on social media, revealing that he had served in the Israeli military (which is compulsory for Israeli citizens).\par \par \uc2 The subsequent exchange, over social media posts and department-wide emails, was \u8220\'20\'1Cso extreme and intolerable that it forced [the student] to leave MIT\u8221\'20\'1D, the complaint further alleged. In the lawsuit, the Brandeis Center also argued that \u8220\'20\'1CZionist is often a codeword for Jew\u8221\'20\'1D. The center is a leading force behind the ongoing push to punish alleged antisemitism on campuses, and is behind dozens of Title VI complaints, according to Mesa\u8217\'20\'19s forthcoming analysis. In addition to MIT, the group has sued Berkeley, Stanford and Harvard.\par \par \uc2 The student, William Sussman, did not respond to a request for comment, while the researcher, Lior Alon, referred questions to the Brandeis Center. Paul Eckles, a senior attorney at the center, said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe feel very confident in the claims we\u8217\'20\'19ve asserted and we\u8217\'20\'19ll have our day in court.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for MIT declined to comment on DeGraff\u8217\'20\'19s case, citing policies on individual personnel matters, but said that the university \u8220\'20\'1Cwill defend itself in court regarding the allegations raised in the lawsuit\u8221\'20\'1D. The spokesperson also cited the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://president.mit.edu/writing-speeches/video-transcript-tensions-campus"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 2023 statement}}}\uc2  by the university president, \uc2 Sally Kornbluth\uc2 , that \u8220\'20\'1Cantisemitism is real, and it is rising in the world. We cannot let it poison our community.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 DeGraff denied that his actions constituted antisemitism. He cited multiple academics, including the Israeli philologist Nurit Peled-Elhanan, a guest speaker in DeGraff\u8217\'20\'19s series, who used the phrase \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1057/9780230611702_21"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mind infection}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D in regards to how Israeli children are taught to view their Arab neighbors. He also noted that a related concept \u8211\'20\'13 that of \u8220\'20\'1Cviruses of the mind\u8221\'20\'1D, a reference to how hate narratives \u8220\'20\'1Cspread like viruses\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 had a long history in academia. And he argued that the researcher he is accused of doxing was a \u8220\'20\'1Cpublic figure\u8221\'20\'1D who had made a number of media appearances condemning pro-Palestinian protests at MIT.\par \par \uc2 Eckles, the Brandeis attorney, argued that DeGraff\u8217\'20\'19s speech \u8220\'20\'1Crises above just mere political discourse\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13and his combative style has earned DeGraff {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thetech.com/2024/07/11/faculty-response-to-de-graff"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 many critics}}}\uc2  among his colleagues.\par \par \uc2 But DeGraff sees the fight over words as part of his job as a linguist, and takes particular issue with MIT\u8217\'20\'19s leadership and the linguistics department \u8211\'20\'13 where he arrived in the 1990s, drawn by the scholarship of the linguist and pro-Palestinian advocate Noam Chomsky \u8211\'20\'13 for failing to recognize what he called the \u8220\'20\'1Cweaponizing\u8221\'20\'1D of language.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLanguage is such a powerful tool of liberation, I felt that this was a place for me to be,\u8221\'20\'1D DeGraff said. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m trying to uphold Chomsky\u8217\'20\'19s principles, for intellectuals to be responsible towards the truth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Shifting tides\par \par \uc2 Brower was hired at a time when universities catered to students increasingly passionate about social justice. But the experience of being investigated over her advocacy for Palestinians\u8217\'20\'19 rights has led her to the conclusion that that commitment was hollow.\par \par \uc2 In July, Brower was cleared of any violations of university policy. But in a meeting closing her case \u8211\'20\'13 in which she finally learned what she had been accused of \u8211\'20\'13 the administrator in charge of the process told her that a number of \u8220\'20\'1Cconcerns\u8221\'20\'1D about her conduct would be referred to the school\u8217\'20\'19s leadership for \u8220\'20\'1Cintervention\u8221\'20\'1D, even though they did not amount to formal violations. They refused to provide those concerns in writing but indicated that they related to Brower\u8217\'20\'19s lack of \u8220\'20\'1Cunderstanding\u8221\'20\'1D of Israel and Palestine, and to the allegations that she created conflict on campus and made Jewish students and faculty feel unsafe.\par \par \uc2 The experience left Brower deeply disillusioned with her university, leading to her decision to quit. Although she struggled with the decision to leave her students, she concluded that Gonzaga was not an institution where she could do \u8220\'20\'1Cmeaningful work\u8221\'20\'1D, she said. In her resignation letter, she offered to continue to mentor her students informally this coming semester.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we cannot speak honestly, clearly and loudly about the genocidal starvation of children \u8211\'20\'13 or at minimum support our brave students who are \u8211\'20\'13 what is our role as scholars?\u8221\'20\'1D she wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CNow is not the time for quiet.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250911el9b002mh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
