\par\par \b \uc2 US economic picture weakens as jobless claims and inflation both rise \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 3,469 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2025\par 14:14 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Economists say trade war is driving up US prices, as inflation picks up in AugustLosses at \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  and \uc2 Waitrose\uc2  owner almost triple to \u163\'00\'A388m\par \par \uc2 3.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 A quick recap\par \par \uc2 More clouds are gathering over the US economy, as new data shows a rise in joblessness as inflation picks up.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/sep/11/john-lewis-deeper-loss-london-tube-strike-fourth-day-ecb-interest-rates-us-inflation-business-live-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-68c2c84f8f086519d326feab#block-68c2c84f8f086519d326feab"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Applications for US unemployment benefits jumped last week}}}\uc2  to the highest level in almost four years; Initial claims rose by 27,000 to 263,000 in the week to 6 September, the highest since October 2021.\par \par \uc2 This latest sign of a weakening jobs market has pushed shares higher on Wall Street, as investors anticipate cuts to US interest rates.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s despite {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/sep/11/john-lewis-deeper-loss-london-tube-strike-fourth-day-ecb-interest-rates-us-inflation-business-live-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-68c2c1448f0880bae9c516ba#block-68c2c1448f0880bae9c516ba"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US inflation rising in August, with the consumer prices up by 2.9% over the last year}}}\uc2 , up from 2.7% in July.\par \par \uc2 Rising food and housing costs were a factor, while economists warned that companies were passing on the cost of tariffs to consumers.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/sep/11/john-lewis-deeper-loss-london-tube-strike-fourth-day-ecb-interest-rates-us-inflation-business-live-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-68c2b4a28f084b3c85a4c896#block-68c2b4a28f084b3c85a4c896"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In the eurozone, the European Central Bank has left interest rates on hold today}}}\uc2 , while nudging some of its growth and inflation forecasts a litte higher.\par \par \uc2 In the UK, the owner of \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  and \uc2 Waitrose\uc2  has said its losses nearly tripled to \u163\'00\'A388m in the first half of this year, as it took a hit from restructuring costs as well as new tax and regulatory charges.\par \par \uc2 John Lewis Partnership\uc2 , which operates 36 department stores and more than 300 \uc2 Waitrose\uc2  supermarkets, said new packaging regulations and increased national insurance contributions had cost it \u163\'00\'A329m, while it spent \u163\'00\'A354m on restructuring its business, mainly on replacing outdated technology.\par \par \uc2 As a result, the employee-owned group\u8217\'20\'19s half year pre-tax losses widened from \u163\'00\'A330m over the same period a year before, despite a 4% rise in sales to \u163\'00\'A36.2bn in the six months to 26 July.\par \par \uc2 2.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 Wall Street opens higher on rate cut hopes\par \par \uc2 Wall Street\u8217\'20\'19s main indexes have opened higher, despite today\u8217\'20\'19s data showing a jump in unemployment claims and consumer prices.\par \par \uc2 Rather than worrying about the state of the labor market, traders seem to be betting that the USFederalReserve is on track to cut interest rates next week.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average has risen by 218 points, or 0.5%, in early trading to 45,708 points.\par \par \uc2 The broader S&P 500 index is 0.25% higher.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at ING agree that the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s focus is now the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 James Knightley\uc2 , ING\u8217\'20\'19s chief international economist, explains:\par \par \uc2 2.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 Curiously, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/sep/11/john-lewis-deeper-loss-london-tube-strike-fourth-day-ecb-interest-rates-us-inflation-business-live-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-68c2c84f8f086519d326feab#block-68c2c84f8f086519d326feab"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 much of the increase in US jobless claims last week}}}\uc2  was recorded in Texas.\par \par \uc2 The number of initial claims (a proxy for layoffs) in Texas almost doubled in the week to 6 September, to 31,908 from 16,604 in the previous seven days.\par \par \uc2 There was also an increase of 2,980 in Michigan, but many other states recorded a drop in claims (before seasonal adjustments).\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  economist Eliza Winger says:\par \par \uc2 2.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 Experts: Fed likely to cut rates to help weak jobs market\par \par \uc2 The jump in US jobless claims last week, and the pick-up in inflation in August, are a worrying sign for America\u8217\'20\'19s economy.\par \par \uc2 Together, they paint a picture of an economy losing jobs, as consumers are hit by rising prices in the shops, as tariffs are passed onto consumers.\par \par \uc2 Atakan Bakiskan, US economist at Berenberg bank, explains:\par \par \uc2 Kathleen Brooks\uc2 , research director at XTB, agrees that the Fed should prioritise the \u8216\'20\'18weak\u8217\'20\'19 jobs market, saying:\par \par \uc2 2.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 New US unemployment claims hit near four-year high\par \par \uc2 Ouch! The number of Americans filing new claims for jobless support has hit its highest level in almost four years.\par \par \uc2 The number of \u8216\'20\'18initial claims\u8217\'20\'19 for unemployment benefits jumped by 27,000 last week to 263,000, the highest level for initial claims since October 23, 2021.\par \par \uc2 That will reinforce concerns that the US labor market has weakened, following recent weak non-farm payroll reports, and Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s news that{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/sep/09/us-created-fewer-jobs-march-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  the US added 911,000 fewer jobs than first estimated for the year to March}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Aaron Hill, chief market analyst at FP Markets, says the jobs slowdown will fan speculation that the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  will cut interest rates, adding:\u8203\'20\'0B\par \par \uc2 1.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 Companies 'passing rising costs from tariffs onto consumers'\par \par \uc2 US inflation \u8220\'20\'1Ccontinued its march higher\u8221\'20\'1D in August, reports KatyStoves, investment manager at MattioliWoods.\par \par \uc2 Stoves says US companies are now passing the cost of tariffs onto customers:\par \par \uc2 1.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 The prices of all six major American grocery store food groups rose in August, today\u8217\'20\'19s US inflation report shows.\par \par \uc2 The index for fruitsandvegetables rose 1.6% over the month, as tomato prices jumped by 4.5% and apple prices increased by 3.5%.\par \par \uc2 The meats, poultry, fish and eggs index increased by 1.0% in August with the beef index up by 2.7%. On an annual basis, meats, poultry, fish, and eggs were 5.6% more expensive than a year ago.\par \par \uc2 The index for nonalcoholicbeverages increased by 0.6% during August and the index for other food at home increased 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 Dairy and related products index and cereals andbakery products both rose by 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 1.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 Tariffs 'clearly hitting' as US inflation rises\par \par \uc2 On a monthly basis, the US inflation report shows prices rose by 0.4% in August alone.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s twice as fast as the 0.2% rise in prices recorded in July.\par \par \uc2 The BLS reports that housing (shelter) and food were key drivers:\par \par \uc2 Heather Long, chief economist at credit union NavyFederal, says \u8220\'20\'1CTariffs are clearly hitting now\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 1.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 US inflation rises to 2.9%\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: US inflation jumped last month, which could harden fears that Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s trade war is driving up the cost of living for Americans.\par \par \uc2 The US consumer prices index rose by 2.9% in the year to August, up from 2.7% in July, in line with forecasts.\par \par \uc2 Food prices rose by 3.2% over the year, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  reports, while energy prices were only up by 0.2% over the last year.\par \par \uc2 Core inflation, which strips out food and energy costs, rose by 3.1% in the 12 months to August.\par \par \uc2 1.31pm BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ecb.europa.eu/press/pr/date/2025/html/ecb.mp250911~6afb7a9490.en.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s statement}}}\uc2  from the \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2  doesn\u8217\'20\'19t include any assessment of the eurozone economy, or cite any particular risk worrying policymakers.\par \par \uc2 It does, though, repeat the \uc2 ECB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s pledge that it is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot pre-committing\u8221\'20\'1D to a particular path for interest rates, and will set policy based on data and infation dynamics.\par \par \uc2 It says:\par \par \uc2 Reuters\u8217\'20\'19 correspondent FrancescoCanepa says this takes being \u8220\'20\'1Cdeliberately\u8221\'20\'1D uninformative to new levels\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 1.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 ECB\uc2  lifts inflation forecast for 2025 and 2026\par \par \uc2 We also have new inflation forecasts from the \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They now predict the cost of living will rise a little faster than it had expected this year and next.\par \par \uc2 ECB\uc2  forecasts now estimate headline inflation will average 2.1% in 2025, up from 2% forecast in June.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is then expected to slip to 1.7% in 2016, up from the 1.6% forecast three months ago, For 2027, it\u8217\'20\'19s seen at 1.9%, below the 2.0% forecast in June.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ECB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s goal is to keep inflation at 2% over the medium term.\par \par \uc2 1.19pm BST\par \par \uc2 New \uc2 ECB\uc2  growth forecasts\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ECB\uc2  has also raised its forecast for eurozone growth this year (hurrah!), but tempered this by slightly cutting its 2026 forecast.\par \par \uc2 It says:\par \par \uc2 1.16pm BST\par \par \uc2 ECB\uc2  leaves eurozone interest rates on hold\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: The \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2  has left interest rates across the eurozone unchanged, at its latest policy meeting.\par \par \uc2 Announcing the decision, the \uc2 ECB\uc2  says:\par \par \uc2 The decision means:\par \par \uc2 * The \uc2 ECB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s deposit facility, used by banks to make overnight deposits with the Eurosystem, remains at 2%.\par \par \uc2 * The main refinancing operations rate, paid when banks can borrow funds from the \uc2 ECB\uc2  on a weekly basis, remains at 2.15%.\par \par \uc2 * The marginal lending facility rate, paid when banks borrow overnight from the \uc2 ECB\uc2 , remains at 2.4%\par \par \uc2 1.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 Tube strike latest\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile in London, the underground system remains seriously disrupted by today\u8217\'20\'19s strike action.\par \par \uc2 Most tube lines are still suspended, with three exceptions:\par \par \uc2 * District Line: Service is operating with minor delays between Upminster and Whitechapel only\par \par \uc2 * Metropolitan Line: Service is operating with minor delays between Baker Street and Watford only.\par \par \uc2 * Piccadilly Line: Service is operating with minor delays between Acton Town and South Harrow and Arnos grove to Cockfosters only.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 12.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 Turkey cuts interest rates by more than expected\par \par \uc2 Over in Istanbul, \uc2 Turkey\u8217\'20\'19s central bank\uc2  has announced a large cut to interest rates, but borrowing costs still remain high.\par \par \uc2 Turkey\u8217\'20\'19s central bank\uc2  has lowered its policy interest rate by 250 basis points to 40.5%, a slightly bigger cut than expected.\par \par \uc2 It eased policy after inflation slowed to 32.95% in August 2025 from 33.52% in July.\par \par \uc2 The Central Bank of the Republic of T\u252\'00\'FCrkiye explained:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 12.11pm BST\par \par \uc2 Back in the UK, the motor finance firm which lost a key case at the supreme court last month has put aside an extra \u163\'00\'A3122m for compensation for UK drivers, but denied that the ruling set precedent for mass payouts.\par \par \uc2 FirstRand\uc2 , which owns UK car lender MotoNovo, said it had taken a 2.7 billion rand provision, and absorbed another R253m in legal and professional fees, in relation to the car finance case, resulting in a R2.9bn (\u163\'00\'A3122m) charge for the financial year to 30 June.\par \par \uc2 However, this did not hinder its financial success, having reported a 10% rise in net profit to R41.82bn - marking a fresh record high, according to Bloomberg.\par \par \uc2 The South African lender is steeling itself for a compensation scheme that could collectively cost lenders \u163\'00\'A318bn. The \uc2 FCA\uc2  scheme, which is due to go out for consultation next month, is meant to draw a line under the car finance scandal, compensating millions of drivers who were overcharged as a result of controversial commission arrangements between lenders and car dealers.\par \par \uc2 It follows a supreme court ruling in August, which ruled against \uc2 FirstRand\uc2  and sided with a former borrower.\par \par \uc2 Judges upheld the case lodged by Marcus Johnson, saying the terms of the deal were \u8220\'20\'1Cunfair\u8221\'20\'1D, due in part to the size of the commission that was paid to the car dealer, as well as the fact that it was never disclosed that \uc2 FirstRand\uc2  had first dibs on the contract.\par \par \uc2 But in filings released alongside its annual report on Thursday, \uc2 FirstRand\uc2  pushed back against any suggestions that Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s case would open the door to a raft of costly claims against the South African lender.\par \par \uc2 It said:\par \par \uc2 12.07pm BST\par \par \uc2 In another sign of trouble in Europe\u8217\'20\'19s largest economy, German business insolvencies have risen this year.\par \par \uc2 The number of standard insolvencies filed in Germany increased by 11.6% in August 2025 compared to the same month last year, statistics body Destatis reported this morning.\par \par \uc2 Destatis also showed there were 12.2% more corporate insolvencies in the first half of this year than in January-June 2024, with consumer insolvencies up by 7.5%.\par \par \uc2 11.52am BST\par \par \uc2 The slowdown in German exports may be on the \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2  governing council\u8217\'20\'19s mind when it sets interest rates later today.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ECB\uc2  is expected to leave eurozone interest rates on hold, as it weighs up the state of the eurozone economy and the outlook for inflation.\par \par \uc2 Michael Field, chief equity strategist at \uc2 Morningstar\uc2 , says:\par \par \uc2 11.16am BST\par \par \uc2 German exports threatened by trade war tariffs, and weak demand\par \par \uc2 Germany\u8217\'20\'19s exporters are warning today that their overseas sales will shrink this year, due to weakening global demand, higher domestic costs and rising protectionism.\par \par \uc2 The BGA trade association has predicted that German exports will slump by 2.5% this year, and warned that many firms are reporting stagnant or falling sales.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe situation remains fragile,\u8221\'20\'1D BGA President DirkJandura warned, adding:\par \par \uc2 Jandura cited rising barriers to commerce, geopolitical tensions and a slowing world economy as key risks to global trade.\par \par \uc2 Germany\u8217\'20\'19s exporters have been hurt by the disruption caused by Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s trade wars, which has resulted in most EU goods entering the US being subject to a 15% baseline tariff.\par \par \uc2 10.27am BST\par \par \uc2 London commuters have increasingly been cycling to work because of this week\u8217\'20\'19s Tube strike, new figures show.\par \par \uc2 New figures from employee benefits provider YuLife showed that cycling miles tracked across London have jumped by 32% this week, from 3,878 to 5,120 miles, as workers turn to their bikes to beat the chaos, PA Media reports.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.39am BST\par \par \uc2 10.15am BST\par \par \uc2 Ryanair\uc2  boss warns Russia-Ukraine war will be issue for airlines for years\par \par \uc2 Michael O\u8217\'20\'19Leary\uc2 , the chief executive of budget airline \uc2 Ryanair\uc2 , has warned that the Russia-Ukraine war will be an ongoing issue for all European airlines for years to come.\par \par \uc2 Speaking after Poland shot down suspected Russian drones in its airspace, O\u8217\'20\'19Leary told \uc2 Ryanair\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s annual general meeting:\par \par \uc2 O\u8217\'20\'19Leary added that \uc2 Ryanair\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s board discussed safety issues at a meeting yesterday.\par \par \uc2 10.04am BST\par \par \uc2 BAE Systems\uc2  leads FTSE 100 risers\par \par \uc2 City traders who have braved the underground strike are pushing share prices higher in London this morning.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE100 index has risen by 34 points, or 0.37%, to 9259 points.\par \par \uc2 Defence company BAESystems is the top riser, up 2.9% at \u163\'00\'A318.85. Other European weapons makers\u8217\'20\'19 shares are also rising today, with tensions rising after 19 Russian drones entered Poland\u8217\'20\'19s airspace on Tuesday evening.\par \par \uc2 9.50am BST\par \par \uc2 London\u8217\'20\'19s Metropolitan Line is now running a minor service, despite today\u8217\'20\'19s strike.\par \par \uc2 Trains are operating with minor delays between Harrow-On-The-Hill and Baker Street \u8211\'20\'13 which will help passengers looking to travel to/from the west of London.\par \par \uc2 9.28am BST\par \par \uc2 British consumer confidence is currently subdued ahead of the government\u8217\'20\'19s budget on 26 November which could bring further tax increases, the boss of retailer the \uc2 John Lewis Partnership\uc2  has warned.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to reporters this morning, chairman JasonTarry said:\par \par \uc2 9.22am BST\par \par \uc2 Back on the London underground, there\u8217\'20\'19s some action on the District Line.\par \par \uc2 District Line services are now operating with minor delays between Upminster (the eastern end of the line) and Whitechapel (in the East End).\par \par \uc2 9.07am BST\par \par \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  results: What the analysts say\par \par \uc2 Retail analyst NickBubb has predicted that \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  will grow its full-year pre-tax profits (before exceptional items) to \u163\'00\'A3200m this year, from \u163\'00\'A3126m a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 He says today:\par \par \uc2 Victoria Scholar, head of investment at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ii.co.uk/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Interactive investor,}}}\uc2 suggests \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  may have benefitted from the cyber attack on \uc2 Marks & Spencer\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 Robyn Duffy, consumer markets senior analyst at RSM UK, says \uc2 John Lewis Partnership\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s turnaround strategy is maintaining momentum, adding:\par \par \uc2 8.39am BST\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Docklands Light Railway\uc2  is also suspended today, due to the strike action.\par \par \uc2 That could hit attendance at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/sep/08/protests-expected-as-51-israeli-arms-makers-among-exhibitors-at-london-trade-fair"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the DSEI UK defence show}}}\uc2 , where weapons manufacturers are showing off their wares this week.\par \par \uc2 A full DLR service is expected to run for the rest of the week, though.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.45am BST\par \par \uc2 8.24am BST\par \par \uc2 Speaking of \uc2 Heathrow\uc2 \u8230\'20\'26. London\u8217\'20\'19s largest airpost has reported that it handled more than eight million passengers in August, a record high.\par \par \uc2 Heathrow\uc2  says it is the first major European airport to hit the 8m passenger mark.\par \par \uc2 It adds that this \u8220\'20\'1Cmilestone achievement\u8221\'20\'1D further cements its role as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe UK\u8217\'20\'19s gateway to growth\u8221\'20\'1D, before warning that it is now operating at \u8220\'20\'1Cfull capacity\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It adds:\par \par \uc2 Heathrow\uc2  says it welcomes the government\u8217\'20\'19s commitment to expanding the airport \u8211\'20\'13 last month, it submitted plans for a third runway.\par \par \uc2 8.13am BST\par \par \uc2 Heads-up, London commuters: the Piccadilly Line is stirring into life.\par \par \uc2 Having been listed as \u8216\'20\'18suspended\u8217\'20\'19 this morning, the PiccadillyLine has been upgraded to \u8220\'20\'1Cpart suspended, minor delays\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 This is because trains are now running with minor delays between ActonTown and SouthHarrow, two stations on the westside of the capital. There is still no service on the rest of the line due to strike action.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s only of limited use for travellers hoping to get all the way into London. It also won\u8217\'20\'19t get passengers out to Heathrow airport (where one leg of the Piccadilly Line terminates, when it\u8217\'20\'19s running).\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.33am BST\par \par \uc2 8.03am BST\par \par \uc2 Waitrose\uc2 , \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s upmarket grocer, racked up record sales in the last six months.\par \par \uc2 JLP\uc2  reports that \uc2 Waitrose\uc2  performed ahead of the market with sales surpassing \u163\'00\'A34bn in the first half of the year for the first time.\par \par \uc2 Sales rose by 6% to \u163\'00\'A34.124bn, up from \u163\'00\'A33.909bn in the 26 weeks to 27 July 2024, with sales volumes up 3% (implying that rising prices were also a factor).\par \par \uc2 7.56am BST\par \par \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  reports that its investments have helped build momentum over the first half, \u8220\'20\'1Cdelivering growth in sales, customer numbers, loyalty and satisfaction\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It cautions that it expects the macroeconomic environment to remain challenging, but insists it is \u8220\'20\'1Cwell-positioned\u8221\'20\'1D to deliver full year profit growth.\par \par \uc2 7.49am BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://metro.co.uk/2025/09/11/tube-strike-day-four-leads-war-zone-cyclists-long-queues-buses-24133773/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Metro newspaper predicts}}}\uc2  another day of \u8220\'20\'1Ccommuter hell\u8221\'20\'1D in London, with long queues for buses, higher prices on Uber and Bolt, and the risk of \u8220\'20\'1Cmiserable cycling journeys\u8221\'20\'1D with rain forecast.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://metro.co.uk/2025/09/11/tube-strike-day-four-leads-war-zone-cyclists-long-queues-buses-24133773/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 They point out }}}\uc2 that most of the Elizabeth line \u8211\'20\'13 which runs from as far as Reading in the west to Shenfield and AbbeyWood in the east \u8211\'20\'13 is running today, along with the buses and Overground trains.\par \par \uc2 However, Elizabeth line trains were not expected to stop at Bond Street, Tottenham Court Road, Farringdon, Liverpool Street and Whitechapel stations before 8am today.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.36am BST\par \par \uc2 7.43am BST\par \par \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s profits were also hit by \u163\'00\'A330m of \u8220\'20\'1Cinvestment in operating costs\u8221\'20\'1D spent on its technology, financial services, and its head office and distribution teams.\par \par \uc2 The partnership says this spending is needed to accelerate its growth, explaining:\par \par \uc2 7.41am BST\par \par \uc2 Tube lines suspended as final day of strike gets underway\par \par \uc2 Travellers in the UK capital are facing another day of disruption, as staff on the \uc2 London Underground\uc2  continue to hold a strike.\par \par \uc2 All 11 underground lines are currently suspended this morning, meaning commuters will be attempting to squeeze onto busus, cycling, or walking to the office.\par \par \uc2 There were already crowds building at the bus stops outside Kings Cross as I cycled into the office early this morning \u8211\'20\'13 good luck to all attempting to get to work today.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, some limited tube services did run, and \uc2 Transport for London (TfL)\uc2  had hoped to run more trains again today.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/rmt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 RMT}}}\uc2  general secretary Eddie Dempsey called on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk/london"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 London}}}\uc2  mayor, \uc2 Sadiq Khan\uc2 , to meet the union.\par \par \uc2 He told the TUC Congress:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.50am BST\par \par \uc2 7.39am BST\par \par \uc2 Introduction: Losses widen at \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  amid rising costs\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 Losses have widened at JohnLewis, as the high street bellwether is hit by rising costs as it tries to nail its turnaround plan.\par \par \uc2 The JohnLewisPartnership has reported an \u163\'00\'A388m pre-tax loss for the first half of its financial year this morning, up from \u163\'00\'A330m a year ago.\par \par \uc2 It points the finger at the higher National Insurance Contributions (NICs) brought in by chancellor RachelReeves in last year\u8217\'20\'19s budget, plus \u163\'00\'A329m of costs from the UK\u8217\'20\'19s new packaging levy.\par \par \uc2 On a brighter note, sales across the partnership \u8211\'20\'13 which includes \uc2 Waitrose\uc2  and JohnLewis department stores \u8211\'20\'13 rose by 4% in the 26 weeks to 26 July 2025, to \u163\'00\'A36.2bn. That may indicate that the turnaround strategy is paying off.\par \par \uc2 Chairman JasonTarry insists that the Partnership is on track to deliver profit growth for the full year, saying:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/mar/13/john-lewis-profits-triple-to-126m-but-hopes-for-staff-bonus-dashed-again"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Last year, the company tripled its profits to \u163\'00\'A3126m}}}\uc2 ; earnings are higher in the second half of the year due to Christmas and Black Friday.\par \par \uc2 Also coming up today.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2  is expected to leave eurozone interest rate on hold, at a governing council meeting overshadowed by the political crisis in France.\par \par \uc2 Investors will also be watching the latest US inflation report, which is expected to show the cost of living rose at a faster pace in August. Headline inflation is forecast to rise to 2.9%, up from 2.7% a month earlier.\par \par \uc2 Kathleen Brooks\uc2 , research director at XTB, sets the scene\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 * 9am BST: \uc2 IEA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s monthly oil market report\par \par \uc2 * 1.15pm BST: European central bank interest rate decision\par \par \uc2 * 1.30pm BST: US inflation report for August\par \par \uc2 * 1.45pm BST: \uc2 ECB\uc2  press conference\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.40am BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250911el9b001jo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
