\par\par \b \uc2 US drugmaker Merck scraps \u163\'00\'A31bn London research centre and cuts 125 science jobs\b0\par\par\uc2 Julia Kollewe \par \uc2 982 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2025\par 21:36 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 New blow to UK\u8217\'20\'19s key life science sector as industry body says country is losing ground on investment and research\par \par \uc2 The US drugmaker Merck has scrapped a \u163\'00\'A31bn London research centre and is laying off 125 scientists in the capital this year, in a big blow to the UK\u8217\'20\'19s important life science sector.\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s government has described life sciences as \u8220\'20\'1Cone of the crown jewels of the UK economy\u8221\'20\'1D and the previous Conservative government had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/plan-to-forge-a-better-britain-through-science-and-technology-unveiled"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vowed to turn the country into a \u8220\'20\'1Cglobal science and technology superpower\u8221\'20\'1D by 2030}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The planned new lab, called the UK Discovery Centre, at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nla.london/projects/belgrove-house"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Belgrove House site opposite St Pancras and King\u8217\'20\'19s Cross stations}}}\uc2 , was already under construction and scheduled to open in two years\u8217\'20\'19 time. It had been expected to employ about 800 people overall, including 180 scientists.\par \par \uc2 As part of its U-turn, Merck, known as \uc2 MSD\uc2  in Europe, will vacate laboratories at the nearby London Bioscience Innovation Centre, which hosts more than 60 life science companies, and the neighbouring \uc2 Francis Crick Institute\uc2  by the end of 2025, with the loss of about 125 scientific jobs.\par \par \uc2 The New Jersey-based company said it would move the research operations to other sites. It is unclear where in the world it would be, but pharmaceutical companies are under pressure from Donald Trump to invest more in the US. The company declined to provider further details.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 MSD\uc2  announced \u8220\'20\'1Cplans to discontinue discovery research operations in the UK and that it no longer plans to occupy the Belgrove House site at King\u8217\'20\'19s Cross\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis \u8230\'20\'26 reflects the challenges of the UK not making meaningful progress towards addressing the lack of investment in the life science industry and the overall undervaluation of innovative medicines and vaccines by successive UK governments,\u8221\'20\'1D it said.\par \par \uc2 MSD\uc2  had previously said the research centre would build on the company\u8217\'20\'19s 100-year heritage in the UK, close to London\u8217\'20\'19s Knowledge Quarter and its Moorgate headquarters. It will keep its HQ and a large animal health site in Milton Keynes.\par \par \uc2 Lengthy negotiations between the government and the industry over the cost of medicines for the UK broke down last month. Under the voluntary pricing and access scheme, companies agree to pay back a certain amount of revenue they make from newer, branded drugs. In 2023, the rebate rate rose to 23.5%, which compares with 5.7% in France and 7% in Germany.\par \par \uc2 Richard Torbett\uc2 , chief executive of the \uc2 Association of the British Pharmaceutical Industry\uc2  (\uc2 ABPI\uc2 ), said: \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a real blow to the UK\u8217\'20\'19s life sciences ambitions. No one wants to hear that successful and innovative companies like \uc2 MSD\uc2  are reducing their investment and footprint in the UK.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Referring to a recent survey from \uc2 Deloitte\uc2 , and the UK\u8217\'20\'19s trade deal with the US, a government spokesperson said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe UK has become the most attractive place to invest in the world, but we know there is more work to do. Through our life sciences sector plan, we\u8217\'20\'19re taking decisive action to further unlock innovation, drive investment and boost growth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The news came as a report showed that the UK had lost ground to rivals in the global race for pharmaceutical investment and research.\par \par \uc2 Investment in life sciences research and development (R&D) in the UK has underperformed against global trends since 2018, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cdn.roxhillmedia.com/production/email/attachment/1740001_1750000/717fb5e5fc95a4c704c4a29d2abaed9bc72fa1cd.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the study}}}\uc2  from the \uc2 ABPI\uc2  and the consulting firm \uc2 PwC.\par \par \uc2 It said a significant slowdown began in 2020, when the rate of growth in R&D investment fell to 1.9% annually, down from 6.3% in the three preceding years and lagging behind the global average of 6.6%. R&D investment by the pharmaceutical industry even fell in 2023, the final year of the study, by nearly \u163\'00\'A3100m.\par \par \uc2 At \u163\'00\'A3795m, investment by foreign life sciences companies in the UK was 58% lower in 2023 than in 2017, when it stood at \u163\'00\'A31.9bn. This meant that the country declined in global rankings from second place in 2017 and 2021 to seventh place in 2023.\par \par \uc2 The UK has also fallen behind on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/may/13/plunge-in-clinical-drug-trials-makes-britain-an-urgent-case-for-treatment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 clinical trials of new medicines}}}\uc2 . It ranked eighth for late-stage industry trials in 2023, down from fourth in 2017, although there were \u8220\'20\'1Csmall signs of improvement\u8221\'20\'1D in 2023, according to the report.\par \par \uc2 Rippon Ubhi, who runs Sanofi\u8217\'20\'19s UK and Ireland business and the French company\u8217\'20\'19s speciality care division, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe findings in this report paint a concerning picture for UK patients and our economy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe UK is increasingly being viewed as \u8216\'20\'18uninvestable\u8217\'20\'19 in global boardrooms due to unprecedented [\uc2 NHS\uc2 ] {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/aug/22/wes-streeting-pharma-firms-nhs-drug-pricing-rebate-scheme"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 clawback rates}}}\uc2  and restrictive patient access to medicines. While other countries are actively investing in innovative medicines for patients, the UK is falling behind.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The UK head of the Swiss pharmaceutical firm \uc2 Novartis\uc2 , Johan Kahlstr\u246\'00\'F6m, has also said high costs means {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pharmacy.biz/novartis-nhs-drug-access-warning/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the UK is \u8220\'20\'1Clargely uninvestable,\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 , while the health secretary, \uc2 Wes Streeting\uc2 , has pledged that he will not allow firms to \u8220\'20\'1Crip off\u8221\'20\'1D taxpayers.\par \par \uc2 The government has set a target to position the UK as the leading life sciences economy in Europe by 2030 and third globally by 2035, while the chancellor, \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2 , has said the UK is at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jan/28/reeves-plans-to-create-silicon-valley-between-oxford-and-cambridge"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 forefront}}}\uc2  of developments in the sector.\par \par \uc2 Torbett said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe UK has a world-class science base and the potential to lead globally in developing the next generation of medicines and vaccines. But without a more competitive environment for investment, we risk losing out to other countries making bold moves to attract internationally mobile investment.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Only 37% of new medicines are made fully available for their licensed indications in the UK, compared with 90% in Germany. \uc2 ABPI\uc2  data shows that more than 60 medicines did not launch in the UK or were delayed between 2019-20 and 2022-23.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250910el9a005eh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
