\par\par \b \uc2 US added just 22,000 jobs in August, continuing slowdown amid Trump tariffs\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani \par \uc2 904 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2025\par 20:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Latest report also contained more bad news \u8211\'20\'13 the US lost 13,000 jobs in June, according to latest survey\par \par \uc2 The US jobs market stalled over the summer, adding just 22,000 jobs in August and continuing a slowdown in the labor market as businesses adjusted to disruptions caused by tariffs.\par \par \uc2 The latest jobs report also contained more bad news. The US lost 13,000 jobs in June, according to the latest survey, the first time it went into the negative since December 2020.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate for August inched up to 4.3%, the highest it has been since 2021.\par \par \uc2 The healthcare sector added 31,000 last month but most other sectors were flat or lost jobs.\par \par \uc2 The report highlighted worrying trends:\par \par \uc2 * Federal employment dropped 15,000 jobs in August, totaling 97,000 jobs lost since January.\par \par \uc2 * Manufacturing jobs went down by 12,000 in August and have tumbled 78,000 for the year.\par \par \uc2 * The racial unemployment gap widened in August. Black Americans are seeing an unemployment rate of 7.5%, compared to 6.1% last August. The unemployment rate for White Americans is 3.7%\par \par \uc2 The closely watched data comes from a monthly survey of employers conducted by the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  (\uc2 BLS\uc2 ), which has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/sep/04/trump-bureau-labor-statistics-chief"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 under attack}}}\uc2  from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  after it revised its findings last month, showing that hiring in early summer was much weaker than initially reported.\par \par \uc2 Last month the \uc2 BLS\uc2  slashed the number of new jobs created in May and June by by more than 250,000. The figures \u8211\'20\'13 revised when the bureau received more reports from businesses and government agencies \u8211\'20\'13 showed hiring over the summer was far weaker than first reported. The revised figures for May and June were 19,000 and 14,000, respectively \u8211\'20\'13 the lowest since the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Those figures were revised again this month. The \uc2 BLS\uc2  revised June\u8217\'20\'19s tally down by 27,000, from +14,000 to -13,000, and the change for July was revised up by 6,000, from +73,000 to +79,000. Employment in June and July combined was 21,000 lower than previously reported.\par \par \uc2 Though Trump claimed the revisions were \u8220\'20\'1Crigged in order to make the Republicans, and me, look bad\u8221\'20\'1D, August\u8217\'20\'19s figures show that the slumped pattern has continued even after Trump fired the bureau\u8217\'20\'19s commissioner in retaliation. Trump has nominated a conservative ally who helped write Project 2025 as the bureau\u8217\'20\'19s commissioner, leaving many economists {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/sep/04/trump-bureau-labor-statistics-chief"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 worried}}}\uc2  about the future of the bureau.\par \par \uc2 And data from other sources besides the bureau has also highlighted stagnation in the labor market. The payroll firm {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://adpemploymentreport.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ADP}}}\uc2  reported on Thursday that private employers added 54,000 jobs in August, nearly 20,000 below expectations. The outplacement firm \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2  also reported that job cuts reached 85,979 in August \u8211\'20\'13 up 39% from July and up 13% compared with August 2024.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe year started with strong job growth, but that momentum has been whipsawed by uncertainty. A variety of things could explain the hiring slowdown, including labor shortages, skittish consumers and AI disruption,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Nela Richardson\uc2 , the chief economist at ADP.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has been monitoring the labor market for signs that it may need to adjust interest rates. At his speech at the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s Jackson Hole, Wyoming, symposium last month, the Fed chair, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/aug/22/jerome-powell-speech-jackson-hole"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 seemed to hint}}}\uc2  that officials were leaning toward a rate cut at their next meeting on 17 September.\par \par \uc2 Though Wall Street investors have been waiting for the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s next rate cut, which would be its first since December, the next cut will probably come with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/aug/22/federal-reserve-trump-rate-cuts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 caveats}}}\uc2 . Powell emphasized that it was still unclear what impact Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariffs and immigration policies will have on the economy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is significant uncertainty about where all of these policies will eventually settle and what their lasting effects on the economy will be,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Powell warned that while there are new jobs being added each month, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe downside risks to employment are rising. And if those materialize, they can do so quickly in the form of sharply higher layoffs and rising unemployment.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Powell, who was first appointed by Trump in 2017 and again by Biden in 2022, reaches the end of his second term in May 2026. Trump earlier this year walked back on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jul/07/trump-federal-reserve-jerome-powell"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 threats}}}\uc2  to fire Powell after stock markets responded poorly to his comments and the supreme court implied that the Fed chair has special protection from termination.\par \par \uc2 But the White House has continued pressuring the Fed, including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/aug/25/trump-fires-federal-reserve-governor-lisa-cook"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 firing}}}\uc2  Lisa Cook, a Fed governor, for alleged {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/aug/24/trump-lawfare-mortgage-fraud-lisa-cook"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mortgage fraud}}}\uc2 , and implying that the weak job numbers are due to the central bank\u8217\'20\'19s inaction. On Friday, Trump and his cabinet zeroed in on Powell again and attacked the Fed for being \u8220\'20\'1Ctoo late\u8221\'20\'1D to lower interest rates.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJerome \u8216\'20\'18Too Late\u8217\'20\'19 Powell should have lowered rates long ago. As usual, he\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1CToo Late\u8217\'20\'19!\u8221\'20\'1D Trump wrote on Truth Social Friday.\par \par \uc2 Lori Chavez-DeRemer, labor secretary, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.foxbusiness.com/media/trump-cabinet-members-target-jerome-powell-weak-economy-august-jobs-numbers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  Fox Business that Powell \u8220\'20\'1Cshould be embarrassed by this report because he has not done his job\u8221\'20\'1D. Meanwhile, Scott Bessent, the treasury secretary, published an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/opinion/the-feds-gain-of-function-monetary-policy-ac0dc38a?gaa_at=eafs&gaa_n=ASWzDAiV_QTy2RcppL0rXEd0J4iayVTOsbxwrHQCctqpq4nv9z__RTnUQ0exzV7JDm0%3D&gaa_ts=68bb31f8&gaa_sig=9tXH_NW94424zQyuxo9Mimjfp0d-LasKdm6XuRgbYtV31ZBLByhcHIUew2n0YYdlbGtl4af5A9me5OhqUML5nA%3D%3D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 op-ed}}}\uc2  in the Wall Street Journal on Friday morning, arguing that the Fed over the years has \u8220\'20\'1Cblurred the lines between monetary and fiscal policy\u8221\'20\'1D and accused the Fed of \u8220\'20\'1Cregulatory overreach\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250905el95003pd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
