\par\par \b \uc2 US hiring slows as jobs market loses momentum; UK construction in longest slump since 2020 \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 3,436 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2025\par 14:36 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Construction activity in longest continuous slump in five years, as UK long-term borrowing costs dips backWhy Trump\u8217\'20\'19s firing of the US jobs chief has economists worried\par \par \uc2 3.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time for a recap.\par \par \uc2 Evidence is mounting that America\u8217\'20\'19s jobs market slowed over the summer, ahead of tomorrow\u8217\'20\'19s eagerly awaited non-farm payroll report.\par \par \uc2 Today we have learned:\par \par \uc2 * Private job creations slowed in August, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/sep/04/global-bond-sell-off-eases-us-jobs-report-japan-debt-auction-car-sales-uk-construction-business-live-news-updates?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-68b9834d8f087bf4418b5743#block-68b9834d8f087bf4418b5743"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 payrolls rising by just 54,000 last month}}}\uc2 , according to data provider ADP\par \par \uc2 * Layoffs jumped, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/sep/04/global-bond-sell-off-eases-us-jobs-report-japan-debt-auction-car-sales-uk-construction-business-live-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-68b978bb8f087bf4418b56a8#block-68b978bb8f087bf4418b56a8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US-based employers announcing 85,979 job cuts last month,}}}\par \par \uc2 * More Americans sought unemployment benefit in the final week of August, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/sep/04/global-bond-sell-off-eases-us-jobs-report-japan-debt-auction-car-sales-uk-construction-business-live-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-68b986928f08bb514e0fc0b6#block-68b986928f08bb514e0fc0b6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the initial claims total rising by 8,000, to 237,000}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On the ADP payrolls slowdown, KathyJones, chief fixed income strategist at Schwab, says:\par \par \uc2 The latest economic signals from the UK are worrying too:\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/sep/04/global-bond-sell-off-eases-us-jobs-report-japan-debt-auction-car-sales-uk-construction-business-live-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-68b94ea98f08020a75f133b0#block-68b94ea98f08020a75f133b0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s construction sector is suffering its longest continuous downturn since the early days of the Covid-19 pandemic.}}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/sep/04/global-bond-sell-off-eases-us-jobs-report-japan-debt-auction-car-sales-uk-construction-business-live-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-68b94a498f087bf4418b5458#block-68b94a498f087bf4418b5458"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Car sales dipped by 2% last month}}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/sep/04/global-bond-sell-off-eases-us-jobs-report-japan-debt-auction-car-sales-uk-construction-business-live-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-68b958838f087bf4418b54cf#block-68b958838f087bf4418b54cf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Two thirds of UK companies cut their proft margins in response to this year\u8217\'20\'19s rise in employment taxes, with nearly half cutting jobs}}}\par \par \uc2 Calm has returned to the bond markets today, after the sell-off earlier this weeks abated. The yield, or interest rate, on UK 30-year bonds has dipped back to 5.56%, having hit 5.75% yesterday \u8211\'20\'13 the highest since 1998.\par \par \uc2 3.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 Electric van demand doubles as UK market shrinks again\par \par \uc2 Back in the UK, registrations of electric vans have doubled even as the overall market shrinks.\par \par \uc2 The SMMT has reported that UK demand for new light commercial vehicles shrank by -13.3% in August with 14,365 new vans, pickups and 4x4s joining UK road. Volumes have fallen every month this year, a worrying sign for the economy.\par \par \uc2 According to the SMMT, new pickup registrations saw the largest percentage drop, down -25.8% to 1,040 units, impacted by recent fiscal changes to reclassify double-cab pickups as cars for benefit-in-kind and capital allowance purposes.\par \par \uc2 Sales of battery electric van (BEV) models rose by 109.5% year-on-year, to 1,902 units.\par \par \uc2 3.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 2.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 Wall Street has opened slightly higher, as traders digest today\u8217\'20\'19s weaker-than-expected private payrolls report.\par \par \uc2 The DowJonesIndustrialAverage has risen by 33 points, or 0.07%, to 45,304 in early trading.\par \par \uc2 The broader S&P 500 share index is up 0.2%.\par \par \uc2 1.52pm BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/sep/04/global-bond-sell-off-eases-us-jobs-report-japan-debt-auction-car-sales-uk-construction-business-live-news-updates?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-68b9834d8f087bf4418b5743#block-68b9834d8f087bf4418b5743"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The lower-than-expected ADP reading of 54k}}}\uc2  signals weakening private sector job growth, amplifying concerns about an economic slowdown, says AaronHill, chief analyst from FP Markets:\par \par \uc2 1.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 US jobless claims total rises\par \par \uc2 Further evidence that the US jobs market is weakening has just landed.\par \par \uc2 The number of Americans filing new claims for unemployment support has risen to 237,000, in the last week of August. That\u8217\'20\'19s an increase of 8,000 from the previous week.\par \par \uc2 Economists had expected a smaller rise, to 230,000. Initial claims are seen as a proxy for job losses, and have been at historically low levels in the last few years.\par \par \uc2 The DepartmentofLabor also reports that the largest increases in initial claims for the week ending August 23 were in NewYork (+1,430), Texas (+1,230), Illinois (+509), Missouri (+206), and Hawaii (+119). The largest decreases were in Iowa (-1,235), Virginia (-857), Maryland (-562), Pennsylvania (-482), and Connecticut (-455).\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 1.27pm BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/sep/04/global-bond-sell-off-eases-us-jobs-report-japan-debt-auction-car-sales-uk-construction-business-live-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-68b9834d8f087bf4418b5743#block-68b9834d8f087bf4418b5743"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s (poor) ADP Payroll report}}}\uc2  is closely watched, ahead of the official US jobs report tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Investors are poised for the August non-farm payroll report due in 24 hours time, as last month\u8217\'20\'19s showed a shock drop in employment \u8211\'20\'13 and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/aug/01/trump-fires-erika-mcentarfer-labor-statistics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prompted Donald Trump to fire the head of the labor statistics board}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 1.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 US hiring slowdown confirmed\par \par \uc2 Just in: Hiring at US companies slowed sharply last month \u8211\'20\'13 another sign that America\u8217\'20\'19s jobs market is cooling.\par \par \uc2 Company payroll operator ADP reports that private sector employment increased by 54,000 jobs in August, barely half the 106,000 jobs created in July.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe year started with strong job growth, but that momentum has been whipsawed by uncertainty,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr \uc2 Nela Richardson\uc2 , chief economist, ADP, adding:\par \par \uc2 ADP reports that manufacturing employment fell by 7,000 jobs last month, while construction added 16,000 and natural resources/mining payrolls rose by 4,000.\par \par \uc2 Payrolls across services companies rose by 42,000; here\u8217\'20\'19s a breakdown:\par \par \uc2 * Trade/transportation/utilities -17,000\par \par \uc2 * Information 7,000\par \par \uc2 * Financial activities -2,000\par \par \uc2 * Professional/business services 15,000\par \par \uc2 * Education/health services -12,000\par \par \uc2 * Leisure/hospitality 50,000\par \par \uc2 * Other services 1,000\par \par \uc2 12.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 US company job cuts jump in August\par \par \uc2 Job cuts at US companies has hit their highest August level since 2020, according to new data from outplacement and coaching firm \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 US-based employers announced 85,979 job cuts last month, which is 39% more than in July and 13% more than in August 2024.\par \par \uc2 This is another indication that the US labour market cooled over the summer\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 August\u8217\'20\'19s total was the highest for the month since 2020 when 115,762 job cuts were recorded. After 2020, it is the highest August total since the Great Recession in 2008, when 88,736 cuts were announced.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Challenger\uc2 , senior vice president and labor expert for \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2 , explains:\par \par \uc2 The report also shows that companies have announced 892,362 job cuts so far this year, the highest YTD since 2020 when 1,963,458 were announced. It is up 66% from the 536,421 job cuts announced through the first eight months of last year and is up 17% from the 2024 full year total of 761,358.\par \par \uc2 12.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  is seeking advice on how to enhance the resilience the UK gilt repo market, and avoid throwing more fuel on a market crisis.\par \par \uc2 The gilt repo market allows banks to borrow money trhough a short-term borrowing arrangement (repurchase agreements, or \u8216\'20\'18repo\u8217\'20\'19), using government debt (\u8216\'20\'18gilts\u8217\'20\'19) as collateral.\par \par \uc2 The dangers of sudden moves in the gilt market were exposed after \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s mini-budget of 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 bond prices fell, leading to forced sales for liability-driven investment pensions funds (\uc2 LDI\uc2  funds), creating a doom loop.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BoE\uc2  is considering various measures that could prevent a meltdown, including potentially creating a central clearing counterparty (\uc2 CCP\uc2 ) to handle repo deals between banks.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/paper/2025/discussion-paper/enhancing-the-resilience-of-the-gilt-repo-market"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 You can read more here.}}}\par \par \uc2 The Bank explains:\par \par \uc2 12.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 Amazon UK\uc2  sales hit \u163\'00\'A329bn\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/amazon"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amazon}}}\uc2  has revealed that UK sales rose to \u163\'00\'A329bn last year, making it slightly larger than \uc2 Asda\uc2 , the UK\u8217\'20\'19s third largest supermarket.\par \par \uc2 However, the pace of growth at the group, which operates cloud services and other digital systems for businesses alongside its retail arm, slowed to 7.4% in 2024 from 12.5% in the prior year.\par \par \uc2 The company now has 75,000 UK employees, about half that of \uc2 Asda\uc2 , and says it paid \u163\'00\'A31bn of UK taxes, up from \u163\'00\'A3932m last year. However that includes business rates, corporation tax and national insurance contributions \u8211\'20\'13 and the company does not break down the figure.\par \par \uc2 The government has said it wants to revise the business rates system from next year so that online retail businesses such as \uc2 Amazon\uc2  pay more and the burden is eased on high street businesses. However, under its current plans, most large expensive buildings, including hospitals and some department stores and supermarkets will also pay more business rates \u8211\'20\'13 alongside the warehouses and offices used by online sellers.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\u8217\'20\'19s UK Services arm is expected to release its full accounts next week. In 2023, the division paid \u163\'00\'A318.7m in \u8220\'20\'1Ccurrent tax\u8221\'20\'1D which is understood to have been largely corporation tax. It was the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/sep/26/amazon-uk-pays-corporation-tax-for-first-time-since-2020"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first time since 2020}}}\uc2  the division had paid corporation tax.\par \par \uc2 The company received a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/jun/01/amazon-uk-services-main-division-pay-no-corporation-tax-for-second-year-in-row-tax-credit-government-super-deduction-scheme"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u163\'00\'A37.8m tax credit in 2022}}}\uc2  and a \u163\'00\'A31.1m credit in 2021 after its investments in infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 In 2024, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said it had invested \u163\'00\'A31.6bn in infrastructure in the UK, including new warehouses, but some of the tax breaks on that it enjoyed in prior years have now been unwound.\par \par \uc2 11.59am BST\par \par \uc2 Goldman: Gold could approach $5,000/oz if Fed independence damaged\par \par \uc2 The gold price has dipped a little today, away from the record highs set during this week\u8217\'20\'19s bond market turmoil.\par \par \uc2 Gold is trading at $3,540 per ounce this morning, down 0.5%, having yesterday hit a peak of $3,578.50 per ounce.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sach\u8217\'20\'19s baseline forecast is that gold hits $4,000 per ounce by mid-2016.\par \par \uc2 But in a note today, they predict it could rise much higher if the independence of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is damaged, leading to higher inflation, lower stock and long-dated bond prices, and an erosion of the dollar\u8217\'20\'19s reserve currency status, pushing more investors into gold.\par \par \uc2 Goldman\u8217\'20\'19s analysts write:\par \par \uc2 11.34am BST\par \par \uc2 German institutes cut growth forecasts\par \par \uc2 We have disappointing economic news from Germany this morning too.\par \par \uc2 A group of top German economic institutes have trimmed their growth forecasts for 2025 and 2026, citing headwinds from the US trade war.\par \par \uc2 The Ifoinstitute now expects gross domestic product to expand by just 0.2% this year, a cut of 0.1 percentage point. Growth is expected to rise to 1.3% in 2026, a drop of 0.2 percentage points from its previous forecast.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Kiel Institute for the World Economy\uc2  made similar reductions, cutting its 2025 growth forecast to 0.1% from 0.3% in June. It now sees GDP rising 1.3% in 2026, down from 1.6% previously.\par \par \uc2 10.53am BST\par \par \uc2 Over in the eurozone, retail sales have dropped again.\par \par \uc2 Retail sales growth in the euro area fell by 0.5% month-on-month in July, larger than the 0.2% fall expected.\par \par \uc2 10.18am BST\par \par \uc2 Firms cut profit margins and jobs after national insurance rate rise\par \par \uc2 Two thirds of UK companies cut their proft margins in response to this year\u8217\'20\'19s rise in employment taxes, new \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  data shows.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BoE\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s poll of \u8216\'20\'18decision makers\u8217\'20\'19 at UK firms has asked how they adjusted to the rise in employers\u8217\'20\'19 national insurance rates, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2025/apr/02/reeves-defends-40bn-tax-increase-in-autumn-budget-as-businesses-prepare-for-nics-hike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 kicked in at the start of April}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Firms were allowed to select more than one option. 66% of firms reported lowering profit margins, 34% raising prices, 46% lowering employment and 20% paying lower wages than they otherwise would have done, the Bank says.\par \par \uc2 10.06am BST\par \par \uc2 Construction slump: what the experts say\par \par \uc2 Overall, the S&P Global UK Construction Purchasing Managers\u8217\'20\'19 Index rose to 45.5 in August, up from 44.3 in July (which was the lowest reading for just over five years).\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s still below the 50-point mark separating expansion from contraction.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s some expert reaction:\par \par \uc2 Max Jones, director of infrastructure andconstruction at \uc2 Lloyds\uc2 , says:\par \par \uc2 Huda As\u8217\'20\'19ad, \uc2 Accenture\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s infrastructure and capital projects lead in the UK and Ireland, says:\par \par \uc2 And here\u8217\'20\'19s Brian Smith, head of cost management at engineering company \uc2 AECOM\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 Updated at 3.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 9.52am BST\par \par \uc2 Employment across the construction sector has also fallen, today\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 PMI\uc2  report shows, as building firms implement hiring freezes and decide not to replace departing staff,\par \par \uc2 S&P Global explains:\par \par \uc2 9.47am BST\par \par \uc2 UK construction firms have been hit by a lack of new projects, S&P Global reports.\par \par \uc2 That led to a solid reduction in purchasing activity across the construction sector.\par \par \uc2 9.41am BST\par \par \uc2 UK construction in longest downturn since early 2020\par \par \uc2 Ouch! Britain\u8217\'20\'19s construction sector is suffering its longest continuous downturn since the early days of the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Construction output slumped again in August, according to the latest poll of purchasing managers from data provider S&P Global.\par \par \uc2 Its construction \uc2 PMI\uc2  has found:\par \par \uc2 * Business activity falls for eighth month in a row, but at slower pace than in July\par \par \uc2 * Solid reductions in new work and employment\par \par \uc2 * Optimism drops to its lowest since December 2022\par \par \uc2 S&P Global says there was a \u8220\'20\'1Csustained downturn in UK construction output\u8221\'20\'1D in August, led by a \u8220\'20\'1Cmarked reductions in the housing and civil engineering segments\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Civil engineering was the weakest-performing segment in August, with business activity decreasing at the fastest pace since October 2020. Housebuilding activity also fell, suggesting the government is strugging to hit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2025/apr/18/britain-houses-labour-plan-more-migrants-skills-gap"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 its target to boost home construction}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tim Moore\uc2 , economics director at \uc2 S&P Global Market Intelligence\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 9.22am BST\par \par \uc2 UK car sales dip, but \uc2 Tesla\uc2  slump ends\par \par \uc2 Just in: UK car sales fell last month, but the slump in demand for \uc2 Tesla\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s vehicles reversed.\par \par \uc2 The Society of Motor Manufacturers and Traders\uc2  has reported that new car registrations fell by 2% in August, to 82,908 units. August is typically the quietest month of the year, due to summer holidays and drivers waiting for new number plates in September.\par \par \uc2 Sales of battery-powered cars rose by almost 15%, which the SMMT attributes to \u8220\'20\'1Cmodel choice, ongoing manufacturer discounts and some limited initial impact from the newly announced Electric Car Grant\u8221\'20\'1D. This lifted BEV\u8217\'20\'19s market share to 26.5%, the highest recorded in 2025.\par \par \uc2 After several months of sliding sales, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  enjoyed a 7% rise in registrations in August. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s carmaker sold 3,243 models last month, up from 3,013 a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a significant improvement on July, when {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/aug/05/chinese-carmakers-buck-market-trend-report-soaring-uk-sales"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tesla sales slumped by 59%}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This may be due to \uc2 Tesla\uc2  launching an updated version of its Model Y car earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Chinese rival BYD quadruled its UK sales in August, to 1,759 from 438 a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Mike Hawes\uc2 , SMMT chief executive, says:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 9.23am BST\par \par \uc2 8.55am BST\par \par \uc2 Oil price drops\par \par \uc2 The price of oil is dropping this morning, on predictions that the Opec+ group could increase production again.\par \par \uc2 Opec+ are due to meet on Sunday when they will consider whether to agree another increase in output targets.\par \par \uc2 OPEC+ had already agreed to raise output targets by about 2.2 million barrels per day from April to September, plus a 300,000 bpd quota increase for the United Arab Emirates.\par \par \uc2 The possibility of a further increase to supply has pushed Brent crude down by around 1% this morning, to below $67 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 8.39am BST\par \par \uc2 UK 30-year yields are dipping\par \par \uc2 UK long-term borrowing costs have fallen a little this morning, as the bond market panic eases.\par \par \uc2 The yield, or interest rate, on 30-year gilts has dipped by two basis points (0.02 percentage points) to 5.58% this morning.\par \par \uc2 Last night they closed at 5.6%, having hit a 27-year high of 5.75% on Wednesday morning.\par \par \uc2 8.21am BST\par \par \uc2 Airline shares fall after \uc2 Jet2\uc2  predicts slower earnings growth\par \par \uc2 Leisure travel group\par \par \uc2 Jet2\uc2  has spooked the travel industry this morning, by predicting weaker earning growth than expected.\par \par \uc2 Jet2\uc2  told investors that it expects its earnings, on an EBIT basis, to be towards the lower end of the analysts\u8217\'20\'19 consensus range.\par \par \uc2 It also cautioned that it has \u8220\'20\'1Climited visibility\u8221\'20\'1D about prospects for this year, as holidaymakers are making bookings later, meaning it still has much of its winter seat capacity still to sell.\par \par \uc2 Steve Heapy\uc2 , \uc2 Jet2\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief executive officer, says:\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 Jet2\uc2  tumbled 23% at the start of trading, and are now dow 14%.\par \par \uc2 Other airlines are suffering too \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 easyJet\uc2  have fallen by 4.2%, while \uc2 British Airways\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 parent company \uc2 IAG\uc2  has lost 2.3%.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.58am BST\par \par \uc2 8.11am BST\par \par \uc2 European stock markets have opened calmly too.\par \par \uc2 In London, the FTSE100 share index is down by just 3 points (-0.04%) at 9174, following some choppy sessions which saw losses on Tuesday and a partial recovery on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Germany\u8217\'20\'19s DAX dipped by 0.27% at the open in Frankfurt.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.11am BST\par \par \uc2 8.01am BST\par \par \uc2 Panmure Liberum: UK must show 'firm hand' to keep bond yields under control\par \par \uc2 Signs of calm in the bond market will be warmly welcomed in the UK Treasury.\par \par \uc2 The jump in Britain\u8217\'20\'19s bond yields risks widening the \u8216\'20\'18black hole\u8217\'20\'19 which \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  is expected to fill in the autumn budget scheduled for 26 November.\par \par \uc2 That could mean either spending cuts or tax rises to persuade the \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility\uc2  that the chancellor is keeping within the fiscal rules.\par \par \uc2 Simon\par \par \uc2 French, chief economist at Panmure Liberum, arugues that UK fiscal and monetary policymakers must demonstrate \u8220\'20\'1Ca firm hand on the tiller\u8221\'20\'1D in the next few weeks, to help sooth UK gilt yields.\par \par \uc2 He writes:\par \par \uc2 French also flags that since the government\u8217\'20\'19s Spring Statement earlier this year, the UK\u8217\'20\'19s ten-year yield has been the highest in the G7, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe longest such outlier run in modern market history\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 That suggests that domestic factors, as well as global concerns, have ben pushing up UK bond yields.\par \par \uc2 7.45am BST\par \par \uc2 Analyst: "The bond market rout could be over"\par \par \uc2 There is a sense of calm in European and US markets today, reports \uc2 Kathleen Brooks\uc2 , research director at \uc2 XTB\uc2 , as the recovery in global bond yields on Wednesday helps sentiment.\par \par \uc2 Brooks writes:\par \par \uc2 That auction is scheduled for 10am UK time\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 7.43am BST\par \par \uc2 Trump asks \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  to uphold his tariffs\par \par \uc2 Uncertainty over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s trade wars are another factor hitting bond prices this week.\par \par \uc2 Last Friday\u8217\'20\'19s court ruling that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariffs are illegal has raised the prospect that the White House might have to refund billions of dollars paid by Americans on imported goods, as well as losing out on future revenues.\par \par \uc2 As the White House has been, ahem, trumpeting the rising income from tariffs, that would be a blow.\par \par \uc2 The prospect that America might need to borrow even more money to cover for a tariff shortfall could have weighed on bond prices this week, pushing up yields.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, Trump asked the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  to uphold his global tariffs on a fast-track schedule. This means the nine members of the Court will now have the finnal say on whether Trump can raise the US\u8217\'20\'19s effective tariff rate to its highest in around a century.\par \par \uc2 If the \uc2 US government\uc2  loses the case, some of the trade deals struck in recent months could also come unstuck.\par \par \uc2 7.32am BST\par \par \uc2 Introduction: Relief as Japan's debt auction proceeds smoothly\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 There are signs that calm may be returning to the global bond market, after Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s sell-off drove government borrowing costs to multi-year highs.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, an auction of 30-year Japanese government bonds proceeded smoothly, helping to support Japan\u8217\'20\'19s debt prices.\par \par \uc2 Encouragingly for Tokyo, demand for its long-term debt held up pretty well \u8211\'20\'13 the bid-to-cover ratio, which measures the amount of bids against the amount of debt on offer, was 3.31, only slightly below the 12-month average of 3.38.\par \par \uc2 This has helped to push down the yields, or interest rates, on Japan\u8217\'20\'19s debt in the bond market \u8211\'20\'13 a relief, after long-term yields hit record highs earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 Hirofumi Suzuki, a strategist at \uc2 SMBC\uc2 , explained:\par \par \uc2 Long-term bonds are under pressure due to concerns about the size of government debt, political obstacles to spending cuts, and structurally higher inflation.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, the yields on UK and US long-term debt both fell back, bringing some relief after Britain\u8217\'20\'19s 30-year borrowing costs hit the highest level since 1998.\par \par \uc2 Bond prices recovered after disappointing US job openings data was released, prompting investors to predict faster interest rate cuts. It showed that for the first time in more than four years, there are fewer open jobs in America than there are job seekers.\par \par \uc2 Jim Reid, market strategist at DeutscheBank, explains:\par \par \uc2 The number of job openings fell to an estimated 7.18m at the end of July, down from 7.36m the month before.\par \par \uc2 Investors will be watching the latest employment data from the US, due later today, for further signs that its labour market is cooling.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 * 9am BST: UK car sales data for August\par \par \uc2 * 9.30am BST: UK construction \uc2 PMI\uc2  for August\par \par \uc2 * 12.30pm BST: US Challenger job cuts report\par \par \uc2 * 1.30pm BST: US jobless claims\par \par \uc2 * 1.15pm BST: US ADP private payrolls data\par \par \uc2 * 3pm BST: US service sector \uc2 PMI\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250904el940018h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
