\par\par \b \uc2 Publications aimed at LGBTQ+ audiences face discrimination from advertisers, editors warn\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Savage Media editor \par \uc2 1,051 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 August 2025\par 06:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Media figures say \u8216\'20\'18gold rush is over\u8217\'20\'19, as attacks on diversity and inclusion have led to some advertisers avoiding outlets\par \par \uc2 Publications aimed at LGBTQ+ and other diverse audiences are facing \u8220\'20\'1Cgood old-fashioned discrimination\u8221\'20\'1D as advertisers avoid them after political attacks on diversity and inclusion campaigns, editors have said.\par \par \uc2 Senior figures at publications aimed at the gay community and other minority groups said a previous \u8220\'20\'1Cgold rush\u8221\'20\'1D to work with such titles was over.\par \par \uc2 There has been a backlash in the US over corporate diversity, equity, and inclusion (DEI) efforts in the past 18 months, which has led to some {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jan/06/mcdonalds-diversity-programs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 big names rolling back their plans}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tag Warner, the chief executive of Gay Times, said his publication, which had been growing digitally in the US, had lost 80% of its advertisers in the past year. It has also lost in excess of \u163\'00\'A35m in expected advertiser revenue.\par \par \uc2 Warner, who has led the outlet since 2019, said his title\u8217\'20\'19s growth had been accompanied by an enthusiasm from brands to embrace LGBTQ+ audiences. He blames an anti-DEI drive in the US for the dramatic shift.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI know that media and marketing is also going through a challenging year anyway, but when we\u8217\'20\'19re thinking about other organisations that don\u8217\'20\'19t talk to diverse themes, they\u8217\'20\'19re not nearly as impacted as we are,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is just good old-fashioned discrimination. Because discrimination doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have to make business sense. Discrimination doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have to be logical. Discrimination is discrimination.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re really experiencing the impact of what happens when voices that are pressuring organisations to give in to less inclusive perspectives start winning. Then it creates this massive behavioural shift in brands and organisations.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Nafisa Bakkar, the co-founder of Amaliah, a publication aimed at \u8220\'20\'1Camplifying the voices of Muslim women\u8221\'20\'1D, said there had been a \u8220\'20\'1Cchange in mood\u8221\'20\'1D among brands and advertisers. \u8220\'20\'1CThere was this DNI [diversity and inclusion] gold rush,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is, I would say, well and truly over.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe work with a lot of UK advertisers, but I would say that the US has a lot more emphasis on what they would call \u8216\'20\'18brand safety\u8217\'20\'19, which I think is a code word for \u8216\'20\'18we don\u8217\'20\'19t want to rock the boat\u8217\'20\'19. I would say there is a lot more focus on this element.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ibrahim Kamara, the founder of the youth platform GUAP, which has a large black and ethnically diverse audience, said he had detected a \u8220\'20\'1Crelative difference\u8221\'20\'1D from 2020 in approaches from brands.\par \par \uc2 He and others cited the economic pressures on advertisers generally in recent years. However, he said the \u8220\'20\'1Chype and the PR around wanting to support and connect with diverse audiences\u8221\'20\'1D had also subsided.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe thing that most people within these kind of spaces can probably agree on is that the energy and the PR is very different now,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was almost a badge of honour to be able to say that you\u8217\'20\'19re supporting certain communities. Now, I\u8217\'20\'19ve seen that lots of the diversity and inclusion people that were hired around that period have probably lost their jobs. It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have the same PR effect any more.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Warner said the anti-DEI impact pre-dated the return of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the White House. Figures such as the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/nov/26/walmart-dei-conservatives-race-gender"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conservative pundit Robby Starbuck}}}\uc2  have been engaged in a long-running anti-DEI campaign, pressuring firms to drop their diversity efforts. However, Warner said Trump\u8217\'20\'19s arrival \u8220\'20\'1Cgave everyone, I think, permission to be honest about it\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Not all publications in the sector have been hit in the same way as Gay Times. Companies with business models less reliant on US advertising, as well as some big players with long-established relationships, said they had managed to negotiate the changing political environment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBrands are nervous, that\u8217\'20\'19s for sure, or careful \u8211\'20\'13 or a combination of both,\u8221\'20\'1D said Darren Styles, the managing director of Stream Publishing, which publishes Attitude magazine. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re aware it can be a lightning rod for a vocal minority. But our experience is that most people are holding their ground, if not doubling down.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Styles also said he was not complacent, however, given the rise of \uc2 Nigel Farage\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Reform party in the UK and its lack of historical support for the LGBTQ+ community.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not incautious about the future,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWho knows what next year will bring, because that narrative is not going away. Obviously, there\u8217\'20\'19s the rise of Reform in the polls.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C[Farage] is quite clearly not an ally to our community and he\u8217\'20\'19s expressed disdain in the past at the awards we\u8217\'20\'19ve given out to people in the trans community. So it is a worry as political momentum gains around there. But I think broadly, consumers in the UK are a bit more capable of thinking for themselves.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mark Berryhill, the chief executive of equalpride, which publishes prominent US titles like Out and The Advocate, said some brands and agencies \u8220\'20\'1Cmay have been a little bit more cautious than they have been in the past\u8221\'20\'1D. However, he said it had so far meant deals had taken longer to be completed, in a tough economic climate.\par \par \uc2 He said the political headwinds made it more important to highlight that working with such titles was simply a sound business decision. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve tried to do a better job in this political climate of just selling the importance of our buying power,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CEverybody\u8217\'20\'19s cautious and I don\u8217\'20\'19t think it\u8217\'20\'19s just LGBTQ. I think they\u8217\'20\'19re cautious in general right now with their work with minority owned companies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe one thing that maybe this whole controversy has helped us with a little bit is to really make brands realise it\u8217\'20\'19s a business decision. It\u8217\'20\'19s not just a charity or something you should do because you feel guilty.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou should do it because it\u8217\'20\'19s the right thing to support LGBTQ journalism. We\u8217\'20\'19re small. We need to get the word out. We have important stories to tell. But it\u8217\'20\'19s also a good business decision. The more we show that side, certain brands will come along.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250831el8v000uo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
