\par\par \b \uc2 DC eateries suffer as workers terrified of Ice stay home: \u8216\'20\'18You don\u8217\'20\'19t want to go outside\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Kira Lerner in Washington \par \uc2 1,016 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 August 2025\par 16:47 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Some restaurants in the US capital have closed their doors as workers stay home rather than risk deportation\par \par \uc2 Hern\u225\'00\'E1n was at the Latin American restaurant that he owns with his brother in the Northwest quadrant of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/washington-dc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington DC}}}\uc2  last week when his staff started getting phone calls and messages about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ice-us-immigration-and-customs-enforcement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement (Ice)}}}\uc2  checkpoints in the neighborhood. The employees, scared that they might be targeted and racially profiled, asked if they could go home.\par \par \uc2 Within hours, Hern\u225\'00\'E1n had to close their doors, and the restaurant hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been open since.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLiterally after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 President Trump}}}\uc2  brought the national guard on DC, everything stopped,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hern\u225\'00\'E1n, who requested his last name not be used due to fears of Ice retaliation. \u8220\'20\'1CEverything disappeared because the bike delivery guys, they\u8217\'20\'19re scared. They\u8217\'20\'19re not on the streets right now. My people, most of my cooks are Spanish[-speaking] and they don\u8217\'20\'19t want to go to DC right now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hern\u225\'00\'E1n, who also owns a restaurant in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/maryland"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Maryland}}}\uc2 , said he was hoping to be able to reopen in a few weeks if and when the Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s takeover of the DC police ends and immigration enforcement arrests become less frequent. But the future is unclear for him and many restaurant owners in the US capital.\par \par \uc2 Already suffering from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/aug/18/trump-washington-dc-national-guard-restaurants"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fewer customers}}}\uc2  due to the administration\u8217\'20\'19s almost three-week-long crackdown, restaurants are also having to contend with staff shortages because many {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/usimmigration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 immigrants}}}\uc2 , both documented and undocumented, fear coming into DC and being on the streets, and some have been detained by Ice.\par \par \uc2 Trump has attempted to claim that the crackdown is benefiting restaurants. \u8220\'20\'1CHalf the restaurants closed, because nobody could go, because they were afraid to go outside,\u8221\'20\'1D he told reporters on Monday about Washington before his intervention. \u8220\'20\'1CNow those restaurants are opening and new restaurants are opening up.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Across the city, the opposite is true. Some restaurants, like Hern\u225\'00\'E1n\u8217\'20\'19s, have had to close their doors. But more have had to make do with fewer workers, fewer delivery drivers and fewer customers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRestaurants will close because you have troops with guns and federal agents harassing people \u8230\'20\'26 making people afraid to go out,\u8221\'20\'1D Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Andr\u233\'00\'E9s, the well-known Washington restaurateur, wrote on social media.\par \par \uc2 Immigrants account for 253,000 workers, or 36% of the workforce, in DC\u8217\'20\'19s restaurants, hotels and related sectors, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://migrantinsider.com/p/report-migrants-drive-washingtons"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to Migrant Insider}}}\uc2 , which cited data from FWD.us. Included in that total are 42,000 self-employed immigrant entrepreneurs running local food and service businesses.\par \par \uc2 Maketto, an Asian restaurant and marketplace in north-east DC, posted on social media on Monday that its immigrant workers had been affected.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLast week, two of our beloved team members were detained while simply walking home from work,\u8221\'20\'1D the restaurant wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CThey remain in custody, and our hearts are with them and their families.\u8221\'20\'1D The restaurant did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Hern\u225\'00\'E1n said the pause in business was incredibly hard on a small, family-owned business, especially after the slow summer months. He\u8217\'20\'19s looking for people who live in DC, speak Spanish and English, and are willing to come into work so that they can reopen. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I don\u8217\'20\'19t know if we can find people,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 About a mile away, near the Columbia Heights neighborhood, a sign in Spanish on the door of Elizabeth Pupuser\u237\'00\'EDa & Deli hung up last week read: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are closed temporarily during the terrible situation in our larger neighborhood. We hope to serve you again soon. Let\u8217\'20\'19s take care of ourselves and stay safe in our homes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Rodriguez, a Salvadorian immigrant who owns the restaurant and has been in Washington for roughly 20 years, told the Guardian that the restaurant was fulfilling some orders that prior customers call in, but its doors were not open to the public.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a very ugly thing because the nerves, the nerves go up,\u8221\'20\'1D Rodriguez said in Spanish about the situation in DC. \u8220\'20\'1CThe clients don\u8217\'20\'19t come for the same reason. Our business relied on construction workers because they are the big groups that came to bring various orders for all their colleagues. And they haven\u8217\'20\'19t worked all month.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She said she paid her workers collectively about $500 a day, and after Trump\u8217\'20\'19s crackdown on 11 August, she was only bringing in $300 a day. She hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been able to pay any of her staff these last two weeks \u8211\'20\'13 only she and her daughter-in-law are coming in.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are seeing what we can do so as not to close definitively,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI know that it is only supposed to be one month, but suddenly it is very complicated because the rent, the bills, you have to pay them the same.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are asking God for this to change because if we don\u8217\'20\'19t, we can\u8217\'20\'19t pay many months of rent,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 The restaurant doesn\u8217\'20\'19t typically allow delivery, but Rodriguez is thinking about changing that if it reopens because so many people don\u8217\'20\'19t want to leave their homes. But even that is difficult, she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday I said, \u8216\'20\'18Today is the right time to add delivery,\u8217\'20\'19 so I called \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  and he told me that he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have workers because most of them have been taken and others are afraid to leave.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ice has reportedly been targeting delivery drivers on mopeds, worsening the crisis for restaurants.\par \par \uc2 Several videos of Ice detaining delivery drivers have spread across social media, and the \uc2 Washington Post{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2025/08/21/dc-police-ice-moped-crackdown-delivery-drivers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2 , citing anonymous police sources, that Ice is accompanying officers with DC\u8217\'20\'19s Metropolitan police department to conduct traffic stops of moped drivers to check their immigration status.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou don\u8217\'20\'19t want to go outside,\u8221\'20\'1D Yonatan Colmenarez, an asylum seeker who came from Venezuela two years ago and has driven a moped around DC, told the Post. \u8220\'20\'1CIt gives you a kind of emotional damage; you\u8217\'20\'19re not sure who they might be.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250829el8t002ux\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
