\par\par \b \uc2 Protests at Glacier as national parks reel from Trump cuts: \u8216\'20\'18They\u8217\'20\'19ve gutted staff, gutted funding\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Cassidy Randall \par \uc2 1,529 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 August 2025\par 15:29 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As Republicans tout their stewardship of public lands, Montana protesters say their park is suffering \u8211\'20\'13 and help is urgently needed\par \par \uc2 Dozens of former rangers, park volunteers, and local residents protested at the gateway to Montana\u8217\'20\'19s Glacier national park on Wednesday against the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2025/feb/22/forest-land-workers-firings-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 staff cuts and hiring freezes}}}\uc2  that have thrown many national parks into crisis, including Glacier.\par \par \uc2 Current and former staffers and watchdog groups say the cuts have meant staff are not able to keep up the facilities and infrastructure. Some say the park has been left with inadequate infrastructure and too little staff to be able to respond to emergencies.\par \par \uc2 Although it might look to visitors like operations in Glacier are normal, \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s like walking down a Hollywood movie set where the front looks great but there\u8217\'20\'19s nothing behind it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sarah Lundstrum, Glacier program manager with the \uc2 National Parks Conservation Association\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The protesters held signs, chanted and waved at tourists during a visit to the park from the Congressional Western Caucus. Hosted by the Montana Republican congressman and former interior secretary Ryan Zinke, the caucus came to the park to showcase the success of the 2020 Great American Outdoors Act, which secured federal funding for protection and maintenance of public lands.\par \par \uc2 Montana\u8217\'20\'19s Republican senator Steve Daines championed that bill during \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s first term, calling it \u8220\'20\'1Cthe greatest conservation win for Montana and the entire country in 50 years\u8221\'20\'1D. In May, Daines introduced the America the Beautiful Act to extend federal funding for projects to address crucial maintenance backlogs.\par \par \uc2 But congressional support for funding projects in national parks comes at a jarring disconnect with the Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s slashing of jobs at national parks countrywide, including at Glacier, where an already overworked staff has been left with little to no bandwidth to implement projects.\par \par \uc2 No congressional Republicans, including Daines or Zinke, have spoken up against the cuts and freezes, and all voted for the One Big Beautiful Bill Act that rescinded $276m from the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  (NPS).\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re supporting the park, but drawing attention to the fact our policymakers are grandstanding in a national park where behind the scenes they\u8217\'20\'19ve gutted staff and gutted funding,\u8221\'20\'1D said Suzanne Hindler, one of the rally\u8217\'20\'19s organizers. She said organizers specifically chose to hold the event outside the park to avoid adding more work for already overburdened park staff during peak tourist season.\par \par \uc2 Hindler emphasized that funding for national parks was crucial. But without the staff to execute the work, new problems would arise with no one to fix them, she said.\par \par \uc2 Jan Metzmaker, a longtime park employee who was on Glacier\u8217\'20\'19s first all-women trail crew in the 1970s, said: \u8220\'20\'1CI can see the deterioration in the services and in the facilities.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey really need to put some money into those, because this place is crazy with people. It\u8217\'20\'19s being loved to death. But there\u8217\'20\'19s no way that they can do the maintenance and all the things that need to be done in the park now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Visitation to national parks reached a record 331.9 million last year. But because of the Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s hiring freezes, terminations, and buyout and early retirement offers, US national parks have lost nearly a quarter of permanent staff, with seasonal hiring behind by nearly 8,000 positions. Further staff cuts, described as \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://irma.nps.gov/Stats/SSRSReports/Park%20Specific%20Reports/Recreation%20Visitors%20By%20Month%20(1979%20-%20Last%20Calendar%20Year)?Park=GLAC"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deep and blunt}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Caggressive and swift\u8221\'20\'1D by National Parks Traveler, the multi-media outlet that covers the \uc2 NPS\uc2 , are held up in court but may still be forthcoming.\par \par \uc2 In Glacier, which has seen a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://irma.nps.gov/Stats/SSRSReports/Park%20Specific%20Reports/Recreation%20Visitors%20By%20Month%20(1979%20-%20Last%20Calendar%20Year)?Park=GLAC"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 7.5% increase}}}\uc2  in visitors from last year\u8217\'20\'19s record high, the park is trying to operate with a 25% loss of staff. Vacancies span from chief ranger and fire positions, wildlife scientists, multiple environmental impact analysis positions, and emergency services, to mechanics, electricians, plumbers and IT positions.\par \par \uc2 After the federal government canceled all national parks\u8217\'20\'19 internet contracts this year, Lundstrum said, Glacier now uses StarLink, which some staffers say is spotty, goes down entirely, and often fails to connect park dispatch and 911 calls. There\u8217\'20\'19s only one IT person remaining to address technical problems, those staffers, who asked to remain anonymous because they fear retaliation for speaking out, in a park that spans the Continental Divide, has no cell service, and regularly sees lost and injured hikers and encounters with wildlife, including the park\u8217\'20\'19s dense population of grizzly bears.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, said a current park employee who spoke on condition of anonymity, there are no longer enough staff to safely respond to emergencies. It\u8217\'20\'19s only luck \u8220\'20\'1Cthat the park hasn\u8217\'20\'19t had any big events this year\u8221\'20\'1D, they said. \u8220\'20\'1CIn past years we\u8217\'20\'19ve had big fires, major search-and-rescue operations, really critical injuries. It\u8217\'20\'19s only a matter of time until there\u8217\'20\'19s an event we can\u8217\'20\'19t respond to appropriately and there\u8217\'20\'19s a mass failure of a system.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And yet the interior secretary, \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 , issued an order in April requiring all parks to remain \u8220\'20\'1Copen and accessible\u8221\'20\'1D despite the reduced staff. In Glacier, that might come at the cost of visitor and staff safety.\par \par \uc2 The Department of the Interior did not immediately respond to a request for comment on the concerns the staffers and watchdog groups raised. The offices of Zinke and Daines also did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 The staffers say that remaining staff are doing \u8220\'20\'1Ctwice the job they used to\u8221\'20\'1D. Law enforcement are covering twice their previous area, maintenance workers are doing jobs they are not trained for, and outside recreation operators, such as Glacier Guides and Montana Raft, are emptying trash and cleaning bathrooms at river accesses to make up for the gaps. The mentality inside the park, said the employee, \u8220\'20\'1Cis that if you\u8217\'20\'19re the only one left, you\u8217\'20\'19ll do whatever you can to help\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Association of National Park Rangers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.anpr.org/release/2025/0808"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  that \u8220\'20\'1Camid federal budget cuts, some seasonal employees at Yosemite national park worked for as long as six weeks without pay in recent months as park supervisors struggled to manage hiring\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 One of the rally attenders, a local woman named Kathy who asked not to be identified by her last name, is a volunteer with the Glacier National Park Association. \u8220\'20\'1CWe do restoration, painting, backcountry patrol, visitor center, vehicle reservations. We want to do things, but unfortunately, we don\u8217\'20\'19t have enough supervisors \u8211\'20\'13 rangers \u8211\'20\'13 to have volunteers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels like the government is setting us up to fail,\u8221\'20\'1D said the Glacier employee.\par \par \uc2 Experts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.doi.gov/document-library/secretary-order/so-3426-ensuring-national-parks-are-open-and-accessiblehttps://wessiler.substack.com/p/in-gops-public-lands-sell-off-the"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 worry}}}\uc2  that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://protectnps.org/2025/06/03/coalition-to-protect-americas-national-parks-calls-presidents-budget-deeply-problematic-and-dangerous/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 budget proposal }}}\uc2 to cut 36% of the national park budget, which could force the closure of up to 350 park units, is a deliberate attempt to sabotage the park system as an excuse to sell those lands for profit.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHollowing out staffing, cutting budgets, changing priorities \u8211\'20\'13 all of that very much lends itself to the idea of essentially causing those agencies to fail at meeting their mandates, and that will lead to the call for privatization,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lundstrum. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause if the government can\u8217\'20\'19t manage that land, then obviously somebody else should, right? In documents like Project 2025, there are calls for the privatization of land, or the selloff of land.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Multiple sources say that morale among Glacier staff is low. \u8220\'20\'1CThe civilian federal workforce used to be non-partisan, so you always felt like you could have your opinion \u8211\'20\'13 liberal or conservative \u8211\'20\'13 without fear of retribution,\u8221\'20\'1D said one employee. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd now the undertone is to stay under the radar. If you speak up and say, \u8216\'20\'18this is wrong\u8217\'20\'19, you pretty much have a target on your back.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One young mother who came to the rally with her two small sons asked not to be identified because her husband is a federal employee; just this month, the justice department {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/justice-department-fired-official-whose-husband-operated-controversial-iceblock-app/#"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fired}}}\uc2  an official whose husband developed a phone app that tracked Ice agents.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHaving these two little guys is just a constant reminder of how much our world is changing, and the need to stand up for it. Everything could be gone in a blink,\u8221\'20\'1D the mother said.\par \par \uc2 Glacier is also the national park poster child for climate change, as its namesake glaciers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.umt.edu/this-is-montana/columns/stories/mountain_science_glacier_part_3.php"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 are predicted}}}\uc2  to be completely gone in the coming decades. Yet the administration, without any pushback from congressional Republicans, has cut and scrubbed climate science and reversed Biden-era initiatives to curb climate change.\par \par \uc2 In his press release, Daines said he introduced the America the Beautiful Act \u8220\'20\'1Cso that people can get outside and enjoy the natural beauty we\u8217\'20\'19re lucky to have here in the US\u8221\'20\'1D, and that he was \u8220\'20\'1Cproud\u8221\'20\'1D to \u8220\'20\'1Cprotect our outdoor way of life for generations to come\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Hildner said she was not fooled. She said: \u8220\'20\'1CTo see capitalism as the driving force for managing lands, rather than conservation, is really terrifying: for myself, for what it means for future generations, and what it means for the planet. How do we as a public help the folks who\u8217\'20\'19ve been elected to govern see what the real costs are?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250822el8m003pe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
