\par\par \b \uc2 US inflation unexpectedly stays at 2.7%; UK ministers appoint advisers for \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  collapse \u8216\'20\'18contingency plans\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Julia Kollewe \par \uc2 2,799 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 August 2025\par 14:05 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 US and China extend 90-day tariff truce; UK labour market slows as employers hold off hiring\par \par \uc2 3.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 US consumer price inflation held at 2.7% in July, unchanged from the month before, confounding expectations of an uptick. However, a core measure that strips out volatile food and energy costs was higher than expected at 3.1%.\par \par \uc2 Economists are still expecting the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to cut interest rates at its meeting in September.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street stocks rose after the data, with the Dow Jones 0.55% ahead, while the FTSE 100 index in London was flat.\par \par \uc2 UK ministers have appointed insolvency advisers to make contingency plans for the potential collapse of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/thames-water"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Thames Water}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The company, which supplies 16 million customers, has been racing to pull together a deal to avoid financial collapse.\par \par \uc2 Employers in the UK have cut annual pay increases and pared back hiring in recent months as the economic slowdown took its toll on the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 Data from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk/office-for-national-statistics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Office for National Statistics}}}\uc2  (ONS), released on Tuesday, showed that UK unemployment edged higher in the three months to June but the official jobless rate was unchanged at 4.7%, a four-year high.\par \par \uc2 Pay growth including bonuses dropped from 5% to 4.6% over the same period. However, stripping out one-off awards, pay growth remained at 5%, suggesting employers had cut back on incentives.\par \par \uc2 Giving a strong indication of employers\u8217\'20\'19 reluctance to hire new staff, the vacancy rate fell by 44,000. The drop of more than 5% in the three months to June on the previous quarter was the 37th consecutive fall in vacancies and took the total to well below pre-pandemic levels at 718,000.\par \par \uc2 Markets breathed a sigh of relief after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  once again delayed implementing sweeping {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-tariffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tariffs}}}\uc2  on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/china"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 China}}}\uc2 , announcing another 90-day pause just hours before the last agreement between the world\u8217\'20\'19s two largest economies was due to expire.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the US president signed an executive order extending the deadline for higher tariffs on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/china"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 China}}}\uc2  until 10 November.Thank you for reading. We\u8217\'20\'19ll be back tomorrow. Take care \u8211\'20\'13 JK\par \par \uc2 2.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 Paloma Faith and Robert Smith among 100 musicians urging Starmer to stop Rosebank oil field\par \par \uc2 Artists including Paloma Faith, Lola Young and The Cure\u8217\'20\'19s lead singer Robert Smith have urged the prime minister to reject future drilling at Rosebank in the North Sea.\par \par \uc2 In a letter addressed to Sir \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  and Energy Secretary \uc2 Ed Miliband\uc2 , the musicians argued that further development of the oil field north west of Shetland would undermine the UK\u8217\'20\'19s climate commitments and the sustainability of the cultural sector. Estimated to contain up to 300m barrels of oil, Rosebank is the UK\u8217\'20\'19s largest untapped oil field.\par \par \uc2 The Labour manifesto rules out granting new licences for new fields, but ministers say that does not apply to Rosebank and \uc2 Shell\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Jackdaw, which already have their licences and are now awaiting environmental consent to begin drilling.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 2.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 Club World Cup and summer tennis bring in the bets for \uc2 Entain\par \par \uc2 A flurry of bets on the football Club World Cup, England\u8217\'20\'19s triumphant European Women\u8217\'20\'19s Championship campaign and summer tennis tournaments helped the UK gambling company \uc2 Entain\uc2  to better-than-expected results, helping it brace for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2025/aug/07/the-guardian-view-on-gambling-a-public-reckoning-with-the-dark-side-of-this-highly-profitable-industry-is-overdue"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 looming tax rises in its domestic market}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The owner of brands including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/ladbrokes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ladbrokes}}}\uc2  and Coral reported an 11% rise in underlying profits to \u163\'00\'A3583m in the first half of the year, on revenues that rose 3% to nearly \u163\'00\'A32.6bn.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 BetMGM\uc2 , \uc2 Entain\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 50%-owned US joint venture, revenues rose by more than a third. US sports betting continues to grow rapidly following the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s overturning in 2018 of a decades-old ban on the practice.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Entain\uc2  also performed strongly in its home market, with online gambling revenues up 21% in the UK and Ireland.\par \par \uc2 2.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 As temperatures are rising again across the UK, residents have warned about unbearable heat inside some of London\u8217\'20\'19s new-builds.\par \par \uc2 Experts say many new homes being built in the UK are not designed to withstand extreme summer temperatures.\par \par \uc2 2.05pm BST\par \par \uc2 Stephen Brown, deputy chief North America economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , said\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.06pm BST\par \par \uc2 1.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 Richard Flax, chief investment officer at the European fund manager Moneyfarm, said:\par \par \uc2 1.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 Food prices in the US rose by 2.9% year-on-year last month, the data showed, while energy prices slipped by 1.6%.\par \par \uc2 So far, there are few signs of trade tariffs pushing up inflation, although core inflation rose to the highest annual rate since February.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 1.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 US inflation unexpectedly stays at 2.7%\par \par \uc2 US inflation unexpectedly held at 2.7% last month, confounding expectations of an uptick, while a core measure came in higher than expected.\par \par \uc2 The annual headline rate of inflation was expected to rise slightly to 2.8%.\par \par \uc2 Stock market futures gained after the figures were released. Spot gold rose by 0.2% to $3,351 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 However, the core rate of inflation, stripping out volatile food and energy costs, was slightly higher than expected at 3.1%, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/cpi.nr0.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This complicates the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s policy decision. It had been expected to lower rates in September, and the data is unlikely to change that expectation.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 1.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 The oil cartel \uc2 Opec\uc2  has lifted its forecast for global oil demand next year, as it trimmed its forecast for supply increases from the US and other producers outside the wider Opec+ group, which includes Russia.\par \par \uc2 World oil demand is estimated to rise by 1.38m barrels per day (bpd) in 2026, the \uc2 Organisation of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\uc2  said in its monthly report, up 100,000 bpd from its previous forecast. Its estimate for this year is unchanged.\par \par \uc2 Rising demand and a drop in supply growth from outside the oil cartel would make it easier for the Opec+ group to pump more oil to claw back market share, after years of cuts aimed at supporting the market and prices.\par \par \uc2 In July, Opec+ raised oil ouput by 335,000 bpd.\par \par \uc2 12.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 US stock futures are flat ahead of US inflation figures, out in 40 minutes, that could be key to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s next interest decision.\par \par \uc2 Economists are expecting a slight uptick in the headline inflation rate to 2.8% in July, from 2.7% the month before.\par \par \uc2 The impact of new trade tariffs and uncertainty over future trade policy under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have complicated the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s decision on rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 There is some relief in markets that the US and China have extended their tariff truce for another 90 days until 10 November, staving off triple-digit increases in each other import duties.\par \par \uc2 Over here, the FTSE 100 index is flat at 9,135 while the German market is down 0.5% and the French bourse has added 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 12.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Liberal Democrat MP for Witney, Charlie Maynard, said:\par \par \uc2 12.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 Here is our full story on \uc2 Thames Water\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 UK ministers have appointed insolvency advisers to make contingency plans for the potential collapse of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/thames-water"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Thames Water}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The company, which supplies 16 million customers, has been racing to pull together a deal to avoid financial collapse.\par \par \uc2 The appointment indicates that \uc2 FTI\uc2  is the frontrunner to act as administrator if the government enacted an SAR, although a court would ultimately approve such a step.\par \par \uc2 The government has been trying to avoid such an outcome, with the Treasury threatening that a potential \u163\'00\'A34bn bill from the SAR {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/may/03/treasury-threatens-defra-with-4bn-bill-if-thames-water-nationalised"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 could be forced}}}\uc2  on to Steve Reed\u8217\'20\'19s environment department. This process would ensure that the taps stayed on for customers but would heap immediate costs on to the government.\par \par \uc2 However, the government\u8217\'20\'19s Water (Special Measures) Act contains a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2025/5/crossheading/special-administration-orders"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 provision for SAR}}}\uc2  costs to be recouped from customer bills further down the line.\par \par \uc2 12.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 Gold prices flat; Swiss metals group wants formal US commitment on gold tariffs\par \par \uc2 In financial markets, spot gold prices are flat after dropping nearly 1.6% on Monday, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  announced there would be no US tariffs on imported goldbars.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGold will not be Tariffed!\u8221\'20\'1D the US president said on his social media account, without giving further details.\par \par \uc2 Spot edged up to $3,352 an ounce earlier. On Friday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/aug/08/gold-spike-trump-tariffs-on-bars-ftse-stock-market-news-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-68958e358f080766d85bb9bf#block-68958e358f080766d85bb9bf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gold futures hit a record high}}}\uc2  after a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/aug/08/gold-futures-soar-to-record-high-after-reports-of-us-tariffs-on-swiss-bars"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruling on the US customs and border protection service\u8217\'20\'19s website suggested that one-kilo bars of gold could be subject to new US import tariffs}}}\uc2 . This would have been especially damaging for Switzerland, a major refining and transit hub for gold. Trump recently imposed a 39% tariff on Swiss exports to the US.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss stock exchange is also flat today.\par \par \uc2 But, the head of the Swiss precious metals association ASFCMP said only a formal decision will provide certainty. Its president Christoph Wild said:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 11.59am BST\par \par \uc2 Investor morale worsens in Germany\par \par \uc2 Investor morale in Germany has worsened markedly, as financial market experts were disappointed by the recent trade deal between the EU and the US, according to a closely watched survey.\par \par \uc2 After increasing for several months, the Centre for European economic research (ZEW)\u8216\'20\'18s indicator of economic sentiment in Germany fell by 18 points to 34.7 points. The assessment of the current economic situation also declined, by 9.1 points.\par \par \uc2 ZEW president, Professor \uc2 Achim Wambach\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 Although initial growth estimates for the eurozone were better than those for Germany in the second quarter, they were revised lower for the currently and are currently at 25.1 points \u8211\'20\'13 11.0 points below July\u8217\'20\'19s reading. The assessment of the current economic situation also worsened, by 7 points.\par \par \uc2 10.46am BST\par \par \uc2 UK ministers appoint insolvency advisers for \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  collapse \u8216\'20\'18contingency plans\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 UK ministers have lined up insolvency practitioners to prepare for the potential collapse of \uc2 Thames Water\uc2 , Britain\u8217\'20\'19s biggest water company.\par \par \uc2 Steve Reed, the environment secretary, has signed off the appointment of \uc2 FTI Consulting\uc2  to advise on contingency plans for \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  to be placed into a Special Administration regime (SAR), in news first reported by Sky News.\par \par \uc2 This puts \uc2 FTI Consulting\uc2  as the frontrunner to act as the company\u8217\'20\'19s administrator if it fails to secure a private sector bailout - although such an appointment would take place in court.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.49am BST\par \par \uc2 10.24am BST\par \par \uc2 Hannah Slaughter, a senior economist at the \uc2 Resolution Foundation\uc2  thinktank, said that while the unemployment rate remained steady at 4.7%, it was up from 4.2% a year ago and 3.9% before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 She said:\par \par \uc2 She added that the job losses were concentrated in low-paying sectors such as retail and hospitality. She said it meant the government was likely to push back against campaigns for a big increase to the minimum wage next year, fearing it would lead to even larger job losses.\par \par \uc2 9.27am BST\par \par \uc2 Here is the government\u8217\'20\'19s, and unions\u8217\'20\'19 response to the latest job market data data from the ONS.\par \par \uc2 Employment minister Alison McGovern said:\par \par \uc2 Paul Nowak, general secretary of the TUC which comprises 48 trade unions and represents 5.5 million workers, said:\par \par \uc2 On the latest zero-hours contracts figures, which show more than a million workers employed on a zero-hours contract, headded:\par \par \uc2 8.48am BST\par \par \uc2 High river temperatures force French power plant to reduce production\par \par \uc2 French nuclear power stations are struggling in the heatwave \u8211\'20\'13 after a swarm of jellyfish forced the shutdown of the Gravelines power plant on the North Sea coast, high river temperatures in other parts of the country mean that another plant will have to reduce output.\par \par \uc2 Power production at France\u8217\'20\'19s Bugey 3 nuclear reactor is expected to be reduced by 500 megawatts (MW) on Wednesday, as high river temperatures reduce the plant\u8217\'20\'19s ability to take in cooling water.\par \par \uc2 A heatwave across France has led to several warnings of power reductions at a number of nuclear plants, particularly on the Rh\u244\'00\'F4ne river in the east and the Garonne in the west.The high water temperature warnings for the Saint Alban plant - down river from the Bugey site - and the Golfech site in the west were moved to Thursday, but restrictions have not yet been issued.\par \par \uc2 Nuclear power accounts for about 70% of total French power consumption every year, but August is the main holiday season, reducing demand for electricity.\par \par \uc2 Today,\par \par \uc2 8.28am BST\par \par \uc2 The Recruitment and Employment Confederation\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s deputy chief executive \uc2 Kate Shoesmith\uc2  said:\par \par \uc2 8.01am BST\par \par \uc2 The breakdown of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ons.gov.uk/employmentandlabourmarket/peopleinwork/employmentandemployeetypes/bulletins/averageweeklyearningsingreatbritain/latest"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ONS data}}}\uc2  shows that the wholesale, retail, hotels and restaurants sector posted the strongest annual regular growth rate (excluding bonuses), at 6.8%, in April to June.\par \par \uc2 The finance and business services sector, which pay out more bonuses, had the lowest annual regular growth rate, at 3.1%.\par \par \uc2 Average annual pay growth was 5.7% for the public sector, and 4.8% for the private sector.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.26am BST\par \par \uc2 7.50am BST\par \par \uc2 Turning to the UK labour market: it is clearly losing momentum, with employers cutting back on bonuses and hiring.\par \par \uc2 Suren Thiru\uc2 , economics director at the \uc2 Institute of Chartered Accountants in England and Wales\uc2 , said:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/aug/07/bank-of-england-cuts-interest-rates-quarter-point-4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Bank of England cut interest rates for the fifth time}}}\uc2  in a year last Thursday amid concerns over a weakening economy, but warned that rising food prices could drive inflation to 4% (the central bank is charged with keeping inflation at 2%).\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.21am BST\par \par \uc2 7.34am BST\par \par \uc2 Oil prices are rising as the extended tariff truce between the US and China eased worries that an escalation of their trade war would disrupt the global economy.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude futures gained 0.4% to $66.90 a barrel, while US West Texas Intermediate crude futures also rose by 0.4% to $64.20.\par \par \uc2 Stock markets in the Asia-Pacific region mostly rose, with Japan\u8217\'20\'19s Nikkei index hitting a record closing high and finishing 2.15% higher.\par \par \uc2 Australian stocks extended their gains for a second day, and also reached a new intra-day all-time high. China is the main export destination for Australian goods.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.57am BST\par \par \uc2 7.19am BST\par \par \uc2 Introduction: US and China extend 90-day tariff truce; UK jobless rate steady, vacancies fall\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 The US and China have extended their truce on trade tariffs for another 90 days, staving off triple-digit duties on imports just as US retailers start stockpiling for the key end-of-year holiday season.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  posted on his Truth Social platform that he signed the executive order for the extension, and that \u8220\'20\'1Call other elements of the Agreement will remain the same\u8221\'20\'1D. Beijing\u8217\'20\'19s Commerce Ministry announced the extension of the tariff pause early on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s executive order stated:\par \par \uc2 The tariff pause was due to expire on Tuesday at 12:01am EDT. The extension until November will be welcomed by US retailers who are now able to buy electronics, toys and other products at lower tariff rates ahead of Christmas.\par \par \uc2 Trump had threatened tariffs on Chinese goods imports of up to 145% while Chinese duties on US goods were set to hit 125%.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ll see what happens,\u8221\'20\'1D the US president said at a news conference on Monday, flagging what he called his good relationship with Chinese president Xi Jinping.\par \par \uc2 China said the extension was \u8220\'20\'1Ca measure to further implement the important consensus reached by the two heads of state during their 5 June call\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The UK labour market continues to cool, according to the latest official figures. Regular pay growth in the UK held steady at 5% but wage growth slowed once bonuses are included, while the unemployment rate stayed at 4.7% and vacancies fell again.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  said average wages, excluding bonuses, grew by 5% between April and June, the same pace as in the three months to May, while total pay growth including bonuses slowed to 4.6% from 5%.\par \par \uc2 Vacancies fell by 44,000 between May and July. The statistics office said feedback suggests \u8220\'20\'1Csome firms may not be recruiting new workers or replacing workers who have left\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 At lunchtime UK time, we\u8217\'20\'19ll be getting the latest US inflation data.\par \par \uc2 The Agenda\par \par \uc2 * 10am BST: Germany/Eurozone ZEW economic survey\par \par \uc2 * 1.30pm BST: US inflation for July (previous: 2.7%; forecast: 2.8%)\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250812el8c0018h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
