\par\par \b \uc2 Trump calls for resignation of Intel chief a day after 100% chip tariff threat\b0\par\par\uc2 Edward Helmore \par \uc2 590 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 August 2025\par 03:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Intel share price drops after US president accuses \uc2 Lip-Bu Tan\uc2  of links to Chinese Communist party\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has called on Intel\u8217\'20\'19s chief executive to resign, alleging \uc2 Lip-Bu Tan\uc2  had ties to the Chinese Communist party, sending the stock of the US chipmaker falling.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe CEO of Intel is highly CONFLICTED and must resign, immediately,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump posted on Truth Social about Tan. \u8220\'20\'1CThere is no other solution to this problem. Thank you for your attention to this problem!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Shares in Intel dropped more than 3% in early trading. The company did not immediately respond to a request for comment on Trump\u8217\'20\'19s post.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s comments came a day after he threatened a 100% tariff on imported semiconductors and chips, which could favor Intel as a US-based semiconductor firm.\par \par \uc2 Trump did exclude \uc2 Taiwan Semiconductor\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2 , companies that have said they plan to increase their investments in US manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2  chief \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  announced from the White House that the company would invest $100bn in US chip fabrication.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s criticism of Intel, which has lagged chipmakers such as \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  in producing graphics-processing chips suitable for AI applications, comes after Arkansas senator Tom Cotton wrote a letter to company chairman Frank Yeary expressing concern over Tan\u8217\'20\'19s investments and ties to semiconductor firms that are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/technology/intel-ceo-invested-hundreds-chinese-companies-some-with-military-ties-2025-04-10/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly linked}}}\uc2  to the CCP and the People\u8217\'20\'19s Liberation Army, the party\u8217\'20\'19s military arm.\par \par \uc2 Cotton, a Republican, asked Intel\u8217\'20\'19s board if Tan had divested his interests and questioned if Tan\u8217\'20\'19s previous leadership of \uc2 Cadence Design Systems\uc2 , a company that last month said it had sold products to China\u8217\'20\'19s National University of Defense Technology, a violation of US export controls.\par \par \uc2 Cotton said Tan controlled dozens of Chinese companies, at least eight of which had ties to the People\u8217\'20\'19s Liberation Army.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the company its board and CEO were \u8220\'20\'1Cdeeply committed to advancing US national and economic security interests and are making significant investments aligned with the President\u8217\'20\'19s America First agenda.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIntel has been manufacturing in America for 56 years,\u8221\'20\'1D the statement said, adding that the company looked forward to its \u8220\'20\'1Ccontinued engagement with the Administration.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Tan, 65, is regarded as an industry veteran in technology and venture capital. He was tapped to lead the once-dominant personal computing and laptop chipmaker in March as part of a turnaround effort. Intel\u8217\'20\'19s market valuation is about $89bn, compared with $4.4tn for rival \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tan, 65, has said he wants to sell off Intel assets that are not core to the company\u8217\'20\'19s revitalization, cut jobs and to delay or cancel projects to reduce operating expenses. Intel has received about $8bn from the Chips and Science Act for US investments, the Biden-era legislation framed as a national security imperative to reduce US dependence on foreign chip production.\par \par \uc2 A number of foreign-based chipmakers have recently announced they are boosting US chip production , including Taiwan\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 TSMC\uc2  and South Korea\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Samsung\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Beneficiaries of the Chips Act besides Intel include \uc2 TSMC\uc2 , \uc2 Micron Technology\uc2 , \uc2 Samsung\uc2 , \uc2 GlobalFoundries\uc2  and \uc2 Texas Instruments\uc2 . But the $1bn grants are dwarfed by more than $400bn in pledged private-sector investments.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration wants Congress to scrap the Chips Act, arguing that tariffs are a more effective incentive for companies to build fabrication plants on US soil, pointing to \uc2 TSMC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision to expand its chipmaking capacity from three to six plants in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250807el8700566\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
