\par\par \b \uc2 NWSL says it has \u8216\'20\'18no duty of care\u8217\'20\'19 for women suing San Diego Wave over assault claims\b0\par\par\uc2 Matthew Hall \par \uc2 1,133 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2025\par 16:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The NWSL and Wave are co-defendants in the suitLeague denies all allegations in caseLegal experts brand league response as \u8216\'20\'18boilerplate\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 In a filing last month, the \uc2 National Women\u8217\'20\'19s Soccer League\uc2  denied all allegations contained in a lawsuit brought by former San Diego Wave employees and called for the suit to be \u8220\'20\'1Cdismissed in its entirety,\u8221\'20\'1D claiming that the league has \u8220\'20\'1Cno duty of care\u8221\'20\'1D to the six women suing the club and the league.\par \par \uc2 The 2024 suit contains allegations of sexual assault of an employee by a co-worker while both employed at the club, along with claims of sexual harassment, racial discrimination, disability discrimination, failure to investigate and prevent harassment and discrimination.\par \par \uc2 In the answer to the allegations filed on 8 July to the Superior Court of California in San Diego, the NWSL\u8217\'20\'19s legal representatives, Los Angeles firm \uc2 Arentfox Schiff\uc2  , deny that the six former Wave employees were \u8220\'20\'1Cdamaged or harmed\u8221\'20\'1D because of any act of the league.\par \par \uc2 The NWSL\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers claim \u8220\'20\'1Cthe doctrine of consent,\u8221\'20\'1D inferring that the sexual assault and harassment claims were based on consensual encounters, and also that certain allegations are beyond California\u8217\'20\'19s statute of limitations. The NWSL\u8217\'20\'19s defense also claims it had no duty of care to San Diego Wave employees as any incidents that occurred were between the individuals and the club \u8211\'20\'13 not the league.\par \par \uc2 According to legal experts interviewed by the Guardian, the NWSL\u8217\'20\'19s response to the lawsuit is a catch-all answer that aims to cover all possible angles of defense.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe defendant has simply listed every affirmative defense that could apply to any of the claims,\u8221\'20\'1D said Professor David Oppenheimer, clinical professor of law at the University of California, Berkeley School of Law. \u8220\'20\'1CThe common term for such an answer is \u8216\'20\'18boilerplate.\u8217\'20\'19 The purpose is to preserve any conceivable defense.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 An NWSL spokesperson said the league could not comment on pending litigation. Arentfox Schiff did not respond to requests for comment and clarification.\par \par \uc2 The six women who filed the lawsuit include one who claims she was sexually assaulted by a San Diego Wave co-worker, another who claims she was sexually harassed by the same co-worker, and Brittany Alvarado, a former videographer for the Wave, claiming workplace-related violations. Three other women are named in the suit for similar alleged violations. Alvarado brought attention to the workplace environment at San Diego Wave after she posted on social media in 2024 that the \u8220\'20\'1CNWSL must take immediate action to remove Jill Ellis from both the San Diego Wave and the league entirely.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ellis, the former USWNT head coach, was Wave president at the time of the events alleged in the lawsuit and had a hands-on role in managing the club. Ellis has since departed San Diego to take a senior role {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://inside.fifa.com/organisation/media-releases/jill-ellis-joins-fifa-as-chief-football-officer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at Fifa as its chief football officer }}}\uc2 . Ellis is not a defendant in the lawsuit but is named within it, described as having \u8220\'20\'1Cset an abusive and toxic workplace culture\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Conce asking random men in Kansas City about their penises\u8221\'20\'1D. (Ellis has filed a separate defamation suit against Alvarado based on her social media post.)\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2025/jul/24/san-diego-wave-nwsl-investigation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Guardian previously revealed that a 2024 investigation }}}\uc2  by the NWSL into San Diego Wave management found that the club\u8217\'20\'19s front office \u8220\'20\'1Ccould have done more\u8221\'20\'1D to address a sexual assault allegation but found no specific issue with how the claim was handled because the alleged victim did not use the term \u8220\'20\'1Csexual\u8221\'20\'1D when describing her experience. The San Diego Wave is a co-defendant in the case with the NWSL.\par \par \uc2 As reported by the Guardian, Jane Doe 1 alleges that a night out in San Diego with a co-worker led to \u8220\'20\'1Cinappropriate activities including a game of \u8216\'20\'18Sexy Jenga\u8217\'20\'19 that led to him pressuring her into non-consensual sexual acts\u8221\'20\'1D. Jane Doe 1 alleges that later at her apartment, while she was drunk, she was pressured into \u8220\'20\'1Csexual activity that she explicitly stated she did not consent to\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The NWSL\u8217\'20\'19s defense argues that consent was given. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PEN&sectionNum=261.6."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Under California law }}}\uc2 , consent requires an individual to freely give agreement to engage in sexual activity \u8220\'20\'1Cand voluntarily and have knowledge of the nature of the act or transaction involved.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another woman, \u8220\'20\'1CJane Doe 2\u8221\'20\'1D, alleged in the lawsuit she was sexually harassed by the same San Diego Wave employee who this time sent her non-work related messages and images via \uc2 Snapchat\uc2  that became increasingly sexual in nature. Jane Doe 2 claims she was terminated by the club after she did not work the minimum number of shifts in her non-full-time role \u8211\'20\'13 shifts that were assigned by the alleged harasser.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn a negligence case, consent is a potential defense when the plaintiff has agreed to participate in an activity knowing that there are significant risks of harm, and willing to subject themselves to them,\u8221\'20\'1D Oppenheimer said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe best examples come from sports, but for the athletes \u8230\'20\'26 In this case, for the statutory claims under the civil rights laws, there is no defense of consent.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Oppenheimer offered a hypothetical scenario for the NWSL to launch a successful defense:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C[The] defendant proves that as each employee was hired they were warned, \u8216\'20\'18You need to know that this is a toxic workplace. We permit emotional and sexual harassment, we retaliate against people who complain, and we fire people in bad faith. If you don\u8217\'20\'19t want to subject yourself to this, don\u8217\'20\'19t work here.\u8217\'20\'19 That might be enough to defend themselves on the negligent hiring and management claim, but it would subject them to liability on the statutory discrimination claims.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The defense also claims the case should be dismissed because the plaintiffs delayed bringing the lawsuit and that a two-year statute of limitations under California law should be enforced. The earliest claims made in the case took place in mid-2022. The suit was filed in October of 2024. The NWSL\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers cite a section of California code of civil procedure stipulating that there is a two-year statute of limitations to sue for \u8220\'20\'1CAn action for assault, battery, or injury to, or for the death of, an individual caused by the wrongful act or neglect of another.\u8221\'20\'1D California law stipulates elsewhere that there is a 10-year statute of limitations for criminal sexual assault claims (the NWSL case in question is civil).\par \par \uc2 The NWSL claims it cannot be held accountable for events described in the lawsuit because they fall outside the relationship between the league and the Wave. The league claims it did not know the incidents occurred nor should it have known.\par \par \uc2 The former San Diego Wave employees have requested a trial by jury.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250805el85004xu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
