\par\par \b \uc2 Ninety laptops, millions of dollars: US woman jailed over North Korea remote-work scam\b0\par\par\uc2 Eric Berger \par \uc2 1,335 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2025\par 00:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Christine Chapman apologizes for role in identity fraud that amassed millions to allegedly aid nuclear weapons program\par \par \uc2 In March 2020, about the time the Covid pandemic started, Christina Chapman, a woman who lived in Arizona and Minnesota, received a message on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  asking her to \u8220\'20\'1Cbe the US face\u8221\'20\'1D of a company and help overseas IT workers gain remote employment.\par \par \uc2 As working from home became the norm for many people, Chapman was able to find jobs for the foreign workers at hundreds of US companies, including some in the Fortune 500, such as \uc2 Nike\uc2 ; \u8220\'20\'1Ca premier Silicon Valley technology company\u8221\'20\'1D; and one of the \u8220\'20\'1Cmost recognizable media and entertainment companies in the world\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The employers thought they were hiring US citizens. They were actually people in North Korea.\par \par \uc2 Chapman was participating in the North Korean government\u8217\'20\'19s scheme to deploy thousands of \u8220\'20\'1Chighly skilled IT workers\u8221\'20\'1D by stealing identities to make it look like they were in the US or other countries. They have collected millions of dollars to boost the government\u8217\'20\'19s nuclear weapons development, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/arizona-woman-sentenced-17m-information-technology-worker-fraud-scheme-generated-revenue"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US justice department}}}\uc2  and court records.\par \par \uc2 Chapman\u8217\'20\'19s bizarre story \u8211\'20\'13 which culminated in an eight-year prison sentence \u8211\'20\'13 is a curious mix of geopolitics, international crime and one woman\u8217\'20\'19s tragic tale of isolation and working from home in a gig-dominated economy where increasingly everything happens through a computer screen and it is harder to tell fact from fiction.\par \par \uc2 The secret North Korean workers, according to the federal government and cybersecurity experts, not only help the US\u8217\'20\'19s adversary \u8211\'20\'13 a dictatorship which has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/aug/04/north-korea-wants-sanctions-eased-on-metal-fuel-and-liquor-and-suits-to-restart-us-talks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hobbled by international sanctions}}}\uc2  over its weapons program \u8211\'20\'13 but also harm US citizens by stealing their identities and potentially hurt domestic companies by \u8220\'20\'1Cenabling malicious cyber intrusions\u8221\'20\'1D into their networks.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COnce Covid hit and everybody really went virtual, a lot of the tech jobs never went back to the office,\u8221\'20\'1D said Benjamin Racenberg, a senior intelligence manager at Nisos, a cybersecurity firm.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCompanies quickly realized: I can get good talent from anywhere. North Koreans and other employment fraudsters have realized that they can trick hiring systems to get jobs. I don\u8217\'20\'19t think that we have done enough as a community to prevent this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 To run the schemes, the North Koreans need facilitators in the United States, because the companies \u8220\'20\'1Caren\u8217\'20\'19t going to willingly send laptops to North Korea or even China\u8221\'20\'1D, said \uc2 Adam Meyers\uc2 , head of counter-adversary operations for \uc2 CrowdStrike\uc2 , a cybersecurity firm.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey find somebody that is also looking for a gig-economy job, and they say, \u8216\'20\'18Hey, we are happy to get you $200 per laptop that you manage,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D said Meyers, whose team has published reports on the North Korean operation.\par \par \uc2 Chapman grew up in an abusive home and drifted \u8220\'20\'1Cbetween low-paying jobs and unstable housing\u8221\'20\'1D, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/documents/69cf2b07-4d43-4b2d-8298-1b1e089f4654.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to documents}}}\uc2  submitted by her attorneys. In 2020, she was also taking care of her mother, who had been diagnosed with renal cancer.\par \par \uc2 About six months after the \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  message, Chapman started running what law enforcement officials describe as \u8220\'20\'1Claptop farms\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In addition to hosting computers, she helped the North Koreans pose as US citizens by validating stolen identity information; sent some laptops abroad; logged into the computers so that the foreign workers could connect remotely; and received paychecks and transferred the money to the workers, according to court documents.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the North Koreans created fictitious personas and online profiles to match the job requirements for remote IT worker positions. They often got the jobs through staffing agencies.\par \par \uc2 In one case, a \u8220\'20\'1Ctop-five national television network and media company\u8221\'20\'1D headquartered in New York hired one of the North Koreans as a video-streaming engineer.\par \par \uc2 The person posing as \u8220\'20\'1CDaniel B\u8221\'20\'1D asked Chapman to join a \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  Teams meeting with the employer so that the co-conspirator could also join. The indictment does not list victims\u8217\'20\'19 full names.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI just typed in the name Daniel,\u8221\'20\'1D Chapman told the person in North Korea, according to court records of an online conversation. \u8220\'20\'1CIf they ask WHY you are using two devices, just say the microphone on your laptop doesn\u8217\'20\'19t work right.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COK,\u8221\'20\'1D the foreign actor responded.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost IT people are fine with that explanation,\u8221\'20\'1D Chapman replied.\par \par \uc2 Chapman was aware that her actions were illegal.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI hope you guys can find other people to do your physical I-9s. These are federal documents. I will SEND them for you, but have someone else do the paperwork. I can go to FEDERAL PRISON for falsifying federal documents,\u8221\'20\'1D Chapman wrote to a group of her co-conspirators.\par \par \uc2 Chapman was also active on social media. In a video posted in June 2023, she talked about having breakfast on the go because she was so busy, and her clients were \u8220\'20\'1Cgoing crazy!\u8221\'20\'1D, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wired.com/story/north-korea-stole-your-tech-job-ai-interviews/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wired reported}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Behind Chapman were racks with at least a dozen open laptops with sticky notes. In October 2023, federal investigators raided her home and found 90 laptops. In February this year, she pleaded guilty to conspiracy to commit wire fraud, aggravated identity theft and conspiracy to launder monetary instruments.\par \par \uc2 Over the three years that Chapman worked with the North Koreans, some of the employees received hundreds of thousands of dollars from a single company. In total, the scheme generated $17m for Chapman and the North Korean government.\par \par \uc2 The fraudsters also stole the identities of 68 people, who then also had false tax liabilities, according to the justice department.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to the court before her sentencing, Chapman thanked the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for arresting her because she had been \u8220\'20\'1Ctrying to get away from the guys that I was working with for awhile [sic] and I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t really sure how to do it\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe area where we lived didn\u8217\'20\'19t provide for a lot of job opportunities that fit what I needed,\u8221\'20\'1D Chapman wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CTo the people who were harmed, I send my sincerest apologies. I am not someone who seeks to harm anyone, so knowing that I was a part of a company that set out to harm people is devastating to me.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Last week, US district court judge Randolph Moss sentenced Chapman to more than eight years in prison; to forfeit $284,000 that was to be paid to the North Koreans, and to pay a fine of $176,000.\par \par \uc2 Chapman and her co-conspirators were not the only ones conducting such fraud. In January, the federal government also charged two people in North Korea, a Mexican citizen and two US citizens for a scheme that helped North Korean IT workers land jobs with at least 64 US companies and generated at least $866,000 in revenue, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/two-north-korean-nationals-and-three-facilitators-indicted-multi-year-fraudulent-remote"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 justice department}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Racenberg, of Nisos, said he expected cybercriminals to use artificial intelligence to \u8220\'20\'1Cget better and better\u8221\'20\'1D at performing such schemes.\par \par \uc2 Companies should conduct \u8220\'20\'1Copen-source research\u8221\'20\'1D on applicants because oftentimes the fraudsters reuse r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 content, Racenberg said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you put the first few lines of the r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 in, you might find two, three other r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9s online that are exactly the same with these very similar companies or similar dates,\u8221\'20\'1D Racenberg added. \u8220\'20\'1CThat should raise some flags.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 During an interview, if there is background noise that sounds like a call center or if the applicant refuses to remove a fake or blurred background, that could also be cause for concern, Meyers, of \uc2 CrowdStrike\uc2 , said.\par \par \uc2 And companies should ask new hires to visit the office to pick up their laptop rather than mail it to them because that allows the company to see if the person who shows up is the same one you interviewed, Racenberg said.\par \par \uc2 Five years after the pandemic, more companies have also started to require employees to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/nov/17/work-from-home-office-mandate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 return to the office}}}\uc2  at least part time. If all corporations did that, would it eliminate the threat?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to prevent all of this from happening, yes,\u8221\'20\'1D Racenberg said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut are we going to go back to that? Probably not.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250803el83001jp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
