\par\par \b \uc2 Wall Street falls as Trump tariffs and weak jobs report hit global markets \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden (now) and Adam Fulton (earlier) \par \uc2 6,408 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 August 2025\par 16:05 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Just 73,000 jobs created in July, and employment in May and June was 258,000 lower than previously reportedUS Politics Live: Trump is \u8216\'20\'18killing jobs and jacking up prices\u8217\'20\'19, Democrats say amid tariff fallout and weak employment growthUS adds 73,000 jobs in July amid economic pressure from Trump\u8217\'20\'19s trade warFull list of new tariff rates\par \par \uc2 4.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time to wrap up\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Global markets have been rattled by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/aug/01/trump-tariffs-live-updates-executive-order-us-trade-war-deals-latest-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-688cb3f48f08fd3663577e01#block-688cb3f48f08fd3663577e01"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a weak US jobs report which suggests Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s trade wars have hurt America\u8217\'20\'19s labor market}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The latest Non-Farm Payroll report showed that the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/useconomy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US economy}}}\uc2  added 73,000 jobs in July, far lower than expected, as the unemployment rate rose to 4.2% from 4.1% in June.\par \par \uc2 The Bureau of Labor also slashed the number of jobs added in recent months. May\u8217\'20\'19s jobs figure was revised down by 125,000, from 144,000 to 19,000, and June was revised down by 133,000, from 147,000 to 14,000 \u8211\'20\'13 a combined 258,000 fewer jobs than previously reported.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the full story:\par \par \uc2 The weak jobs report rocked Wall Street, where the Dow Jones industrial average is down 496 points, or 1.1%, at 43,634 points.\par \par \uc2 US bond yields also fell sharply, as traders raised their bets on cuts to US interest rates this year.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  repeated his demand for lower borrowing costs, claiming that Federal Reserve chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  was \u8220\'20\'1Ca stubborn MORON\u8221\'20\'1D for not acting faster.\par \par \uc2 Stocks have fallen {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/aug/01/trump-tariffs-live-updates-executive-order-us-trade-war-deals-latest-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-688cde588f083d65b8bf1ae0#block-688cde588f083d65b8bf1ae0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in London, and across Europe}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Investors were already anxious, after Trumpannounced new tariffs on dozens of US trading partners.\par \par \uc2 Last night, as the latest deadline to reach deals approached, the US president signed an executive order imposing tariffs ranging from 10% to 41%.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/aug/01/trump-tariffs-live-updates-executive-order-us-trade-war-deals-latest-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-688c0fd28f086217179dec3e#block-688c0fd28f086217179dec3e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rates were set at 25% for India, 20% for Taiwan and 30% for South Africa}}}\par \par \uc2 ahead of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s self-imposed deadline of 1 August for striking trade deals with countries worldwide.\par \par \uc2 Trump also extended the deadline for a tariff agreement with Mexico by another 90 days.\par \par \uc2 Our US Politics liveblog has full reaction to the jobs report, and the trade war:\par \par \uc2 4.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s been a heavy sell-off across European markets too, amid trade war fears and jitters about the US economy.\par \par \uc2 Germany\u8217\'20\'19s DAX was down around 2.5% as trading ended, with the situation in Paris even worse \u8211\'20\'13 France\u8217\'20\'19s CAC 40 has shed 2.85%.\par \par \uc2 Italy\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE MIB lost 2.55%.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 4.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 4.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100's lowest close in over a week\par \par \uc2 Tariff anxiety, and the weak US jobs report, have dragged London\u8217\'20\'19s stock market down to its lowest closing level in over a week.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 index of blue-chip shares has ended the day down 64 points, or -0.7%, at 9,068 points. That takes it away from record highs set yesterday, and is the lowest close since 23 July.\par \par \uc2 Banks were among those worst hit, pounded by expectations of interest rate cuts, along with some mining companies.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 4.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 4.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 This post from Harvard professor JasonFurman, who chaired \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Council of Economic Advisers, shows how US jobs creation has been weaker this year than in 2023 and 2024:\par \par \uc2 4.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 As well as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/aug/01/trump-tariffs-live-updates-executive-order-us-trade-war-deals-latest-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-688cb8e48f0871e4e6fe9ef4#block-688cb8e48f0871e4e6fe9ef4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 demanding interest rate cuts (again) today}}}\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  also called Fed chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Ca stubborn MORON\u8221\'20\'1D, and urged top \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials to seize control if Powell fails to cut borrowing costs.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/aug/01/trump-jerome-powell-tariffs-federal-reserve"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s }}}\uc2 the full story:\par \par \uc2 3.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 Stephen Miran, who chairs \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Council of Economic Advisors, has conceded that July\u8217\'20\'19s US jobs report \u8220\'20\'1Cisn\u8217\'20\'19t ideal\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But Miran also insists that better times are ahead.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to CNN, Miran suggests that more than half the downward revisions to employment growth in May and June (which cut employment totals by 258,000) were due to \u8220\'20\'1Cquirks of the seasonal adjustment process\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He also attributes some of the drop in employment to the fact the White House has \u8220\'20\'1Celiminated about a million jobs for foreign born workers\u8221\'20\'1D, according to a transcript provided by CNN.\par \par \uc2 Miran adds:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 3.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 3.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 A key measure of US job creation is its weakest since early in the Covid-19 pandemic, reports \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Lisa Abramowicz:\par \par \uc2 3.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 US manufacturers blame tariffs as activity falls\par \par \uc2 More bad news: economic activity in the US manufacturing sector contracted in July for the fifth month in a row.\par \par \uc2 The latest poll of purchasing managers at US factories, just released by the \uc2 Institute of Supply Management (ISM)\uc2 , shows that new orders and backlogs contracted in July, suggesting a weakening in demand.\par \par \uc2 Prices also rose \u8211\'20\'13 perhaps a sign of the impact of tariffs? \u8211\'20\'13 while both exports and imports contracted.\par \par \uc2 This pulled the \uc2 ISM\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2  down to 48% in July, from 49% in June.\par \par \uc2 Susan Spence, chair of the \uc2 ISM\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s manufacturing business survey committee:, says:\par \par \uc2 Some companies blamed the disruption and confusion caused by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s trade wars.\par \par \uc2 One, in the \u8220\'20\'1Capparel, leather and allied products\u8221\'20\'1D sector, said:\par \par \uc2 A second, in the electrical equipment, appliances and components sector, reported:\par \par \uc2 A third company, though, warned that higher interest rates were depressing the construction industry, while a fourth reported strong sales growth driven by datacenter construction.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 3.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 3.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 A cut to US interest rates in September looks \u8220\'20\'1Cincreasingly likely\u8221\'20\'1D, argues JamesKnightley, ING\u8217\'20\'19s chief international economist.\par \par \uc2 Knightley says:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 3.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 2.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 Some countries get reprieve in latest Trump tariffs\par \par \uc2 Lesotho isn\u8217\'20\'19t the only country to receive a reprieve from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As flagged earlier ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/aug/01/trump-tariffs-live-updates-executive-order-us-trade-war-deals-latest-news?page=with%3Ablock-688cabbd8f08fd3663577d65#block-688cabbd8f08fd3663577d65"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 see here}}}\uc2 ), Lesotho was facing 50% tariffs on 2 April, an existential threat to its textile industry, but came out on Friday with a 15% rate.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a relief for a state which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/czdnv6gpezjo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump said \u8220\'20\'1Cnobody has ever heard of\u8221\'20\'1D when he halted USAID earlier this year}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Also getting a drastic reduction in tariffs are Madagascar, down from 47% on 2 April to 15% on 1 August, and Botswana, down from 37% to 15%\par \par \uc2 Liechtenstein, the wealthy European state best known as a financial centre, has seen rates slashed from 37% to 15%, while the Falkland Islands have gone from 41% to 10%.\par \par \uc2 Cambodia went from 49% to 19%, while Iraq only got a four-point reduction, from 39% to 35%\par \par \uc2 Updated at 3.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 2.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 Wall Street opens with a bump\par \par \uc2 Ding ding goes the opening bell on the New York\par \par \uc2 Stock\par \par \uc2 Exchange \u8211\'20\'13 the signal for an early burst of selling!\par \par \uc2 The main US stock indices have fallen sharply at the start of trading, as investors react to the flurry of tariffs announced last night and today\u8217\'20\'19s weak US jobs report.\par \par \uc2 The DowJonesindustrialaverage (which contains 30 large US companies) has fallen by 1.1% at the start of trading, shedding 501 points to 43,629.\par \par \uc2 The broader S&P 500 index is down 1.2%, while the tech-focused Nasdaq has lost 1.5% \u8211\'20\'13 with \uc2 Amazon\uc2  falling almost 7% after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/jul/31/amazon-earnings-tariffs-future"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issuing disappointing forecasts last night}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s weak payroll report has punctured the narrative of a resilient US economy, says \uc2 Kathleen Brooks\uc2 , research director at XTB, explaining:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 2.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 Most of the 73,000 jobs created across the US economy in July were in healthcare.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll report shows that healthcare added 55,000 jobs in July, above the average monthly gain of 42,000 over the prior 12 months. That includes 34,000 in \u8220\'20\'1Cambulatory health care services\u8221\'20\'1D and 16,000 at hospitals.\par \par \uc2 Social assistance employment rose by 18,000.\par \par \uc2 But on the other side of the ledger, federal government employment levels fell by 12,000 in July and are down by 84,000 since reaching a peak in January.\par \par \uc2 That won\u8217\'20\'19t give a full insight into Doge-related job cuts, though, as employees on paid leave or receiving ongoing severance pay are counted as employed.\par \par \uc2 There was little change in employment levels in other major industries, including mining, quarrying, and oil and gas extraction; construction; manufacturing; wholesale trade; retail trade; transportation and warehousing; information; financial activities; professional and business services; leisure and hospitality; and other services, the Bureau for Labor Statistics adds.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 2.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 US jobs report: what the experts say\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payrolls report does not paint a pretty picture, points out Nicholas Hyett, investment manager at investment service Wealth Club:\par \par \uc2 Seema Shah\uc2 , chief global strategist at \uc2 Principal Asset Management\uc2 , fears the US jobs market will keep weakening:\par \par \uc2 Thomas\par \par \uc2 Ryan, North America economist, agrees that today\u8217\'20\'19s weak jobs report will embolden the dovish policymakers at the Federal Reserve who want to cut interest rates, saying:\par \par \uc2 [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jul/30/federal-reserve-interest-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Reminder, the Fed left rates on hold on Wednesday, in a split vote}}}\uc2 .]\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 2.06pm BST\par \par \uc2 This chart shows just how bad the last three months have been for job creation in the US:\par \par \uc2 2.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 Trump demands interest rate cut\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has again repeated his plea for the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  to cut interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after today\u8217\'20\'19s weak jobs report was released, Trump posted on his Truth Social site:\par \par \uc2 [Factcheck: tariffs are paid by the company or individual importing goods into the US. They only way they can \u8220\'20\'1Cbring in dollars\u8221\'20\'1D is if the exporting company lowers its prices, to keep its goods competitive, meaning the importer doesn\u8217\'20\'19t suffer a relative loss once it has paid the tariff.]\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 1.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 US dollar slides after weak jobs report\par \par \uc2 The US dollar is being hammered on the foreign exchange markets, as today\u8217\'20\'19s weak jobs report alarms traders.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, which tracks the greenback against a basket of currencies, is now down 0.9% today.\par \par \uc2 The euro has surged by almost a cent and a half, to $1.154, while the pound is up three-quarters of a cent at $1.328.\par \par \uc2 1.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s snap reaction from HeatherLong, chief economist at credit union NavyFederal:\par \par \uc2 1.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s surprisingly weak US jobs report is causing ructions in the market.\par \par \uc2 The yields, or interest rates, on \uc2 US government\uc2  bonds are falling sharply \u8211\'20\'13 a sign that investors are piling into bonds because they expect cuts to US interest rates to support the economy, or are simply seeking a safe-haven asset.\par \par \uc2 Reuters has the details:\par \par \uc2 UK government\uc2  bond yields \u8211\'20\'13 which track the cost of Britain\u8217\'20\'19s government borrowing \u8211\'20\'13 have also dropped.\par \par \uc2 1.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 US job creation revised sharply lower\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: Employment growth across America has been much weaker than previously thought over the last three months \u8211\'20\'13 a sign that the US labor market may be cooling.\par \par \uc2 The latest non-farm payroll, just released, shows that US employment rose by just 73,000 in July, rather weaker than the 110,000 new jobs expected.\par \par \uc2 But the big shock comes in the latest revisions to payrolls, with previous estimates for both May and June being revised sharply lower.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  now estimates that just 19,000 new jobs were created in May, 125,000 fewer than the 144,000 previously estimated.\par \par \uc2 June\u8217\'20\'19s data has been revised down too - showing that just 14,000 new jobs were created, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jul/03/us-economy-jobs-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 not the 147,000 reported a month ago}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That means 258,000 fewer jobs were created in May and June than previously thought.\par \par \uc2 The US unemployment rate has risen to 4.2% from 4.1% in June.\par \par \uc2 This surprisingly weak data may be a sign that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s trade wars, and the associated uncertainty, have cause more damage to the US economy than previously thought. There could also be an impact from cost-cutting DOGE program pushed by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BLS\uc2  says:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 1.19pm BST\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s tariff playbook is becoming clearer following last night\u8217\'20\'19s announcement, says PaulDiggle, chief economist at AberdeenInvestments.\par \par \uc2 This playbook consists of:\par \par \uc2 * A 10% global baseline tariff\par \par \uc2 * A rate of at least 15% on countries with goods trade deficits with the US\par \par \uc2 * An additional 40% on goods transhipped to evade higher rates elsewhere\par \par \uc2 * A variety of new country-specific rates on smaller trading partners, such as 20% on Taiwan or 39% on Switzerland\par \par \uc2 * Trump has also announced a 35% tariff on Canada, although the carve-out for USMCA-compliant trade remains [that\u8217\'20\'19s the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ustr.gov/trade-agreements/free-trade-agreements/united-states-mexico-canada-agreement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 United States-Mexico-Canada Agreement}}}\uc2 ]\par \par \uc2 Diggle adds that there are still plenty of uncertainties, including where US-China tariffs settle\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 1.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 Last night\u8217\'20\'19s tariff announcement contained some good news for the African country of Lesotho.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has lowered Lesotho\u8217\'20\'19s tariff rate to 15%, having previously threatened it with a 50% rate.\par \par \uc2 That should make it easier for manufacturers in Lesotho \u8211\'20\'13 which last monthdeclared a national state of disaster over the country\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Chigh rates of youth unemployment and job losses\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 to sell their goods to American consumers.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cgeqe09wnn8o"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The BBC reported earlier this week}}}\uc2  that a Lesotho garment factory, which has produced Trump-branded golf shirts, was at risk of shutting down due to the high tariffs\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 12.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 A summary\par \par \uc2 A quick recap.\par \par \uc2 Stock markets in Europe and across Asia-Pacific countries have fallen after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  announced new tariffs on dozens of US trading partners.\par \par \uc2 Last night, as the latest deadline to reach deals approached, Trump signed an executive order imposing tariffs ranging from 10% to 41%.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/aug/01/trump-tariffs-live-updates-executive-order-us-trade-war-deals-latest-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-688c0fd28f086217179dec3e#block-688c0fd28f086217179dec3e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rates were set at 25% for India, 20% for Taiwan and 30% for South Africa}}}\par \par \uc2 ahead of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s self-imposed deadline of 1 August for striking trade deals with countries worldwide.\par \par \uc2 Trump also extended the deadline for a tariff agreement with Mexico by another 90 days.\par \par \uc2 Share prices have weakened in response \u8211\'20\'13 with Germany\u8217\'20\'19s DAX down 1.9% and France\u8217\'20\'19s CAC losing 2.2%, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/aug/01/trump-tariffs-live-updates-executive-order-us-trade-war-deals-latest-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-688ca0048f0871e4e6fe9d1b#block-688ca0048f0871e4e6fe9d1b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 European stock markets fell to a one-month low}}}\uc2 .\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/aug/01/trump-tariffs-live-updates-executive-order-us-trade-war-deals-latest-news?page=with%3Ablock-688c57e18f086217179dedec#block-688c57e18f086217179dedec"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Asia-Pacific stock markets were on track for their worst week since April}}}\uc2 , with Japan\u8217\'20\'19s Nikkei225 losing 0.6%,\par \par \uc2 South Africa\u8217\'20\'19s stock market is now down almost 1.5%.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/aug/01/trump-tariffs-live-updates-executive-order-us-trade-war-deals-latest-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-688c486c8f086217179ded99#block-688c486c8f086217179ded99"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Canada\u8217\'20\'19s prime minister, Mark Carney, said he was disappointed that Donald Trump was raising its tariffs from 25% to 35%}}}\uc2 .\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/aug/01/trump-tariffs-live-updates-executive-order-us-trade-war-deals-latest-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-688c94e08f086217179df06a#block-688c94e08f086217179df06a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 And there was shock in Switzerland, which has been lumbered with a 39% tariff rate}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 which manufacturers fear will lead to job losses.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 12.11pm BST\par \par \uc2 European selloff deepens\par \par \uc2 The European stock market selloff is gathering pace.\par \par \uc2 After a volatile morning, Germany\u8217\'20\'19s DAX index and Italy\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE MIB are both down 1.9% while France\u8217\'20\'19s CAC has lost 2.3%.\par \par \uc2 Joshua Mahony, chief market analyst at financial services group Rostro, reports that sentiment in the markets has \u8216\'20\'18soured\u8217\'20\'19 after last night\u8217\'20\'19s tariff announcements from the White House.\par \par \uc2 12.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 Many companies will be confused about how the new US tariffs apply to them, warns Andrew Wilson, deputy secretary-general of the \uc2 International Chamber of Commerce (ICC)\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Wilson explains:\par \par \uc2 As an example, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/07/further-modifying-the-reciprocal-tariff-rates/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the information released by the White House last night }}}\uc2 showed that Brazil now faced a tariff of 10%. However, on Wednesday Trump signed an executive order confirming that the US would impose 50% tariffs on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/brazil"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brazil}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And as my colleague Lisa O\u8217\'20\'19Carroll explained {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/aug/01/trump-tariffs-live-updates-executive-order-us-trade-war-deals-latest-news?page=with%3Ablock-688c75268f086217179deeb2#block-688c75268f086217179deeb2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in her earlier post}}}\uc2 , the \uc2 European Union\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s trade deal with the US (a 15% tariff, agreed last weekend) is not stacked on top of a pre-existing tariff, meaning it\u8217\'20\'19s a better deal than many other countries.\par \par \uc2 11.47am BST\par \par \uc2 The US dollar has hit its highest level in two months against a basket of currencies today.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index has risen by 0.1%, on track for its seventh daily rise in a row.\par \par \uc2 Traders may be calculating that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariffs will be inflationary, pushing prices paid by consumers and making it harder for the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  to lower interest rates. They could also be factoring in the damage that the trade war will do to other countries, which could force their central banks to keep interest rates lower.\par \par \uc2 11.30am BST\par \par \uc2 Job loss fears over Swiss tariffs\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision to hit Switzerland with a 39% tariff rate has caused shock in the markets, and in the Swiss Confederation.\par \par \uc2 Swiss president Karin Keller-Sutter posted on X last night that she spoke to DonaldTrump on Thursday, but that the two country\u8217\'20\'19s hadn\u8217\'20\'19t reached an agreement.\par \par \uc2 She explained:\par \par \uc2 The Swiss government says ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/aug/01/trump-tariffs-live-updates-executive-order-us-trade-war-deals-latest-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-688c56418f086217179dede0#block-688c56418f086217179dede0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 see earlier post}}}\uc2 ) it \u8220\'20\'1Ccontinues to strive for a negotiated solution\u8221\'20\'1D over a tariff announcement which it \u8220\'20\'1Cnotes with regret\u8221\'20\'1D; manufacturers are warning that jobs are at risk.\par \par \uc2 Stefan Brupbacher, director of manufacturers\u8217\'20\'19 association \uc2 Swissmem\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 Swissmechanic, an industry lobby group, called the 39% rate \u8220\'20\'1Cdangerous.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It urged the government to keep negotiating in the remaining time until the tariffs are set to come into effect on 7 August, which otherwise risks leaving the economy becoming \u8220\'20\'1Cone of the few countries that permanently struggle with structural competitive disadvantages\u8221\'20\'1D, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 As flagged earlier, shares in \uc2 Watches of Switzerland\uc2  have fallen sharply in London \u8211\'20\'13 they\u8217\'20\'19re now down almost 9%.\par \par \uc2 Kathleen Brooks\uc2 , research director at XTB, says Switzerland got the rough end of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s trade war, explaining:\par \par \uc2 11.06am BST\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s selloff has dragged European stock markets down to a one-month low.\par \par \uc2 The pan-European Stoxx600 index is now down 1.1% at its lowest level since late June.\par \par \uc2 European pharmaceuticals stocks have dropped after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wrote to executives at 17 companies on Thursday, demanding they match their US prices for prescription drugs with the lowest price offered in other developed nations.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 11.13am BST\par \par \uc2 10.55am BST\par \par \uc2 Taiwan\u8217\'20\'19s government is hoping that it can negotiate its new US tariff down, before the new deadline of 7 August.\par \par \uc2 Taiwanese president Lai Ching-te said today that the new 20% tariff rate set by the Trump administration on goods imported from the island is \u8220\'20\'1Ctemporary\u8221\'20\'1D, and the government expects to negotiate a lower figure.\par \par \uc2 Lai also noted that rates for semiconductors, electronics as well as information and communication technology will be subject to separate U.S. sectoral tariffs and are still to be worked out.\par \par \uc2 Lai told a press briefing:\par \par \uc2 10.12am BST\par \par \uc2 French wine exporters see \u8364\'20\'AC1bn losses from Trump tariff\par \par \uc2 France\u8217\'20\'19s wine and spirits industry expects to lose \u8364\'20\'AC1bn should the US go ahead with imposing a 15% import tariff on their products next week, as the \uc2 European Union\uc2  makes a last-ditch effort to obtain an exemption, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 The levy, due to come into force on 7 August, will likely cut a quarter of France\u8217\'20\'19s annual exports from the sector, the country\u8217\'20\'19s federation of wine and spirit exporters FEVS said in a statement on Friday.\par \par \uc2 It will also jeopardize the jobs of the 600,000 people directly employed by the industry, they fear.\par \par \uc2 FEVS President Gabriel Picard said:\par \par \uc2 9.55am BST\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariffs are a huge blow to global commerce, warns Atakan Bakiskan, US economist at Berenberg bank.\par \par \uc2 Bakiskan\u8217\'20\'19s verdict is that that the situation is bad, but could have been even worse, explaining:\par \par \uc2 9.40am BST\par \par \uc2 The US trade war has been hampering UK and eurozone manufacturers, new data shows.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pmi.spglobal.com/Public/Home/PressRelease/11c24cf457044d5bbf797425e3b780b5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The latest poll of purchasing managers at British factories}}}\uc2 , just released, highlights that new orders at UK manufacturers fell in July.\par \par \uc2 Data provider S&P Global says:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pmi.spglobal.com/Public/Home/PressRelease/85ffe3c6cf5d41399a2ccbca9721ddb9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A survey of eurozone factories}}}\uc2 , also released this morning, found that their supply chains remained strained in July, with delivery times lengthening.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Cyrus de la Rubia, chief economist at HamburgCommercialBank, explains:\par \par \uc2 9.17am BST\par \par \uc2 Capital Economics: New US tariff regime still not the end of the story\par \par \uc2 President Trump\u8217\'20\'19s latest flurry of tariffs implies that the US effective tariff rate will rise to about 18%, from 2.3% last year, reports StephenBrown, deputy chief North America economist at City consultancy Capital Economics.\par \par \uc2 Brown\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.capitaleconomics.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told clients this morning}}}\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 Updated at 9.17am BST\par \par \uc2 9.06am BST\par \par \uc2 The headline 15% tariff rate applying to most EU goods is better than most countries as it is not in addition, or stacked on top of, a pre-existing tariff, say experts.\par \par \uc2 That was the snap analysis of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s tariff list by DavidHenig, director at the European Centre for International Political Economy.\par \par \uc2 He posted on social media:\par \par \uc2 The EU says its pre-Trump average tariff was 4.8%, arguing therefore, that the 15%, is closer to others who have secured a 10% baseline tariff.\par \par \uc2 The UK\u8217\'20\'19s 10% tariff is stacked on top of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/graphics/USA-TRUMP/TARIFFS/jnpwjmqklvw/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the average most favoured nation tariff rate of 3.7%}}}\uc2  that applied before Trump was elected, bringing the post-Trump average to 13.7% tariff.\par \par \uc2 By contrast the EU\u8217\'20\'19s 15% is inclusive of the pre-Trump MFN of 4.8%.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 11.46am BST\par \par \uc2 9.04am BST\par \par \uc2 The US stock market is set to fall when trading begins in New York in five and a half hours.\par \par \uc2 The futures market indictates that the S&P 500 \u8211\'20\'13 the broad index of US stocks \u8211\'20\'13 is on track to fall around 0.85%.\par \par \uc2 The futures contract for the tech-focused Nasdaq index is down 0.87%, while the DowJonesIndustrialAverage (which tracks 30 large US companies) futures is down 0.9%.\par \par \uc2 8.46am BST\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 Watches of Switzerland\uc2 , the London-listed timepiece retailer, have fallen by over 5% after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  hit Swiss imports to the US with a 39% tariff.\par \par \uc2 Traders will be calculating that \uc2 Watches of Switzerland\uc2  will either suffer weaker US sales (American customers will pay the tariff, so its prices will be less competitive), or be forced to cut its prices in response (hitting its profits).\par \par \uc2 8.36am BST\par \par \uc2 The Falkland Islands is the only trading partner apart from the UK that is specified in the White House list as having a 10% tariff rate on its exports to the US.\par \par \uc2 It is one of 14 British overseas territories and its top export to the US is non-fillet frozen fish.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://oec.world/en/profile/bilateral-country/usa/partner/flk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 According to the Observatory of Economic Complexity, it sold just under $26m (\u163\'00\'A319.6m) of the fish to the US in 2023}}}\uc2 , accounting for 96% of its $27.4m sales to the US in 2023.\par \par \uc2 The order states that goods imported from every nation on Earth will be subject to a 10% tariff except for goods from the 92 countries listed in an annex that are subject to higher tariff rates.\par \par \uc2 Australia, which is not listed in the annex, said it assumed that its tariff was 10%.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.59am BST\par \par \uc2 8.30am BST\par \par \uc2 South Africa's rand hits two-month low after US sets 30% tariff\par \par \uc2 South Africa\u8217\'20\'19s financial markets have been rattled by Trump\u8217\'20\'19s decision to impose a 30% tariff on its exports to the US.\par \par \uc2 South Africa\u8217\'20\'19s JSEFTSE all share index has fallen by 1.2% in morning trading, with \u8216\'20\'18consumer cyclicals\u8217\'20\'19 the worst-performing sector.\par \par \uc2 The South African rand is on the backfoot this morning too. It dipped to a two-month low of 18.24 against the US dollar, its lowest level since mid-May.\par \par \uc2 8.17am BST\par \par \uc2 European stock markets fall\par \par \uc2 Stock markets across Europe have dropped, after{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/31/trump-extends-deadline-tariff-deal-mexico"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Donald Trump intensified his trade war last night.}}}\par \par \uc2 Germany\u8217\'20\'19s DAX index has dropped by 1.1% at the start of trading in Frankfurt, while France\u8217\'20\'19s CAC fell by almost 1% and Spain\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 IBEX\uc2  lost 0.6% \u8211\'20\'13 even though Europe reached a trade deal with the US at the start of this week.\par \par \uc2 That, and the 0.5% drop on the London stock market ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/aug/01/trump-tariffs-live-updates-executive-order-us-trade-war-deals-latest-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-688c66688f086217179dee4b#block-688c66688f086217179dee4b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 see here}}}\uc2 ), shows concerns that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariffs will weigh on the global economy, weakening trade growth.\par \par \uc2 8.05am BST\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100 opens lower\par \par \uc2 London\u8217\'20\'19s stock market has opened in the red, as the City digests{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/aug/01/full-list-tariffs-country-rate-donald-trump-executive-order"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s swathe of tariffs on trading partners.}}}\par \par \uc2 The FTSE100 index of blue-chip shares has dropped by over 0.5%, down 50 points at 9082 points.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a fairly mild drop, taking the \u8216\'20\'18Footsie\u8217\'20\'19 away from the record high set yesterday, following the modest losses in Asia-Pacific markets earlier.\par \par \uc2 Tony\par \par \uc2 Sycamore, market analyst at IG, explains:\par \par \uc2 7.55am BST\par \par \uc2 In the currency markets, the Swiss franc has dipped against the US dollar after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  imposed a 39% tariff on imports from Switzerland.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc is down 0.15% at 0.813 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 7.43am BST\par \par \uc2 Trump tariffs: What the analysts say\par \par \uc2 Financial experts are digesting the new tariff rates announced by the US last night.\par \par \uc2 Jim Reid, market strategist at DeutscheBank, points out that the effective US tariff rate is the highest since the 1930s, when the protectionist Smoot\u8211\'20\'13Hawley Tariff Act was in place, exacerbating the Great Depression.\par \par \uc2 Reid explained:\par \par \uc2 Michael Brown, senior research strategist at brokerage Pepperstone, points out that the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  failed to secure many trade deals during the last few months:\par \par \uc2 7.30am BST\par \par \uc2 Switzerland 'continues to strive for a negotiated solution'\par \par \uc2 Switzerland\u8217\'20\'19s government has said it \u8220\'20\'1Cnotes with great regret\u8221\'20\'1D that the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/aug/01/trump-tariffs-live-updates-executive-order-us-trade-war-deals-latest-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-688c0fd28f086217179dec3e#block-688c0fd28f086217179dec3e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 White House has announced a higher 39% tariff on Swiss imports}}}\uc2  to the United States.\par \par \uc2 In a post on X, the Swiss Federal Council has said \u8220\'20\'1CSwitzerland has been and continues to be in contact with the responsible authorities in the US,\u8221\'20\'1D adding that it \u8220\'20\'1Ccontinues to strive for a negotiated solution\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The government said it would analyse the new situation and decide on how to proceed.\par \par \uc2 7.23am BST\par \par \uc2 Asia-Pacific stock markets are on track for their worst week since April (the last time \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  announced a flurry of tariffs).\par \par \uc2 MSCI\u8217\'20\'19s broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan has fallen 1.1% today, Reuters reports, bringing its total loss this week to 2.2%. That would be its biggest fall since the week ending on 11 April, after Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18Liberation Day\u8217\'20\'19 tariff announcement at the start of April.\par \par \uc2 Most markets across Asia are still showing losses today.\par \par \uc2 South Korea\u8217\'20\'19s KOSPI is leading the fallers, down 4%, after Seoul\u8217\'20\'19s government rolled out plans to raise taxes on investors and companies.\par \par \uc2 China\u8217\'20\'19s CSI300 share index is down 0.75%, even though {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2025/07/31/treasury-secretary-bessent-says-we-have-the-makings-of-a-deal-with-china.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said yesterday}}}\uc2  that Washington and Beijing \u8220\'20\'1Chave the makings of a deal\u8221\'20\'1D, although some details still need to be worked out.\par \par \uc2 6.54am BST\par \par \uc2 Norway still aims for US trade agreement\par \par \uc2 Across Europe, politicians are digesting the latest escalation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s trade war.\par \par \uc2 Norwegian Minister of Trade and Industry CecilieMyrseth told public broadcaster NRK this morning that Norway continues to seek a trade agreement with the US, after the White House announced a 15% tariff on its exports.\par \par \uc2 6.32am BST\par \par \uc2 Carney says Canada accounts for only 1% of US fentanyl imports\par \par \uc2 Further to \uc2 Mark Carney\uc2  saying Canada is \u8220\'20\'1Cdisappointed\u8221\'20\'1D at Trump\u8217\'20\'19s 35% tariff on Canadian goods, the prime minister said the \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cwill act to project Canadian jobs, invest in our industrial competitiveness, buy Canadian and diversity our export markets\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Carney said parts of Canada\u8217\'20\'19s economy including lumber, steel, aluminium and automobiles were heavily impacted by American tariffs but despite the steep levy on US-bound Canadian goods outside of the US-Mexico-Canada trade agreement, Canada remained committed to the deal.\par \par \uc2 Carney said in his statement that the US had justified its 35% rate on the basis of the cross-border flow of fentanyl, \u8220\'20\'1Cdespite the fact that Canada accounts for only 1% of US fentanyl imports and has been working intensively to further reduce these volumes\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Carney said Canada was also working internally to \u8220\'20\'1Ccut down trade barriers to build one Canadian economy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s Carney\u8217\'20\'19s statement via {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bsky.app/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bluesky}}}\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.04am BST\par \par \uc2 5.59am BST\par \par \uc2 Mark Carney\uc2  'disappointed' at 35% Trump tariffs on Canada\par \par \uc2 Canadian prime minister \uc2 Mark Carney\uc2  has said he is disappointed at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  raising tariffs from 25% to 35% on Canadian goods outside of the US-Mexico-Canada trade agreement.\par \par \uc2 More on Carney\u8217\'20\'19s statement in a moment.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 6.11am BST\par \par \uc2 5.48am BST\par \par \uc2 Just to recap, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  signed an executive order on Thursday that would have new tariffs on a large array of US trading partners go into effect in seven days \u8211\'20\'13 the next step in his trade agenda that will test the global economy and alliances.\par \par \uc2 The order was issued shortly after 7pm, the Associated Press reports, and came after a flurry of tariff-related activity in recent days, as the White House announced agreements with various nations and blocs ahead of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s self-imposed deadline of 1 August.\par \par \uc2 Also on Thursday, Trump announced that he would extend trade negotiations with Mexico for 90 days.\par \par \uc2 But the vast majority of nations are continuing to face uncertainty ahead of the coming deadline. And while a handful of trade deals have also trickled in, many details remain hazy \u8211\'20\'13 with businesses and manufacturers around the world bracing for heightened operating costs and potential price hikes regardless.\par \par \uc2 5.31am BST\par \par \uc2 In Canada, the mayor of an Ontario city has expressed defiance over the 35% tariffs \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has imposed on the country and called for people to \u8220\'20\'1Cbuy nothing\u8221\'20\'1D from the US.\par \par \uc2 Carolyn Parrish, mayor of the city of Mississauga, near Toronto, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/carolynhparrish/status/1951073229004489166"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posted on X}}}\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 Trump on Thursday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/31/trump-canada-tariffs-order"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increased tariffs from 25% to 35%}}}\uc2  on all products not covered by the US-Mexico-Canada trade agreement.\par \par \uc2 As {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/aug/01/trump-tariffs-live-updates-executive-order-us-trade-war-deals-latest-news?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-688c24db8f08fd3663577822#block-688c24db8f08fd3663577822"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported earlier}}}\uc2 , the president told NBC News on Thursday he was open to further discussions with Canada, adding that he may even speak with Canadian PM \uc2 Mark Carney\uc2  later in the night.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.39am BST\par \par \uc2 5.12am BST\par \par \uc2 Most Asian currencies slipped to multi-month lows on Friday, with South Korea\u8217\'20\'19s won and Malaysia\u8217\'20\'19s ringgit leading declines, as investors fled riskier regional assets after the US\u8217\'20\'19s sweeping new tariffs.\par \par \uc2 The won bore the brunt of the selloff, tumbling 0.62% to a two-month low of 1,400.6 against the US dollar, while the ringgit shed 0.5% to hit its weakest level since 23 June 23, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 The broad-based retreat extended across the region, with the Philippine peso, Taiwan dollar and Thai baht all declining more than 0.3% as the tariff fallout rippled through Asian markets.\par \par \uc2 The MSCI emerging market currency gauge has already fallen well over 1% so far this week, snapping in July from a six-month rally. It fell over 0.3% on Friday.\par \par \uc2 4.59am BST\par \par \uc2 Malaysia says its revised US tariff rate has been achieved without compromising the nation\u8217\'20\'19s sovereign rights after it stood firm on various \u8220\'20\'1Cred line\u8221\'20\'1D issues.\par \par \uc2 Malaysia\u8217\'20\'19s trade ministry said on Friday that the positive outcome of the US tariff talks followed sustained engagement between both governments and was a significant achievement of Malaysia\u8217\'20\'19s thorough and methodical negotiating process, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 The US imposed a 19% tariff rate on Malaysia. The ministry said:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.01am BST\par \par \uc2 4.45am BST\par \par \uc2 Across the Tasman Sea, New Zealand was hit with 15% tariffs.\par \par \uc2 The country\u8217\'20\'19s trade minister \uc2 Todd McClay\uc2  said he was hoping to have talks with his US counterparts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe first step will be to talk to them directly. And we\u8217\'20\'19ve engaged in a lot. In fact, it\u8217\'20\'19s been very good engagement,\u8221\'20\'1D he told \uc2 Radio New Zealand\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Local news outlet {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.stuff.co.nz/business/360777940/nz-exporters-slapped-15-us-tariff"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Stuff.com}}}\uc2  reported that the opposition Labour party\u8217\'20\'19s trade spokesperson, \uc2 Damien O\u8217\'20\'19Connor\uc2 , said the new tariff rate was a \u8220\'20\'1Cslap in the face\u8221\'20\'1D for exporters and could lead to higher costs at home.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 4.53am BST\par \par \uc2 4.31am BST\par \par \uc2 There was good news for Australia in the announcements from Trump \u8211\'20\'13 in that it was not mentioned at all.\par \par \uc2 Guardian Australia understands the \uc2 US government\uc2  confirmed to the Australian embassy in Washington DC that the baseline 10% tariff rate would remain in place.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe White House has confirmed that no country has reciprocal tariffs lower than Australia,\u8221\'20\'1D a spokesperson for Australia\u8217\'20\'19s minister for trade, Don Farrell, said on Friday morning.\par \par \uc2 Get the view from Australia here:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 4.53am BST\par \par \uc2 4.25am BST\par \par \uc2 Amy Hawkins, our senior China correspondent, has been reporting on the impact of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s trade war on China\u8217\'20\'19s fast-fashion capital Guangzhou. There, she found millions of workers toiling day and night in informal workshops to produce cheap garments for export. Business was slow, she reports:\par \par \uc2 Read the full story here:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 4.42am BST\par \par \uc2 4.14am BST\par \par \uc2 More on Cambodia now \u8211\'20\'13 deputy prime minister Sun Chanthol has thanked DonaldTrump for his understanding in Cambodia\u8217\'20\'19s negotiations to reduce a tariff rate to 19% after initially being set at 49% then later 36% \u8211\'20\'13 among the world\u8217\'20\'19s highest levies, Reuters reporting.\par \par \uc2 The news agency quoted Chanthol \u8211\'20\'13 Cambodia\u8217\'20\'19s top trade negotiator \u8211\'20\'13 as saying in an interview: \u8220\'20\'1CIf the US maintained 49% or 36%, that industry would collapse in my opinion,\u8221\'20\'1D referring to the garment and footwear manufacturing sector, the biggest economic driver in the country of 17.6 million people.\par \par \uc2 Cambodia has a big trade surplus with the US, with its exports to the American market accounting for 37.9% of its total shipments in 2024, valued at close to $10bn, according to official data.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 4.15am BST\par \par \uc2 3.59am BST\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee was expected to open slightly weaker on Friday\par \par \uc2 amid worries over the impact of the steep US tariffs on Indian exports and persistent portfolio outflows.\par \par \uc2 Reuters reports the one-month non-deliverable forward indicated the rupee would open in the 87.65-87.70 range versus the US dollar, compared with 87.5950 in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 The rupee declined about 2% in July, with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s threat of a 25% levy on Indian goods \u8211\'20\'13 alongside an unspecified penalty \u8211\'20\'13 pushing it closer to its all-time low of 87.95.\par \par \uc2 Economists estimate the 25% tariff announced could shave off India\u8217\'20\'19s 2025-26 growth by up to 40 basis points, but analysts and investors also believe India may be able to achieve a lower rate via negotiations.\par \par \uc2 3.50am BST\par \par \uc2 Cambodia\u8217\'20\'19s deputy prime minster has said the new 19% tariff rate protects its garment manufacturing industry with its 1 million workers and allows the country to be competitive with its peers.\par \par \uc2 Reuters quotes Sun Chanthol as saying the US\u8217\'20\'19s previous tariff rate \u8211\'20\'13 set up to 49% \u8211\'20\'13 would have caused the industry to collapse.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 3.54am BST\par \par \uc2 3.38am BST\par \par \uc2 Asian stocks tumble amid new Trump tariffs\par \par \uc2 Shares in Asia fell on Friday after the US hit dozens of trading partners with high tariffs.\par \par \uc2 MSCI\u8217\'20\'19s broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan fell 0.7%, bringing the total loss this week to 1.8%. South Korea\u8217\'20\'19s KOSPI plunged 3% while Taiwanese shares fell 0.9%, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 Japan\u8217\'20\'19s Nikkei dropped 0.4%. Chinese blue chips were flat and Hong Kong\u8217\'20\'19s Hang Seng index eked out a small gain of 0.2%.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt this point, the reaction in markets has been modest, and I think part of the reason for that is the recent trade deals with the EU, Japan, and South Korea have certainly helped to cushion the impact,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tony Sycamore, an analyst at IG.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 4.44am BST\par \par \uc2 3.29am BST\par \par \uc2 The tariff list - in full\par \par \uc2 Here is a searchable list of the latest reciprocal tariffs announced by the White House:\par \par \uc2 3.25am BST\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said his new steep tariffs are going \u8220\'20\'1Cvery well, very smooth\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 but that he\u8217\'20\'19s open to more deals.\par \par \uc2 The US president also told NBC News he was open to further discussions with Canada, adding that he may even speak with Canadian prime minister \uc2 Mark Carney\uc2  later in the night, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 Trump signed an executive on Thursday order raising tariffs on Canadian goods to 35% from 25% on all products not covered by the US-Mexico-Canada trade agreement.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 4.44am BST\par \par \uc2 3.19am BST\par \par \uc2 Opening summary\par \par \uc2 Welcome to our live coverage of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s sweeping tariff regime.\par \par \uc2 The US president signed an executive order on Thursday imposing reciprocal tariffs from 10% to 41% on US imports from dozens of countries and foreign locations. Rates were set at 25% for India, 20% for Taiwan and 30% for South Africa ahead of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s self-imposed deadline of 1 August for striking trade deals with countries worldwide.\par \par \uc2 He extended the deadline for a tariff agreement with Mexico by another 90 days.\par \par \uc2 Brazil\u8217\'20\'19s tariff rate was set at 10%, but a previous order signed by Trump placed a 40% tariff on some Brazilian goods, to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/30/us-brazil-lula-trump-tariffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 punish the country for prosecuting its former president}}}\uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  over an alleged coup attempt after the 2022 election.\par \par \uc2 In other key news:\par \par \uc2 * Canadian imports will face tariffs of 35%, not the current 25%, the White House announced. Trump had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/jul/31/trump-canada-trade-deal-carney-palestinian-statehood"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 threatened on Wednesday}}}\uc2  that Ottawa\u8217\'20\'19s move to recognise a Palestinian state would make agreeing a trade deal \u8220\'20\'1Cvery hard\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * Some of the world\u8217\'20\'19s poorest and most war-torn countries were hit with punitive rates, including Syria, which faces a levy of 41%; Laos and Myanmar with rates of 40%; Libya with a rate of 30%; Iraq with 35% and Sri Lanka with 20%. Switzerland faces a rate of 39%. The rates are set to go into effect in seven days, according to the order.\par \par \uc2 * Thailand\u8217\'20\'19s finance minister said on Friday that a 19% tariff rate had been agreed \u8211\'20\'13 significantly lower than the 36% level announced in April and better aligned with other countries in the region. Vietnam and Indonesia reportedly negotiated tariffs of 20% and 19% respectively.\par \par \uc2 * China faces a separate deadline for its higher tariffs of 12 August, with an extension {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/29/us-and-china-poised-to-extend-tariff-truce-after-failing-to-find-resolution-at-talks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to the truce agreed in principle}}}\uc2  but yet to be approved by the White House.\par \par \uc2 * By 31 July just eight countries or economic blocs had reached formal agreements with the White House: the UK, Vietnam, Indonesia, Philippines, South Korea, Japan, Pakistan and the EU.\par \par \uc2 \u8211\'20\'13 With {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/helen-livingstone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Helen Livingstone}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/lisaocarroll"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lisa O\u8217\'20\'19Carroll}}}\uc2  and agencies\par \par \uc2 Updated at 3.28am BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250801el81000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
