\par\par \b \uc2 Low-wage workers reeling over Trump\u8217\'20\'19s looming Snap cuts as food prices rise\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,159 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 August 2025\par 11:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Experts predict changes to \u8216\'20\'18food stamps\u8217\'20\'19 program affecting millions of Americans will cause poverty and hunger to rise\par \par \uc2 Poverty and hunger will rise as a result of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s unprecedented cuts to the US federal \u8220\'20\'1Cfood stamps\u8221\'20\'1D program, according to experts. Low-income workers who rely on the aid are braced for dire consequences.\par \par \uc2 Katie Giede, a single mother and waitress in Conyers, Georgia, is one of the 42 million Americans who use the supplemental nutrition assistance program (Snap). Even with the maximum benefit permitted, she struggles to afford food for her and her child.\par \par \uc2 She makes $3 an hour plus tips at the fast-food chain Waffle House, where she has worked for 11 years. The company {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/16/waffle-house-workers-strike-minimum-wage-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deducts}}}\uc2  meals from workers\u8217\'20\'19 pay check per shift, regardless of whether they eat one or not.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur pay is already so little that we\u8217\'20\'19re struggling with everything,\u8221\'20\'1D Giede told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CSingle mothers like myself are reliant upon the benefits like Snap and Medicaid. So when you go and you cut that as well, now you have mothers out here that are not only worried at night because they already can\u8217\'20\'19t afford housing or a vehicle, but we\u8217\'20\'19re also worried what is our kid is going to eat? Because we no longer have help.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Giede said she received $450 a month for her and her child. She said working too many hours or receiving too much incomewas a constant concern,due to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fns.usda.gov/snap/recipient/eligibility"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eligibility}}}\uc2  cut-offs.\par \par \uc2 According to an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.urban.org/urban-wire/cuts-snap-one-big-beautiful-bill-act-would-widen-persistent-gap-between-benefits-and"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 analysis }}}\uc2 by the Urban Institute, at the end of 2024, even the maximum Snap benefit would not cover the cost of a modestly priced meal in 99% of all counties in the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI dread that trip to the grocery store every week, because you have to sit down and you really have to budget,\u8221\'20\'1D said Giede. \u8220\'20\'1CEvery time you go, you\u8217\'20\'19re having to make the choice between something that\u8217\'20\'19s healthy or something that\u8217\'20\'19s cheaper, just so you can get enough to last all week.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are so many people in this country that rely on these benefits, and with these cuts, half of the people that are surviving right now off of this are going to lose their benefits. That\u8217\'20\'19s not even just people not eating a little bit. They\u8217\'20\'19re already not eating enough, so we\u8217\'20\'19re going to lose lives over this. It\u8217\'20\'19s those of us at the bottom that are really feeling it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Waffle House did not respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cbig, beautiful bill\u8221\'20\'1D set the stage for significant cuts to Snap by shifting higher administrative costs to each state, expanding work reporting requirements and imposing restrictions on non-citizen eligibility.\par \par \uc2 Many lower-wage workers have grown more reliant on Snap in recent years. US food prices rose by 23.6% between 2020 and 2024, according to official data. While inflation has since moderated, grocery costs remain high.\par \par \uc2 As a result of the latest Snap changes,states will be responsible for 75% of administrative costs of handling the program from 2027, up from 50% cost-sharing with the federal government, which is likely to strain state budgets.\par \par \uc2 From 2028, for the first time states will be forced to pick up some of the multibillion-dollar bill for Snap benefits. The state of New York, for example, faces a budget impact of about $1.2bn, according to the Food Research and Action Center (Frac), a non-profit advocacy group.\par \par \uc2 While such shifting costs have raised fears that states will cut back Snap support,expanded work requirements have sparked concern that few people will be eligible. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.urban.org/urban-wire/cuts-snap-one-big-beautiful-bill-act-would-widen-persistent-gap-between-benefits-and"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Analysis}}}\uc2  by the Urban Institute found about 22.3 million US families are set to lose some or all of their Snap benefits.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a very targeted, well-thought-out plan of dismantling the Snap program that federal policy makers won\u8217\'20\'19t take responsibility for, because it is the states, it is the governors who will have to cut resources for Snap, who will have to cut the program in order to say we can\u8217\'20\'19t operate this because of what\u8217\'20\'19s happening at the federal level,\u8221\'20\'1Dsaid Gina Plata-Nino, Snap deputy director at the Frac.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSnap is a very important ecosystem at the local level, at the state level and the federal level, because billions of dollars go into states, and this federal money supports local economies,\u8221\'20\'1D she added. \u8220\'20\'1CAll of these proposals threaten this very delicate balance.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The White House deferred comment to the office of management and budget, which did not respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 States across the US are braced for stark consequences.\u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to have worse hunger and ultimately, worse poverty,\u8221\'20\'1D said Seth DiStefano, policy outreach director at the West Virginia Center on Budget and Policy. \u8220\'20\'1CThere are entire regions of West Virginia where there aren\u8217\'20\'19t 20 hours a week [expanded Snap work requirement] of anything to apply for. What do you tell those families?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re talking families with kids now that are going to be subjected to these harsh work reporting requirements. We\u8217\'20\'19re talking folks in their 60s, literally in communities where there are no jobs, none, and ripping away the one outlet to their basic needs that\u8217\'20\'19s available to them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Among the employers with the most workers reliant on Snap is \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , the largest private employer in the US, as much of its workforce {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/economy/archive/2025/05/part-time-jobs-underwork/682768/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 receives}}}\uc2  only part-time hours.\par \par \uc2 Christina Gahagan, 66, has worked at \uc2 Walmart\uc2  for a decade in western New York at several stores. She is currently based at a store in Geneseo, New York.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI would say at least 50% of the people in my store rely on food stamps to make ends meet for their families,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gahagan. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re always trying to figure out where the best deals are, coupon clipping at lunch and reading circulars to see who\u8217\'20\'19s got the best deal on whatever, just to make their money stretch.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She has cut down on costly grocery items, eating cheaper junk food to get by.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was paying $9 a pound for roast beef. Today it\u8217\'20\'19s almost $13 a pound. So I\u8217\'20\'19m not buying full pounds any more. I\u8217\'20\'19m just buying a half a pound and making it work and just eating crap in between, like buying myself some chips out of the vending machine or something,\u8221\'20\'1D Gahagan said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Walmart\uc2  is the largest employer in the US. We rival \uc2 Amazon\uc2  almost dollar for dollar in what we do. You would think a company like that could shell out a little bit more money per hour for associates in the store across the board, so that there aren\u8217\'20\'19t people who are having to depend so heavily on public assistance.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  did not respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250801el81003bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
