\par\par \b \uc2 Outcry as \uc2 US agriculture department\uc2  to cut salaries and relocate staff\b0\par\par\uc2 Nina Lakhani and Gabrielle Canon \par \uc2 1,346 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 July 2025\par 21:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Experts warn \u8216\'20\'18half-baked\u8217\'20\'19 restructuring will further erode support for US farmers and hamper wildfire response\par \par \uc2 Thousands of employees at the \uc2 US Department of Agriculture\uc2  will be forced to take salary cuts and relocate out of the Washington DC area, as part of a major restructuring that experts warn will further weaken support for American farmers and complicate wildfire response.\par \par \uc2 In a memorandum issued on Thursday, the agriculture secretary, \uc2 Brooke Rollins\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usda.gov/sites/default/files/documents/sm-1078-015.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 outlined the \u8220\'20\'1Ckey pillars\u8221\'20\'1D behind the department\u8217\'20\'19s reorganization}}}\uc2 , focused on reducing its financial footprint, removing resources from the capital, eliminating management and consolidating workforces responsible for a range of functions, including freedom of information requests, tribal relations, grants and human resources.\par \par \uc2 More than half of employees working in the Washington DC area will be relocated to five locations \u8211\'20\'13 Raleigh, North Carolina; Kansas City, Missouri; Indianapolis, Indiana; Fort Collins, Colorado; and Salt Lake City, Utah. Several key \uc2 USDA\uc2  offices will be shuttered in the capital region.\par \par \uc2 The move follows wide-ranging and often chaotic cuts to staff and services being implemented under Trump 2.0, as the administration seeks to dismantle the federal government and fund tax cuts for the wealthy including the president\u8217\'20\'19s billionaire donors.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPresident Trump was elected to make real change in Washington, and we are doing just that by moving our key services outside the Beltway and into great American cities across the country,\u8221\'20\'1D Rollins said in the statement. \u8220\'20\'1CWe will do so through a transparent and commonsense process that preserves \uc2 USDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s critical health and public safety services the American public relies on.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a video call, Rollins informed \uc2 USDA\uc2  staff that they would be advised about new assignments \u8211\'20\'13 and homes \u8211\'20\'13 over the next few months.\par \par \uc2 More than 90% of the department\u8217\'20\'19s almost 100,000 employees are already based in county and regional offices, including at regional research institutions, farm loan offices and conservation facilities. The reorganization will leave only 2,000 of the current 4,600 \uc2 USDA\uc2  staff in the Beltway.\par \par \uc2 The department will also eliminate or scale down regional offices, combining them into \u8220\'20\'1Chub locations to the greatest extent possible\u8221\'20\'1D, according to the memo.\par \par \uc2 Rollins said that the changes would help the \uc2 USDA\uc2  better serve its \u8220\'20\'1Ccore constituents\u8221\'20\'1D of farmers, ranchers and US producers, focus on the administration\u8217\'20\'19s priorities and eliminate management layers and bureaucracy.\par \par \uc2 But experts warned that the latest cuts and consolidation of key departments focused on civil rights and small and disadvantaged businesses will further hamstring the agency, which is already reeling\par \par \uc2 The latest upheaval follows widespread cuts to Biden era agricultural programs, research grants and staff across the country, which along with Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariff chaos and deepening climate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/15/farmers-trump-tariffs-bailout-extreme-weather"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 chaos has caused panic among many farmers.}}}\par \par \uc2 In a written statement, Amy Klobuchar, the Democratic ranking member of the Senate agriculture, nutrition and forestry committee, condemned the plan as a \u8220\'20\'1Chalf-baked proposal\u8221\'20\'1D and called for \uc2 USDA\uc2  officials to appear before Congress.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA reorganization of this scale will impact \uc2 USDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ability to provide critical services to Americans and undermine the agency\u8217\'20\'19s trusted expertise that farmers and families count on \u8230\'20\'26 we must have an immediate hearing before more damage is done.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s move further guts the government\u8217\'20\'19s ability to protect public health, the environment and food safety. The real-world consequences will be severe, directly affecting people\u8217\'20\'19s lives,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rebecca Wolf, senior policy analyst at \uc2 Food & Water Watch\uc2  Senior Food, a non-profit research and advocacy group.\par \par \uc2 The reorganization is at least partly a cost-cutting measure, according to Rollins, and the relocated staff could see significant salary reductions due to lower rates paid outside the capital due to difference in the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 But details were scant on how the plan will unfold, especially when it comes to management and administration of firefighters at the \uc2 US Forest Service\uc2 , an agency housed within the \uc2 USDA\uc2 . The \uc2 USFS\uc2 , which employs the bulk of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s largest firefighting force, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/jul/22/us-firefighter-shortage-wildfires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 is facing severe staffing shortages}}}\uc2 , a Guardian investigation found this week, as wildfires rage across the country.\par \par \uc2 Rollins emphasized that the plan will ensure continued support for fire operations and other activities critical to the department\u8217\'20\'19s mission, but there are concerns that further workforce cuts and administrative focus lost to the reorganization during the peak of fire season could have disastrous effects.\par \par \uc2 As fire risks sharply rise, crews have already begun to feel the impacts from previous{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/19/summer-wildfire-preparedness"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  cuts to budgets and workers at the agency}}}\uc2  that support wildfire mitigation and response. The programs incentivized by the Trump administration to sharply shrink the federal government rely on voluntary resignations and early retirements, which undercut the agency\u8217\'20\'19s potential to make strategic decisions about its workforce.\par \par \uc2 Roughly 1,400 workers with fire qualifications signed on for the programs, leaving holes on teams that play crucial roles in emergency response, especially during the busiest times of the season. Acknowledging the need to backfill these positions, Rollins called for some to return to active duty through the end of the season \u8211\'20\'13 only 65 have been reinstated, according to a department spokesperson.\par \par \uc2 The Forest Service will also see its nine regional offices phased out over the next year and all research stations will be consolidated into one, housed in Fort Collins, Colorado.\par \par \uc2 Experts were struggling to make sense of the announcement, and shared concerns about how another layer of change could cause more chaos and disorganization as fire risks continue to surge.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUntil we know more specifically about the fire program it\u8217\'20\'19s hard to determine what some of the outcomes of this could be,\u8221\'20\'1D said Riva Duncan, a retired \uc2 USFS\uc2  fire officer and vice-president of the Grassroots Wildland Firefighters advocacy group. Duncan added that the consolidation of \uc2 USFS\uc2  research stations could be problematic because each does fire research and has a focus specific to the landscapes where the station is housed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is another example of decisions being made by people who haven\u8217\'20\'19t bothered to learn or understand the work,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 It is also unclear if the reorganization is designed to align with plans from the Trump administration to combine federal firefighters into a single agency, under the Department of the Interior. Those plans were left in limbo on Tuesday, after the House appropriations committee determined \u8220\'20\'1Cchanges in budgetary and management structure spark concerns about impacted agencies\u8217\'20\'19 abilities to consistently meet critical performance benchmarks\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Government Accountability Office\uc2  (GAO) study was ordered to evaluate the feasibility of the plan, and the House of Representatives adjourned for its August recess on Wednesday, delaying any budget votes until at least September.\par \par \uc2 More than 15,300 employees have already left the \uc2 USDA\uc2  since Trump took office, opting for buyouts and early retirement through the administration\u8217\'20\'19s{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2025/05/03/more-than-15000-employees-resign-agriculture-department-trump-00324834"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  deferred resignation plan}}}\uc2 . Similar staff-cutting measures have been implemented across the federal government, overseen by the so-called department of government efficiency, the quasi-government agency created by the billionaire Trump donor \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is less a reorganization and more a dismantling. This mass relocation will be costly. It will also result in the mass resignation of staff, which means a major loss of capacity at \uc2 USDA\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ben Lilliston, director of rural strategies and climate change at the \uc2 Institute for Agriculture and Trade Policy\uc2  (\uc2 IATP\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CContrary to the secretary\u8217\'20\'19s statement, the \uc2 USDA\uc2  is already understaffed. There was no effort to get input from Congress, the public or farmers about this reorganization.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A similar \uc2 USDA\uc2  relocation program during the first Trump term led to a smaller, less efficient, less experienced and less diverse workforce, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gao.gov/assets/gao-23-104709.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 GAO, the bipartisan federal government watchdog.}}}\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 USDA\uc2  workforce grew 8% during the Biden administration, while salaries rose 15%, largely on temporary funding, the department said on Thursday, as Rollins confirmed that the cuts would continue.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis reorganization is another step of the department\u8217\'20\'19s process of reducing its workforce,\u8221\'20\'1D Rollins wrote in the memo, noting that programs to incentivize early retirements and voluntary resignations will continue to reduce the staff numbers further.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250724el7o006hd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
