\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s time to rethink how we measure labor in the US\b0\par\par\uc2 Gene Marks \par \uc2 819 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 July 2025\par 14:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Between AI, changes under Trump and a \u8216\'20\'18tougher environment\u8217\'20\'19, what do we really know about the jobs market?\par \par \uc2 Last week the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/lifestyle/careers/job-listings-labor-market-373b4331?mod=hp_featst_pos4.%22"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wall Street Journal}}}\uc2  reported that, as a result of the \u8220\'20\'1Ctougher environment\u8221\'20\'1D in the labor market, companies are \u8220\'20\'1Cin control again\u8221\'20\'1D and are warning applicants of \u8220\'20\'1Clong hours and few boundaries\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 At the same time some industries are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.staffingindustry.com/news/global-daily-news/us-needs-46m-workers-annually-or-will-face-labor-crisis-report-warns"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reporting worker shortages}}}\uc2  due to Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s immigration crackdown. And then there\u8217\'20\'19s artificial intelligence, which is about to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2025/05/28/ai-jobs-white-collar-unemployment-anthropic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 devastate}}}\uc2  the jobs market \u8211\'20\'13 or is about to create {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/Business/ai-create-new-jobs-despite-gloomy-forecasts-experts/story?id=123607557"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new jobs.}}}\par \par \uc2 Amid all this change, what do we really know about the jobs market?\par \par \uc2 Just the other week the Department of Labor said the economy added {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jul/03/us-economy-jobs-report#:~:text=US%20adds%20147%2C000%20jobs%20in,and%20employment%20data%20%7C%20The%20Guardian"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 147,000 new jobs}}}\uc2  to the economy. So that seems encouraging? But the official figures are subject to revision \u8211\'20\'13 and big ones too. The labor department has said its own numbers were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/u-s-economy-added-818000-fewer-jobs-than-first-reported-sign-job-market-has-been-slowing#:~"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overstated}}}\uc2  by as much as 818,000 during the first eight months of 2024.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jul/06/gen-z-saving-retirement-employers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Born into crisis, gen Z is saving for retirement like no other generation | Gene Marks}}}\par \par \uc2 The payroll giant ADP is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://mediacenter.adp.com/2025-07-02-ADP-National-Employment-Report-Private-Sector-Employment-Shed-33,000-Jobs-in-June-Annual-Pay-was-Up-4-4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more in line with}}}\uc2  the some pessimists on Wall Street, saying that the private economy shrank by 33,000 in June. But their competitor, \uc2 Paychex\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.paychex.com/employment-watch/#!/news-release/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  that small business employment \u8211\'20\'13 which represents about half of the country\u8217\'20\'19s workers \u8211\'20\'13 has continued to \u8220\'20\'1Chold steady\u8221\'20\'1D throughout this year. Job openings \u8220\'20\'1Cjumped\u8221\'20\'1D to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.advisorperspectives.com/dshort/updates/2025/07/01/jolts-report-job-openings-may-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 six-month high}}}\uc2  last month. Does anyone know how the labor market is actually doing?\par \par \uc2 No economist, no government agency, no academic that I know has yet figured out how many Americans are working or not. Why? Because it\u8217\'20\'19s not just about jobs anymore. It\u8217\'20\'19s about income. The \u8220\'20\'1Cjobs\u8221\'20\'1D data we read isn\u8217\'20\'19t relevant.\par \par \uc2 Last week a report in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fortune.com/2025/07/03/overemployed-workers-five-jobs-six-figure-salaries-infiltrate-tech-startups-reddit-community/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fortune}}}\uc2  introduced us the concept of the \u8220\'20\'1Cover-employed\u8221\'20\'1D. These are workers \u8211\'20\'13 many in the tech industry \u8211\'20\'13 that are holding down more than one job at a time, with some making as much as $3,000 per day working for multiple employers. But the over-employed trend goes beyond this.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s because in the same week, new data from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.yahoo.com/news/bosses-remote-workers-spend-2-161143804.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics}}}\uc2  revealed what we already knew: workers working remotely were logging two hours less hours per day than their counterparts coming into the office. So what are they doing with this extra time? Maybe they\u8217\'20\'19re watching \uc2 Netflix\uc2 . Others were generating more income for themselves doing other things, like starting their own businesses.\par \par \uc2 According to new data from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/BABATOTALSAUS"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Census Bureau}}}\uc2 : almost 460,000 applications were filed for new businesses in June alone, a level almost twice the amount of the monthly average before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 We all know these \u8220\'20\'1Cworkers\u8221\'20\'1D. Some have multiple full-time jobs. Others have multiple part-time jobs. Many have multiple sources of income. They drive Ubers. They have \uc2 Etsy\uc2  shops. They\u8217\'20\'19re selling used sneakers on \uc2 eBay\uc2  and working late shifts under the table at the local bar. They write programs. They work with data. They do it all! Are we taking all of these people into consideration when analyzing the \u8220\'20\'1Cjob market?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some of the \u8220\'20\'1Cover-employed\u8221\'20\'1D and really just underpaid and need the extra work to make ends meet, unfortunately. According to a new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nerdwallet.com/article/finance/2025-financial-goals-midyear-check-in-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NerdWallet data}}}\uc2 , nearly two in five Americans are aiming to make more money this year and 10% have started a side business or second job just to cover basic necessities.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jun/22/small-business-economic-outlook"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Small business owners aren\u8217\'20\'19t jumping for joy about the US economy \u8211\'20\'13 but they still aren\u8217\'20\'19t jumping ship}}}\par \par \uc2 In this increasingly complicated \u8220\'20\'1Cjobs\u8221\'20\'1D market perhaps it is time for economists to stop evaluating the labor market in terms of jobs. This is quickly becoming obsolete. We need to measure income.\par \par \uc2 How many people over the age of 18 in the US are earning more than, say $50,000 per year, taking into consideration all sources of income and inflation-adjusted? What percentage is that compared to the working-age population? How has that increased or decreased over time?\par \par \uc2 Isn\u8217\'20\'19t this more relevant than the numbers we\u8217\'20\'19re getting now? And shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t the numbers come from actual, real data and not from an unreliable Department of Labor survey that\u8217\'20\'19s revised 10 times after it is initially published? Can\u8217\'20\'19t we get this information from tax returns, social security and private sources such as \uc2 Etsy\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 eBay\uc2 , ADP and \uc2 Paychex\uc2 ? Can\u8217\'20\'19t this be calculated and updated monthly and annually? Of course it can.\par \par \uc2 Major economic policy decisions still hinge on the unemployment rate and jobs growth. Politicians get re-elected or ejected on this data. But in a job market that\u8217\'20\'19s being shaken by AI, immigration and the rapid rise of side gigs, it\u8217\'20\'19s time we rethought how we measure the health of the labor market.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250720el7k001s0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
