\par\par \uc2 Environment\par \b \uc2 How Trump\u8217\'20\'19s anti-immigrant policies could collapse the US food industry \u8211\'20\'13 visualized\b0\par\par\uc2 Rita Liu and Nina Lakhani \par \uc2 1,050 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 July 2025\par 22:18 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The president is threatening to deport essential farm workers, grocery clerks and food delivery drivers. But without them, shelves could go empty and prices could soar\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://viewer.gutools.co.uk/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s assault on immigrants is starting to hit the American food supply.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, farmers who have for years depended on undocumented people for cheap labor \u8211\'20\'13 to plant, harvest and haul produce \u8211\'20\'13 have reported that workers are staying home to avoid raids by \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  (Ice). In Los Angeles, restaurants and food trucks have been forced to close as the immigrants who cook and wait tables fear Ice and other law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFarm workers in many states are thinking about leaving the country because they are facing more obstacles to work under this anti-immigrant administration,\u8221\'20\'1D said Elizabeth Rodriguez, director of farm worker advocacy with National Farm Worker Ministry, a longstanding organization in south Texas. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are scared, there are fewer opportunities, and they are no longer prospering here. Their fear will soon be seen in the harvest, when the quantities of produce are depleted.\u8221\'20\'1D https://interactive.guim.co.uk/embed/iframe-wrapper/0.1/boot.js" data-canonical-url="https://interactive.guim.co.uk/uploader/embed/2025/07/archive-zip/giv-325544nNQMZEQE29O/" data-alt="graphs show that immigrants make up 27% of agricultural workers nationwide. In some states it\u8217\'20\'19s even higher: in California, foreign-born workers are nearly two-thirds of the agricultural workforce. And in Florida, they make up more than half of crop production workers."> {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://interactive.guim.co.uk/uploader/embed/2025/07/archive-zip/giv-325544nNQMZEQE29O/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Interactive}}}\par \par \uc2 From farm to table, at least one in five jobs in the food industry is carried out by immigrants, the equivalent of 14 million workers across the sector. This includes 27% of agricultural workers nationwide and 33% of meatpackers. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://restaurant.org/getmedia/a3912d4b-9fd5-42f5-989c-fbc8e8929772/nra-data-brief-restaurant-employee-demographics-april-2025.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In restaurants, 46%}}}\uc2  of chefs and 31% of cooks were born outside the US \u8211\'20\'13 mostly in Mexico, China, Guatemala and El Salvador.\par \par \uc2 These jobs are critical: immigrants made up a disproportionate number of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.jec.senate.gov/public/_cache/files/6750b0f0-c851-4fee-9619-295582fd44e8/immigrants-are-vital-to-the-us-economy-final.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cessential\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  workers during the Covid-19 pandemic, and many were exposed to unsafe conditions so that crops could be harvested, cows milked and takeout delivered.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhether it\u8217\'20\'19s the workers behind the scenes in meatpacking plants or on the frontlines of the grocery store, our country relies heavily on the labor of immigrants to keep our food system running and our families fed,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mark Lauritsen, international vice-president at \uc2 United Food and Commercial Workers International Union\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWithout a stable, skilled workforce, safety and quality can decline, shelves can sit empty and grocery prices could rise even more.\u8221\'20\'1D https://interactive.guim.co.uk/embed/iframe-wrapper/0.1/boot.js" data-canonical-url="https://interactive.guim.co.uk/uploader/embed/2025/07/archive-zip/giv-32554L8SYClqfS78T/" data-alt="graphs show immigrants comprise 33% of meat processing workers. In Nebraska, they make up 58% of those workers. More than one-fifth of all truck transportation workers are immigrants. There are 462,000 migrants working in grocery stores, or 15% of the workforce. They also make up more than one-third of the workforce in commercial bakeries."> {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://interactive.guim.co.uk/uploader/embed/2025/07/archive-zip/giv-32554L8SYClqfS78T/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Interactive}}}\par \par \uc2 Yet food industry jobs, from fields to slaughterhouses and supermarkets to delivery drivers, are notoriously difficult and often low-paid: sometimes as little as $7.25 per hour, the federal minimum wage. Farmhands are often paid only a few dollars per box of tomatoes or cabbages harvested - backbreaking work with no shade. American food companies rely on undocumented {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.jec.senate.gov/public/_cache/files/6750b0f0-c851-4fee-9619-295582fd44e8/immigrants-are-vital-to-the-us-economy-final.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 people for almost half}}}\uc2  of the most physical jobs, including the farm laborers who cultivate crops, tend livestock and build fences, as well as the meat processors who slaughter, eviscerate and package at high speeds.\par \par \uc2 Most Americans understand this. In the run-up to last year\u8217\'20\'19s election, 75% of registered voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2024/10/21/most-us-voters-say-immigrants-no-matter-their-legal-status-mostly-take-jobs-citizens-dont-want/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told Pew Research}}}\uc2  that they believed undocumented immigrants mostly fill jobs US citizens don\u8217\'20\'19t want to do.\par \par \uc2 And now, with Ice raids and mass deportations, these jobs have become even more dangerous. At least one farm worker, Jaime Alan\u237\'00\'EDs, a 57-year-old Mexican man, has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/14/jaime-alanis-california-ice-raid-death"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 died after falling from a greenhouse trying to escape armed Ice agents}}}\uc2  during a raid in southern California last week. In response to these terrifying Ice raids which are spreading and becoming more violent, some farm workers in California are planning a strike in coming weeks and will be calling on consumers to boycott produce.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is appalling to see the threat of violence and deportation that immigrant workers face every day. These people play a crucial role in restaurants, and more importantly in the community,\u8221\'20\'1D said Elyanna Calle, a restaurant worker in Austin, Texas, and president of Restaurant Workers United. \u8220\'20\'1CA raid, a deportation means the destruction of a life someone has fought to build; it means the destruction of families and vibrant communities. I have seen my co-workers fear for their safety, I have seen them go into fight or flight mode at the prospect of an Ice raid \u8211\'20\'13 this is not something that any person, any immigrant deserves.\u8221\'20\'1D https://interactive.guim.co.uk/embed/iframe-wrapper/0.1/boot.js" data-canonical-url="https://interactive.guim.co.uk/uploader/embed/2025/07/archive-zip/giv-32554bXR8L2nQ34Eu/" data-alt="Graphs show that according to the \uc2 National Restaurant Association\uc2 , 4m immigrants work in the industry, including 31% of cooks, 18% of waitstaff and more than 17% of food preparation and counter workers."> {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://interactive.guim.co.uk/uploader/embed/2025/07/archive-zip/giv-32554bXR8L2nQ34Eu/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Interactive}}}\par \par \uc2 If the Trump administration oversees even a fraction of its promised mass deportation of 11 million undocumented immigrants, it could lead to major disruptions across the food system: crops left to spoil in the fields, supermarket shelves unstocked, takeout deliveries delayed and food prices soaring even higher. It could also upend rural economies that depend on migrant workers and their families who live, work and go to school in small declining communities.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll of this will have a huge impact on the rest of us because the immigrant community contributes much more than their labor; they pay taxes,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rodriguez. \u8220\'20\'1CThey invest in the economy, and if they make less money, we all make less, and when corporations make less, they increase prices so we lose again.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Data for this piece, unless otherwise noted, come from the \uc2 Migration Policy Institute\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250717el7h0025v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
