\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Farm worker dies a day after chaotic immigration raid at California farm\b0\par\par\uc2 Diana Ramirez-Simon and agencies \par \uc2 1,043 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 July 2025\par 10:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jaime Alanis died after sustaining injuries during raid where authorities say they arrested about 200 people\par \par \uc2 A farm worker died on Friday from injuries that he sustained a day earlier in raids on two California cannabis farm sites as US immigration authorities confirmed they arrested 200 workers after a tense standoff with authorities.\par \par \uc2 Jaime Alanis\u8217\'20\'19s death was confirmed in a social media post by the \uc2 United Farm Workers\uc2  advocacy group. \u8220\'20\'1CWe tragically can confirm that a farm worker has died of injuries they sustained as a result of yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s immigration enforcement action,\u8221\'20\'1D the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/UFWupdates/status/1943778822761197939"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 post read}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  said in a statement that authorities executed criminal search warrants in Carpinteria and Camarillo, California, on Thursday. They arrested immigrants suspected of being in the country illegally and there were also at least 10 immigrant children on site, the statement said.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/07/los-angeles-park-federal-officers-national-guard"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Reality TV spectacle\u8217\'20\'19: outrage as federal agents raid LA neighborhood with horses and armored cars}}}\par \par \uc2 Four US citizens were arrested for \u8220\'20\'1Cassaulting or resisting officers\u8221\'20\'1D, the department said. Authorities were offering a $50,000 reward for information leading to the arrest of one person suspected of firing a gun at federal agents. At least one worker was hospitalized with grave injuries.\par \par \uc2 During the raid, crowds of people gathered outside Glass House Farms at the Camarillo location to demand information about their relatives and protest against immigration enforcement. A chaotic scene developed outside the farm that grows tomatoes, cucumbers and cannabis as authorities clad in helmets and uniforms faced off with the demonstrators. Acrid green and white billowing smoke then forced community members to retreat.\par \par \uc2 Glass House, a licensed California cannabis grower, said in a statement that immigration agents had valid warrants. The company said workers were detained and it was helping provide them with legal representation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGlass House has never knowingly violated applicable hiring practices and does not and has never employed minors,\u8221\'20\'1D the statement said.\par \par \uc2 It is legal to grow and sell cannabis in California with proper licensing. State records show the company has multiple active licenses to cultivate cannabis.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, about two dozen people waited outside the Camarillo farm to retrieve the cars of loved ones and speak to managers about what happened. Relatives of Jaime Alanis, who has worked picking tomatoes at the farm for 10 years, said he called his wife in Mexico during the raid to tell her immigration agents had arrived and that he was hiding with others inside the farm.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe next thing we heard was that he was in the hospital,\u8221\'20\'1D Juan Duran, Alanis\u8217\'20\'19s brother-in-law, said in Spanish, his voice breaking.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Strater, national vice-president of the \uc2 United Farm Workers\uc2 , said Alanis was injured after a 30ft fall from a building during the raid.\par \par \uc2 After immigration agents arrived at Glass House\u8217\'20\'19s farm in Camarillo on Thursday morning, workers called family members to let them know authorities were there. Relatives and advocates headed to the farm about 50 miles (80km) north-west of downtown Los Angeles to try to find out what was going on, and began protesting outside.\par \par \uc2 Federal authorities formed a line blocking the road leading through farm fields to the company\u8217\'20\'19s greenhouses. Protesters were seen shouting at agents wearing camouflage gear, helmets and gas masks. The billowing smoke drove protesters to retreat. It was unclear why authorities threw the canisters or if they released chemicals such as teargas.\par \par \uc2 Ventura county fire authorities responding to a 911 call of people having trouble breathing said three people were taken to nearby hospitals.\par \par \uc2 At the farm, agents arrested workers and removed them by bus. Others, including US citizens, were detained at the site for hours while agents investigated.\par \par \uc2 The incident came as federal immigration agents have ramped up arrests in southern California at car washes, farms and \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  parking lots, stoking widespread fear among immigrant communities.\par \par \uc2 The mother of an American worker said her son was held at the worksite for 11 hours and told her agents took workers\u8217\'20\'19 cellphones to prevent them from calling family or filming and forced them to erase cellphone video of agents at the site.\par \par \uc2 The woman said her son told her agents marked the men\u8217\'20\'19s hands with ink to distinguish their immigration status. She spoke to the Associated Press on condition of anonymity because she feared reprisals from the government.\par \par \uc2 United Farm Workers\uc2  said in statement that some US citizens were not yet accounted for.\par \par \uc2 Maria Servin, 68, said her son had worked at the farm for 18 years and was helping to build a greenhouse. She said she spoke to her son, who is undocumented, after hearing of the raid and offered to pick him up.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe said not to come because they were surrounded and there was even a helicopter. That was the last time I spoke to him,\u8221\'20\'1D Servin, a US citizen, said in Spanish.\par \par \uc2 She said she went to the farm anyway but federal agents were shooting teargas and rubber bullets and she decided it was not safe to stay. She and her daughter returned to the farm on Friday and were told her son had been arrested on Thursday. They still don\u8217\'20\'19t know where he is being held.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI regret 1,000 times that I didn\u8217\'20\'19t help him get his documents,\u8221\'20\'1D Servin said. ea05a110-2f0f-41ea-ba0a-8d9189dbddb7\par \par \uc2 The best public interest journalism relies on first-hand accounts from people in the know.\par \par \uc2 If you have something to share on this subject you can contact us confidentially using the following methods.\par \par \uc2 Secure Messaging in the Guardian app\par \par \uc2 The Guardian app has a tool to send tips about stories. Messages are end to end encrypted and concealed within the routine activity that every Guardian mobile app performs. This prevents an observer from knowing that you are communicating with us at all, let alone what is being said.\par \par \uc2 If you don't already have the Guardian app, download it ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apps.apple.com/app/the-guardian-live-world-news/id409128287"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 iOS}}}\uc2 /{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.guardian"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Android}}}\uc2 ) and go to the menu. Select \u8216\'20\'18Secure Messaging\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 SecureDrop, instant messengers, email, telephone and post\par \par \uc2 See our guide at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tips"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 theguardian.com/tips}}}\uc2  for alternative methods and the pros and cons of each.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250714el7e002