\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US state department issues first of 1,350 termination orders after court lifted ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Andrew Roth in Washington \par \uc2 742 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2025\par 01:44 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Career diplomats and other staff begin receiving notices amid Trump administration cull of about 15% of workforce\par \par \uc2 The US state department has begun issuing the first of more than 1,350 termination notices as part of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/10/state-department-layoffs-rif-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 huge reorganisation}}}\uc2  of America\u8217\'20\'19s diplomatic corps under the secretary of state, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/marco-rubio"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Marco Rubio}}}\uc2 , according to internal documents and US diplomats at the state department on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Career diplomats and other staff began to receive the notices on Friday morning, days after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/08/supreme-court-trump-federal-layoffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supreme court lifted a ban}}}\uc2  on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2  moving forward with mass firings of government employees that will affect hundreds of thousands of federal workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHearing \u8216\'20\'18I got mine!\u8217\'20\'19 around the floor,\u8221\'20\'1D one current state department employee told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 In an internal email obtained by the Guardian on Friday, state department workers were told that nearly 3,000 employees would leave as part of a vast reorganisation meant to align the department\u8217\'20\'19s goals with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s stated goal of putting America first.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/22/marco-rubio-state-department-overhaul"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Marco Rubio announces sweeping reorganisation of US state department}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis [Reduction in Force] comprises 1,107 civil service and 246 Foreign Service employees with domestic assignments,\u8221\'20\'1D read the email. Another 1,600 employees are believed to have left the service via a voluntary redundancy scheme in recent months.\par \par \uc2 The state department had confirmed on Thursday that it planned to move forward with the layoffs.\par \par \uc2 The cull of approximately 15% of the state department workforce is unprecedented for the diplomatic corps, and confusion over the process as well as the temporary court injunction against mass firings of government employees have made the rollout even more chaotic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt a moment of great global instability \u8211\'20\'13 with war raging in Ukraine, conflict between Israel and Iran, and authoritarian regimes testing the boundaries of international order \u8211\'20\'13 the United States has chosen to gut its frontline diplomatic workforce,\u8221\'20\'1D said a statement from the \uc2 American Foreign Service Association\uc2 , a professional group that represents US diplomats. \u8220\'20\'1CWe oppose this decision in the strongest terms.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMorale has been rock-bottom since they announced [the reorganisation],\u8221\'20\'1D one US diplomat told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CI think for some people the fact it is finally happening is a relief but it\u8217\'20\'19s awful,\u8221\'20\'1D the person added.\par \par \uc2 Some advocacy groups reported that the state department was unable to process the hundreds of RIFs simultaneously due to the limitations of internal systems.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe state department has apparently encountered a problem with the RIF software that caps the number of emails that can be sent out at a time, so rather than one big RIF at 10.30am tomorrow, we may see \u8216\'20\'18Rolling RIFs\u8217\'20\'19 throughout the day,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote Karl Stoltz, co-president of the Public Diplomacy Council of America, in an email to members.\par \par \uc2 A senior state department official said: \u8220\'20\'1CThis is simply not true. Email systems always take time to roll these out, and the timeline has been consistent with expectations. We did not have plans for one big email blast at a specific time, and did not indicate any such time to the workforce.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Members of the PDCA and other groups that support federal employees were expected to meet at the state department at 4pm on Friday in order to \u8220\'20\'1Cclap out\u8221\'20\'1D employees who had received RIFs and to \u8220\'20\'1Cprotest the department\u8217\'20\'19s actions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A senior state department official on Thursday told journalists that the department was \u8220\'20\'1Cgoing to work to handle this in a manner that preserves, to the maximum extent possible, the dignity of federal employees and foreign service officers and civil servants who are affected by this.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not easy for anyone,\u8221\'20\'1D the official said.\par \par \uc2 The layoffs were also criticised by Democratic senators, who said that the administration\u8217\'20\'19s decision \u8220\'20\'1Cundermines our national security\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile there are targeted reforms that our government can pursue to maximize the impact of every tax dollar, that\u8217\'20\'19s not what this is,\u8221\'20\'1D read the statement, which was co-signed by 10 Democratic senators, including Senator Jeanne Shaheen, ranking member of the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  foreign relations committee.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are active conflicts and humanitarian crises in Ukraine, Sudan, Gaza, Haiti and Myanmar \u8211\'20\'13 to name a few. Now is the time to strengthen our diplomatic hand, not weaken it. From pursuing peaceful resolutions to out-competing China diplomatically and economically, we can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to not have experienced diplomats at the table.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250711el7b002jq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
