\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Something stinks in Philadelphia \u8211\'20\'13 and it\u8217\'20\'19s not just the uncollected garbage\b0\par\par\uc2 Arwa Mahdawi \par \uc2 594 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2025\par 12:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The city give its sanitation workers a rise only after eight days of strike action. But there was no shortage of money for the police or mayor Cherelle Parker and her inner circle\par \par \uc2 If nobody stopped me, I could talk about urban sanitation for hours on end. Unfortunately, somebody (normally my wife) always stops me \u8211\'20\'13 usually in the first minute or two. It seems few people share my {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/jun/29/obsessed-litter-middle-age-philadelphia-filthadelphia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pathological fixation}}}\uc2  with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2025/mar/18/i-was-always-terrified-of-confrontation-then-i-hit-my-40s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 littering}}}\uc2  and ways to optimise waste management.\par \par \uc2 Until recently, that is. Now every single resident of Philadelphia, my home town, is talking trash. Almost {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/philadelphia-labor-strike-trash-911-july-4-969cfa2d8f9695b199013690ad694555"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 10,000 city workers}}}\uc2 , members of the city\u8217\'20\'19s largest blue-collar union, went on strike for eight days, affecting everything from libraries to public swimming pools. But the most visible signs of the strike were the huge piles of trash that accumulated (nicknamed \u8220\'20\'1CParker Piles\u8221\'20\'1D after mayor Cherelle Parker) because there was no garbage pickup. Did I mention it has been boiling hot? You can imagine the situation. The rats are in heaven; I am in hell.\par \par \uc2 Early on Wednesday, a deal was finally done. The trash is still there, however. While I have been a woman on the verge of a nervous breakdown, it seems the stinky trash piles did exactly what they were meant to do: remind everyone that the people who keep society running are underpaid and overworked. The world had a brief moment of appreciation for essential workers during the pandemic, but, after everyone finished their nightly clapping, nothing changed, did it? The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oxfam.org/en/press-releases/ten-richest-men-double-their-fortunes-pandemic-while-incomes-99-percent-humanity"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rich just got richer}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The strike started because the mayor said there wasn\u8217\'20\'19t enough money in the city\u8217\'20\'19s budget to give the union what it wanted, which was better benefits and a 5% wage increase {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://6abc.com/post/trash-piles-parts-philadelphia-blue-collar-city-workers-remain-strike/16934731/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a year for three years}}}\uc2 . (The union has accepted a three-year deal that will represent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/philadelphia/news/philadelphia-strike-ended-over-afscme-dc33-union/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a 14% pay increase}}}\uc2  for members over the four years of Parker\u8217\'20\'19s term; the agreement is still to be ratified.)\par \par \uc2 Sanitation workers in Philadelphia make {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcphiladelphia.com/news/local/philadelphia-mayor-office-salary-raise-district-council-33-union-strike-money-wages/4226820/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $39,000 to $42,000 (\u163\'00\'A329,000 to \u163\'00\'A331,000) a year}}}\uc2 . I am obviously no expert in the city\u8217\'20\'19s budget, but it\u8217\'20\'19s funny what there was money for before the strike. There was a cool $877m (\u163\'00\'A3645m) for the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://phlcouncil.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/FY25-Police-Budget-Testimony.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Philadelphia police department}}}\uc2 . Which, by the way, is spending $1.3m on a new uniform that is a slightly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://archive.is/64vkf#selection-649.146-649.295"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 darker shade of blue}}}\uc2 . There was enough money to pay Parker the same sort of salary ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://archive.is/D9485"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about $270,000}}}\uc2 ) as the mayor of the significantly larger New York City. There was enough money, according to analysis by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.inquirer.com/politics/philadelphia/mayor-cherelle-parker-salary-staff-pay-20240531.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Philadelphia Inquirer}}}\uc2  last year, for 16 of Parker\u8217\'20\'19s closest cabinet members to collectively earn $3.5m \u8211\'20\'13 $1m more than their peers earned under the previous mayor.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDon\u8217\'20\'19t tell me what you value,\u8221\'20\'1D Joe Biden used to say. \u8220\'20\'1CShow me your budget and I\u8217\'20\'19ll tell you what you value.\u8221\'20\'1D Now that a deal has been done, I hope Philadelphia starts valuing clean streets \u8211\'20\'13 because the current situation stinks.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Arwa Mahdawi is a Guardian columnist\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Do you have an opinion on the issues raised in this article? If you would like to submit a response of up to 300 words by email to be considered for publication in our{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tone/letters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  letters}}}\uc2  section, please {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:guardian.letters@theguardian.com?body=Please%20include%20your%20name,%20full%20postal%20address%20and%20phone%20number%20with%20your%20letter%20below.%20Letters%20are%20usually%20published%20with%20the%20author%27s%20name%20and%20city/town/village.%20The%20rest%20of%20the%20information%20is%20for%20verification%20only%20and%20to%20contact%20you%20where%20necessary."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 click here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 9 July 2025 to include details of the pay deal agreed between the mayor of Philadelphia and the public sector workers\u8217\'20\'19 union on the morning of 9 July.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250709el79001gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
