\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  clears way for Trump officials to resume mass government firings\b0\par\par\uc2 David Smith in Washington \par \uc2 1,057 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2025\par 21:58 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Justices lift lower court order that froze \u8216\'20\'18reductions in force\u8217\'20\'19 federal layoffs while litigation in case proceeded\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US supreme court}}}\uc2  has cleared the way for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 administration}}}\uc2  to resume plans for mass firings of federal workers that critics warn could threaten critical government services.\par \par \uc2 Extending a winning streak for the US president, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.documentcloud.org/documents/25993246-24a1174-order/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the justices on Tuesday}}}\uc2  lifted a lower court order that had frozen sweeping federal layoffs known as \u8220\'20\'1Creductions in force\u8221\'20\'1D while litigation in the case proceeds.\par \par \uc2 The decision could result in hundreds of thousands of job losses at the departments of agriculture, commerce, health and human services, state, treasury, veterans affairs and other agencies.\par \par \uc2 Democrats condemned the ruling. Antjuan Seawright, a party strategist, said: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m disappointed but I\u8217\'20\'19m not shocked or surprised. This rightwing activist court has proven ruling after ruling, time after time, that they are going to sing the songs and dance to the tune of Trumpism. A lot of this is just implementation of what we saw previewed in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/project-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Project 2025}}}\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/27/trump-supreme-court-press-conference"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump basks in triumph as supreme court kicks away another guardrail}}}\par \par \uc2 Project 2025, a plan drawn up by \uc2 the conservative Heritage Foundation\uc2  thinktank, set out a blueprint for downsizing government. Trump has claimed that voters gave him a mandate for the effort and he tapped billionaire ally \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to lead the charge through the \u8220\'20\'1Cdepartment of government efficiency\u8221\'20\'1D, or Doge, though Musk has since departed.\par \par \uc2 In February, Trump announced \u8220\'20\'1Ca critical transformation of the federal bureaucracy\u8221\'20\'1D in an executive order directing agencies to prepare for a government overhaul aimed at significantly reducing the workforce and gutting offices.\par \par \uc2 In its brief unsigned order on Tuesday, the supreme court said Trump\u8217\'20\'19s administration was \u8220\'20\'1Clikely to succeed on its argument that the executive order\u8221\'20\'1D and a memorandum implementing his order were lawful. The court said it was not assessing the legality of any specific plans for layoffs at federal agencies.\par \par \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , the liberal justice, was the sole member of the nine-person court to publicly dissent from the decision, which overturns San Francisco-based district Judge Susan Illston\u8217\'20\'19s 22 May ruling.\par \par \uc2 Jackson wrote that Illston\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Ctemporary, practical, harm-reducing preservation of the status quo was no match for this court\u8217\'20\'19s demonstrated enthusiasm for greenlighting this president\u8217\'20\'19s legally dubious actions in an emergency posture\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 She also described her colleagues as making the \u8220\'20\'1Cwrong decision at the wrong moment, especially given what little this Court knows about what is actually happening on the ground\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Illston had argued in her ruling that Trump had exceeded his authority in ordering the downsizing, siding with a group of unions, non-profits and local governments that challenged the administration. \u8220\'20\'1CAs history demonstrates, the president may broadly restructure federal agencies only when authorized by Congress,\u8221\'20\'1D she wrote.\par \par \uc2 The judge blocked the agencies from carrying out mass layoffs and limited their ability to cut or overhaul federal programmes. Illston also ordered the reinstatement of workers who had lost their jobs, though she delayed implementing this portion of her ruling while the appeals process plays out.\par \par \uc2 Illston\u8217\'20\'19s ruling was the broadest of its kind against the government overhaul pursued by Trump and Doge. Tens of thousands of federal workers have been fired, have left their jobs via deferred resignation programmes or have been placed on leave.\par \par \uc2 The administration had previously challenged Illston\u8217\'20\'19s order at the San Francisco-based ninth US circuit court of appeals but lost in a 2-1 ruling on 30 May. That prompted the justice department to make an emergency request to the supreme court, contending that controlling the personnel of federal agencies \u8220\'20\'1Clies at the heartland\u8221\'20\'1D of the president\u8217\'20\'19s executive branch authority.\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs had urged the supreme court to deny the justice department\u8217\'20\'19s request. Allowing the Trump administration to move forward with its \u8220\'20\'1Cbreakneck reorganization\u8221\'20\'1D, they wrote, would mean that \u8220\'20\'1Cprograms, offices and functions across the federal government will be abolished, agencies will be radically downsized from what Congress authorized, critical government services will be lost and hundreds of thousands of federal employees will lose their jobs\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s rejection of that argument on Tuesday was welcomed by Trump allies. Pam Bondi, the attorney general, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/AGPamBondi/status/1942681682462597624"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posted on the X social media platform}}}\uc2 : \u8220\'20\'1CToday, the Supreme Court stopped lawless lower courts from restricting President Trump\u8217\'20\'19s authority over federal personnel \u8211\'20\'13 another Supreme Court victory thanks to @thejusticedept attorneys. Now, federal agencies can become more efficient than ever before.\par \par \uc2 The state department {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/StateDept/status/1942685546175746379"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote on X}}}\uc2 : \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s near unanimous decision from the Supreme Court further confirms that the law was on our side throughout this entire process. We will continue to move forward with our historic reorganization plan at the State Department, as announced earlier this year. This is yet another testament to President Trump\u8217\'20\'19s dedication to following through on an America First agenda.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/27/trump-supreme-court-birthright-citizenship-scotus"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US supreme court limits federal judges\u8217\'20\'19 power to block Trump orders}}}\par \par \uc2 In recent months the supreme court has sided with Trump in some major cases that were acted upon on an emergency basis since he returned to office in January.\par \par \uc2 It cleared the way for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s administration to resume deporting migrants to countries other than their own without offering them a chance to show the harms they could face. In two cases, it let the administration end temporary legal status previously granted on humanitarian grounds to hundreds of thousands of migrants.\par \par \uc2 It also allowed Trump to implement his ban on transgender people in the US military, blocked a judge\u8217\'20\'19s order for the administration to rehire thousands of fired employees and twice sided with Doge. In addition, the court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/27/trump-supreme-court-press-conference"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 curbed the power of federal judges}}}\uc2  to impose nationwide rulings impeding presidential policies.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday the Democracy Forward coalition condemned the supreme court for intervening in what it called Trump\u8217\'20\'19s unlawful reorganisation of the federal government. It {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://democracyforward.org/updates/omnibus-rif-scotus-stay/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said in a statement}}}\uc2 : \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s decision has dealt a serious blow to our democracy and puts services that the American people rely on in grave jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis decision does not change the simple and clear fact that reorganizing government functions and laying off federal workers en masse haphazardly without any congressional approval is not allowed by our Constitution.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250708el78003mn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
