\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US adds 147,000 jobs in June, surpassing expectations amid Trump trade war\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani in New York \par \uc2 723 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2025\par 16:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Economists anticipated drop, but 8,000 new positions were added in June compared with May, with unemployment rate down to 4.1%\par \par \uc2 The US economy added 147,000 jobs in June, a sign of continuing strength in the labor market amid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s trade war.\par \par \uc2 The number of jobs added surpassed expectations, as economists largely anticipated a drop in openings. Instead, 3 ,000 more jobs were added in June compared with May, according to new job figures from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  (\uc2 BLS\uc2 ). The unemployment rate actually decreased to 4.1%, down from 4.2% in May.\par \par \uc2 Job gains were seen in state government and healthcare, which saw increases of 47,000 and 39,000 jobs, respectively. Meanwhile, federal government job losses continued, with another 7,000 roles down in June, as the Trump administration continues to cut jobs. The total job loss in the federal government has been 69,000 since January.\par \par \uc2 Though the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-tariffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 president\u8217\'20\'19s tariffs}}}\uc2  have rocked the US stock market, which has seen a dramatic rebound after dipping down 15% in the spring, economists have been worrying that the labor market has just been slower to show sensitivity to the tariffs.\par \par \uc2 New data had shown employers showing signs of hesitancy. The payroll firm ADP found that the private sector {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2025/07/02/adp-jobs-report-june-2025.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lost 33,000 jobs}}}\uc2  in June, far below the 100,000 increase that was expected, and the first decrease since March 2023.\par \par \uc2 The dip in job openings does no necessarily mean companies are laying off more workers; rather, they are creating fewer new positions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThough layoffs continue to be rare, a hesitancy to hire and a reluctance to replace departing workers led to job losses last month,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Nela Richardson\uc2 , chief economist at ADP, in a statement.\par \par \uc2 John Waldron\uc2 , president of \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , said the American economy was \u8220\'20\'1Cbreaking more to the positive\u8221\'20\'1D than many expected after Trump unveiled sweeping tariffs in early April.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe US economy is surprisingly resilient, both in terms of consumer spend [and] the labor dynamic,\u8221\'20\'1D Waldron told reporters on Thursday. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd just general economic economic activity is stronger than one might have guessed if you were sitting here on April 3, projecting what the tariff policy would deliver.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Data from \uc2 BLS{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-07-01/us-job-openings-unexpectedly-rise-to-highest-since-november"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 that measures job openings and turnovers}}}\uc2  in the labor market found that while job openings had climbed in May, to its highest level since November, the vast majority of openings were concentrated in the leisure and hospitality industry. Economists with \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  said the spike in new jobs could be temporary as companies opened new positions in response to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s crackdown on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/usimmigration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 immigrants}}}\uc2 , fearing that immigrant employees could lose work permits.\par \par \uc2 The White House has spent the last few months downplaying the impact tariffs have on the domestic economy, despite anxiety from consumers and businesses over the impact tariffs have on prices.\par \par \uc2 The deadline for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s 90-day pause on some of his highest tariffs is scheduled to expire next week, as the White House tries to broker deals with dozens of countries that could face high tariffs. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/01/federal-reserve-chair-blames-trump-tariffs-for-preventing-interest-rates-cut"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Federal Reserve chair blames Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariffs for preventing interest rate cuts}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 The White House announced on Tuesday a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/02/trump-vietnam-trade-tariffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deal with Vietnam}}}\uc2 , whose products were scheduled to face a 46% tariff. The country agreed to a 20% tariff rate, with no tariffs placed on US exports. The deal with Vietnam follows deals Trump has made with the UK and China, but there are dozens of other countries whose exports could face high tariffs without a deal.\par \par \uc2 Amid economic uncertainty, Trump has tried to pass blame on to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and its chair, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/jerome-powell"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jerome Powell}}}\uc2 . On Monday, Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/01/federal-reserve-chair-blames-trump-tariffs-for-preventing-interest-rates-cut#:~:text=Asked%20if%20the%20Fed%20would,be%20passed%20on%20to%20consumers."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sent an open letter}}}\uc2  to Powell demanding that the Fed lower interest rates.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe\u8217\'20\'19s costing us a fortune because he keeps the rate way up,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump wrote on social media.\par \par \uc2 Powell, in turn, has said that the Fed has not lowered interest rates because of economic uncertainty caused by Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariffs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn effect, we went on hold when we saw the size of the tariffs,\u8221\'20\'1D Powell said. \u8220\'20\'1CEssentially all inflation forecasts for the United States went up materially as a consequence of the tariffs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250703el73001gz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
