\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Cancer experts alarmed over \u8216\'20\'18gut-wrenching\u8217\'20\'19 Trump plan to cut research spending by billions\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,032 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2025\par 12:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 More people will die due to White House\u8217\'20\'19s plans to slash nearly $2.7bn from \uc2 National Cancer Institute\uc2 , workers warn\par \par \uc2 More patients may die as a result of plans drawn up by the Trump administration to cut billions of dollars from the \uc2 National Cancer Institute\uc2  (\uc2 NCI\uc2 ), veteran federal government workers and experts have warned.\par \par \uc2 Nearly $2.7bn would be cut from the agency, which is the largest funder of cancer research in the world \u8211\'20\'13 a decline of 37.2% from the previous year \u8211\'20\'13 under a budget proposal for 2026, in the latest effort to cut staff and funding.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese cuts are absolutely gut wrenching,\u8221\'20\'1D Erin Lavik, former deputy director and chief technology officer at the \uc2 NCI\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s division of cancer prevention, told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 Lavik was fired along with a swath of probationary workers at the institute in February; put on administrative leave in response to a judge\u8217\'20\'19s ruling to halt the firings in March; and then terminated again in April.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re not making things more efficient or better,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat\u8217\'20\'19s being left is sort of the non-impactful iterative work, and we\u8217\'20\'19re pruning all of the potential for transformative science.\u8221\'20\'1D <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/09/nih-scientists-letter-health-research-cuts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NIH scientists go public to denounce Trump\u8217\'20\'19s deep cuts in health research}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 The American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network has cautioned that the proposed cuts \u8220\'20\'1Cwill set this nation back dramatically in our ability to reduce death and suffering\u8221\'20\'1D and noted that cancer is expected to kill more than 618,000 Americans this year.\par \par \uc2 Julie Nickson, vice-president of Federal Advocacy and Coalitions, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThis wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t just be a blow to science, it\u8217\'20\'19s a blow to families, communities, and our economy. Every day counts in the fight against cancer and with more than 2 million Americans expected to be diagnosed with this horrible disease in 2025 alone, now is not the time to go backwards.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jennifer R Brown, secretary of the \uc2 American Society of Hematology\uc2  and director of the Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) Center of the Division of Hematologic Malignancies at \uc2 Dana-Farber Cancer Institute\uc2 , told the Guardian that cuts under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have \u8220\'20\'1Calready been devastating\u8221\'20\'1D, with key research halted that can\u8217\'20\'19t easily be restarted.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat the public needs to know is that the science that may not sound so obvious, or that they may not know that much about, is really what drives our cancer treatments and our cancer cures. And so if we cut that, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to lose it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Brown.\par \par \uc2 Cancer research historically funded by the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  (\uc2 NIH\uc2 ), which houses the \uc2 NCI\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1Cis the basic science that figures out what to target in the cancer cell\u8221\'20\'1D, she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThen a drug may be developed that may be from an academic, it may be from a pharma company, but the trials are then also run by academics and pharma in collaboration, and academics who are funded by \uc2 NIH\uc2 , who do the legwork to figure out how the drug is working in patients.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPharma companies take the drug to the finish line. And so if we don\u8217\'20\'19t have this basic research, we\u8217\'20\'19re not going to be able to identify new targets, and that means we\u8217\'20\'19re not going to have new therapies, and ultimately more patients may die.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Brown sees a direct link between \uc2 NIH\uc2 -funded academic research and cancer drugs for chronic lymphocytic leukemia that helped patients live longer.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople who would have died in a few months, lived for years with the first version of this drug,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of staff have been terminated from the \uc2 NCI\uc2  in recent months, including dozens of communications workers. \u8220\'20\'1COur website, cancer.gov, is used worldwide and is the ground truth for cancer information,\u8221\'20\'1D said one fired communications employee at the institute, who requested to remain anonymous. \u8220\'20\'1CScience isn\u8217\'20\'19t finished until it\u8217\'20\'19s communicated.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Between 28 February and 8 April, more than $180m in \uc2 NCI\uc2  grants were cancelled by the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 NIH\uc2  declined to comment, deferring to comments on the budget proposal cuts to the office of management and budget, which did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 NIH\uc2  did not comment on how many employees at the agency remain after several rounds of cuts and layoffs. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/may/27/trump-safety-cuts-workplace-risks-niosh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s safety research cuts heighten workplace risks, federal workers warn}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 Lavik said the cuts are likely to threaten large-scale research programs, such as the National Community Oncology Research program, which covers community hospitals all over the US and ensures patients have access to clinical trials, cutting edge cancer care, prevention and screenings.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am deeply concerned about the future of these really important clinical trials programs that are really hard to rebuild if you stop them,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIn the prevention program, there are large scale screening trials, and they have large data sets. We were working really hard on policies to make those data sets more accessible and available to the research community. And we\u8217\'20\'19re all gone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Drastic cuts across federal science funding is causing scientists to consider leaving science and eliminating opportunities for younger scientists to enter training pipelines as undergraduate researchers, graduate researchers and postdocs, according to Lavik.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe things that are transformative are fundamentally high-risk, high-reward research,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe start to move into the clinic, and that\u8217\'20\'19s what leads to the new kinds of treatments that don\u8217\'20\'19t just help a little bit, but really change the face of how we treat patients, how we prevent cancers, how we treat other conditions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou have to be willing to do lots and lots of things that don\u8217\'20\'19t work. There are so many ways we should be more efficient about what we do. But to do that, you really need those young scientists, those new people in the field; you need the people who\u8217\'20\'19ve come into the \uc2 NIH\uc2  and the \uc2 NCI\uc2 , who are thinking differently about doing things and willing to take those big swings.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250626el6q001s1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
