\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 America had open borders until 1924. Racism and corporate greed changed that\b0\par\par\uc2 Daniel Mendiola \par \uc2 1,468 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 June 2025\par 13:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The primary justifications for early immigration laws were xenophobia, eugenics, and overt racism. Understanding the history of immigration is essential\par \par \uc2 The US immigration system is a scam that dehumanizes people for profit. Communities across the country have had enough.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/09/los-angeles-protests-visual-guide"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 protests in Los Angeles}}}\uc2  have invited a long overdue conversation about the true nature of the US immigration system. While the immediate catalysts for the protests were ramped up {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/09/los-angeles-immigration-raids-detained"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ice raids}}}\uc2  attempting to meet \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s arbitrary deportation {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/may/29/trump-ice-arrest-quota"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quotas}}}\uc2 , the protests spring from a deeper history.\par \par \uc2 In reality, the protests reflect decades-long frustrations with an abusive immigration system designed to dehumanize immigrants, weaken workers and keep wealth flowing upward. Ice\u8217\'20\'19s recent tactics were only the last straw.\par \par \uc2 Excellent articles have shed light on why Los Angeles in particular, with generations of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/12/los-angeles-ice-protests-immigration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 immigrant communities}}}\uc2  and a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/11/los-angeles-protest-history"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 history of immigrant rights}}}\uc2  movements, has emerged as an epicenter of resistance. Whether immigrants themselves, or families, neighbors, coworkers, or friends of immigrants, people in these communities have long experienced the trauma of a system that renders people \u8220\'20\'1Cillegal\u8221\'20\'1D just for doing basic things like getting a job. Similar statements could be made for other major sites of protest such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/may/10/new-jersey-ras-baraka-ice"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New Jersey}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/13/new-york-protest-trump-immigration-policies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/09/chicago-immigrants-trump-mass-deportation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Chicago}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/09/protests-trump-ice-raids-colorado"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Denver}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2025/06/08/texas-ice-protests-houston-san-antonio/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Houston}}}\uc2 . <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2025/jun/13/los-angeles-protests-trump-alex-padilla"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 How would recent events in America appear if they happened elsewhere? | Moira Donegan}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 While much of the news coverage has turned toward the US president\u8217\'20\'19s mobilization of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/11/trump-administration-news-updates-today"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 military}}}\uc2  and what that means for his growing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/10/trump-administration-los-angeles-national-guard"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 authoritarian}}}\uc2  tendencies, this is only half the story. To fully understand what is at stake in the protests, we can\u8217\'20\'19t lose sight of the thing that drove people to protest in the first place: a violently unfair immigration system that is an affront to us all.\par \par \uc2 It is worth noting that this immigration system is not an original component of US governance. Whereas the first government under the US constitution formed in 1789, there were no federal immigration laws until the Chinese Exclusion Act of 1882, and even this law was limited in the sense that it banned a specific class of immigrants. The US did not have closed borders until the Immigration Act of 1924, which established national origins quotas across the board.\par \par \uc2 The primary justifications for these early immigration laws were xenophobia, eugenics, and overt racism. By the 1990s, however, multinational corporations understood that closed borders \u8211\'20\'13 especially combined with free trade agreements freeing multinational companies to shop around for \u8220\'20\'1Ccheap\u8221\'20\'1D workers, while at the same time constraining the options of workers to move around and look for better jobs \u8211\'20\'13 were a powerful weapon in their arsenal to squeeze ever more profit out of global supply chains. While cleverly hidden behind discourses of \u8220\'20\'1Csecurity\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Csovereignty,\u8221\'20\'1D our immigration system is actually a scam rigged to guarantee an upward flow of wealth at the cost of human rights.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2  (Nafta) illustrates this dynamic. Signed in 1992, Nafta created a free trade zone among Mexico, Canada and the US, specifically making it easier for goods, capital and corporations to move freely while conspicuously ignoring the movement of workers. Far from an oversight, as the scholar {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tupress.temple.edu/books/ethical-borders"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bill Ong Hing has written}}}\uc2 , this was the whole point of the agreement.\par \par \uc2 While no US labor unions or other human rights representatives had a seat at the table, the US advisory committee for trade and negotiations \u8211\'20\'13 composed almost entirely of representatives of multinational corporations \u8211\'20\'13 led the negotiations, ensuring that the agreement followed corporate interests. The drafters wanted easier access to cheaper Mexican labor, but they understood that if Mexicans had the same rights as companies to cross borders in search of better opportunities, then the \u8220\'20\'1Cinvisible hand\u8221\'20\'1D of supply and demand might make this labor less cheap. Accordingly, immigration restrictions helped to rig the game. In line with these interests, the Clinton administration, in power when the agreement took effect in 1994, not only went along with the plan to leave immigrants out of the deal, but also doubled down on closed borders with harsh new measures to restrict immigration through the Illegal Immigration Reform and Immigrant Responsibility Act (IIRIRA) of 1996.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, Nafta and the IIRIRA worked hand-in-hand to trap Mexican workers and give artificial negotiating advantages to multinational corporations. The mechanism made sure that Mexicans would have to either stay put on their side of the border and tolerate whatever working conditions were available, or live without legal status if they did \u8220\'20\'1Cvote with their feet\u8221\'20\'1D to seek better opportunities in the US. In either case, they were far more vulnerable to exploitation. Unsurprisingly, this {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2014/jan/04/nafta-20-years-mexico-regret"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 harmed workers}}}\uc2  all around, especially Mexicans, leading to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://carnegieendowment.org/posts/2004/02/mexican-employment-productivity-and-income-a-decade-after-nafta?lang=en"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stagnant wages}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thenation.com/article/archive/the-maquiladora-workers-of-juarez-find-their-voice/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 harsh working}}}\uc2  conditions, and irregular migration that forced people into an exploitative informal economy, even as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aflcio.org/reports/nafta-20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 productivity}}}\uc2  and corporate profits soared.\par \par \uc2 Significantly, Nafta was not an isolated case, but rather an embodiment of how the US immigration system enshrines this major power imbalance between labor and capital. In fact, the same Clinton administration and private sector advisory committee that oversaw the implementation \uc2 Nafta\uc2  also played a key role in creating the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2  in 1995 following similar principles. Today, multinational corporations continue to move freely around the world, while people seeking a better life continue to face restrictive borders enforced by state violence. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2025/jun/11/the-guardian-view-on-trump-and-deportation-protests-the-king-of-confected-emergencies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Guardian view on Trump and deportation protests: the king of confected emergencies | Editorial}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 At the same time, we as taxpayers pay increasingly absurd sums of money for the violent border security measures that keep this system in place. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.americanimmigrationcouncil.org/research/the-cost-of-immigration-enforcement-and-border-security"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 American Immigration Council}}}\uc2  has calculated that since 1994, the annual budget for the US Border Patrol has risen from $400m dollars to more than $7bn in 2024 \u8211\'20\'13 an increase of over 700% even when factoring in inflation. They further estimate that since the creation of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  in 2003, the federal government has spent more than $400bn dollars on the various agencies that carry out immigration enforcement.\par \par \uc2 Under the current Trump administration, these numbers are set to soar even further. In the same \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2025/may/26/the-guardian-view-on-trumps-big-beautiful-bill-reject-the-con-of-a-class-war-manifesto"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 big, beautiful}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D spending bill that is already facing backlash for slashing public programs while offering enormous tax cuts to the wealthiest Americans, a massive increase in spending for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s signature deportation plan is included. The \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  (CBO) estimates that this will add $168bn to the deficit over the next five years \u8211\'20\'13 already an extreme amount \u8211\'20\'13 though the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cato.org/blog/deportations-add-almost-1-trillion-costs-gops-big-beautiful-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cato Institute}}}\uc2  has noted that the CBO calculation left out key variables. In fact, Cato finds that the number could actually be closer to $1tn.\par \par \uc2 In short, our immigration system is a massive grift. It divides communities, separates families, hurts workers, and subjects people to state violence for doing normal things like working at an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2025/05/31/nx-s1-5418970/san-diego-immigration-raid-restaurant"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Italian restaurant}}}\uc2  or going to church on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.boston25news.com/news/local/video-shows-ice-agents-detain-man-chelsea-mothers-day-tensions-high-amid-several-operations/NW4HJT4QCREDTKOOGFK75X6AUU/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mother\u8217\'20\'19s Day}}}\uc2 . And we as taxpayers subsidize the companies profiting on this abusive system.\par \par \uc2 As I have previously written, the Trump administration has distinguished itself from previous governments by intentionally targeting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2025/jun/05/trump-legal-immigration-crusade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legal immigrants}}}\uc2 . However, as protesters flood the streets with signs saying \u8220\'20\'1CNo One is Illegal,\u8221\'20\'1D the deeper significance of this protest movement becomes clear. The message is that someone\u8217\'20\'19s right to life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t depend on their immigration status. And it certainly shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t depend on the whims of multinational corporations who have essentially coopted violent border enforcement for their own profits.\par \par \uc2 As a final thought, I think people are also tired of all the gaslighting. Despite the barrage of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/01/protecting-the-american-people-against-invasion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 official rhetoric}}}\uc2  claiming that tough immigration measures are for our own good \u8211\'20\'13 that they make our {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.migrationpolicy.org/article/deportations-unintended-consequences"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 communities safer}}}\uc2 , that they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/economy/archive/2024/10/immigration-working-class-wages/680128/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 protect jobs}}}\uc2 , that we shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t feel bad because immigrants don\u8217\'20\'19t deserve to be treated as we would want to be treated ourselves \u8211\'20\'13 we know from both academic analysis and our lived experiences that these are all vicious lies, and the policies that spring from these lies have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/feb/04/us-mexico-border-migrant-deaths-volunteers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deadly}}}\uc2  consequences for real human beings. For me, the recent protests demonstrate that communities across the country are standing up to reject these lies.\par \par \uc2 As I think about the significance of this movement, I am reminded of a passage from Karla Cornejo Villavicencio\u8217\'20\'19s 2020 book {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/558229/the-undocumented-americans-by-karla-cornejo-villavicencio/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Undocumented Americans}}}\uc2 . Reflecting on the power of storytelling as a counterweight to the deluge of dehumanizing assaults immigrants face on a daily basis, she concludes: \u8220\'20\'1CWhat if this is how, in the face of so much sacrilege and slander, we reclaim our dead?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 People are protesting because they are fed up. And they are right to be.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Daniel Mendiola is a professor of Latin American history and migration studies at \uc2 Vassar College\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250617el6h0018n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
