\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 At \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 , Ice raids terrorize the workers who helped build LA: \u8216\'20\'18They just come and grab you\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Maanvi Singh in Los Angeles \par \uc2 1,329 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2025\par 17:33 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Undocumented laborers have long gathered in the chain\u8217\'20\'19s parking lots hoping to get hired, but now there is a \u8216\'20\'18terrible fear\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The white vans zipped into the parking lot of the \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  in central Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Pedro watched from the corner as masked federal immigration agents emerged before grabbing and handcuffing people.\par \par \uc2 There were a hundred or so day laborers milling about the home improvement megastore\u8217\'20\'19s sprawling parking lot \u8211\'20\'13 soliciting construction work from homeowners and contractors \u8211\'20\'13 plus \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  shoppers and a number of food vendors. Everyone was suddenly frantic.\par \par \uc2 It took a few seconds for Pedro to grasp what he was seeing. Unlike most of the other workers and vendors there, the 27-year-old from Mexico had a legal residency status, so he jumped up to help, trying to usher away workers. Schools were out for the summer, so a couple of the vendors had brought their children with them that day. \u8220\'20\'1CImagine \u8211\'20\'13 young children,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey all started to run.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That raid \u8211\'20\'13 and subsequent ones at a nearby garment manufacturer in downtown LA \u8211\'20\'13 sparked massive protests in LA, which the Trump administration tried to quell by mobilizing thousands of national guard troops. It also disrupted and destabilized the little economy at the \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  parking lot, along Wilshire Boulevard and Burlington Avenue, in ways that Pedro said he\u8217\'20\'19s still trying to understand.\par \par \uc2 In the days after, vendors selling lunch and fruits stopped coming. Hardly any workers came. \u8220\'20\'1CLook \u8211\'20\'13 nothing, just silence,\u8221\'20\'1D Pedro said as he gestured around. \u8220\'20\'1CI have never seen anything like this before. Not here. Never here in LA.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The immigration enforcement raids in California have been targeting undocumented immigrant workers all across southern California \u8211\'20\'13 at donut shops, car washes, factories and farms.\par \par \uc2 But among the most common sites have been Home Depots. The chain has long maintained an unofficial, symbiotic relationship with the undocumented laborers who gather in store parking lots, hoping to get hired for a day of painting, landscaping or roofing.\par \par \uc2 Federal agents trialled the tactic in January, when they rounded up immigrants at a \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  in California\u8217\'20\'19s agricultural Kern county. Over the past week, agents have visited \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  parking lots in the LA suburbs of Huntington Park, Santa Ana and Whittier.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese workers, this community, has been here for decades,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jorge Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s, a senior organizer at a day labor center called Central American Resource Center (Carecen). The group maintains a resource center for workers abutting the \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  lot in LA\u8217\'20\'19s Westlake neighborhood, near downtown. On Fridays, the organization distributes food there \u8211\'20\'13 but since the raid, they have stopped, to avoid putting workers and families at risk.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of emotions, a lot of sadness,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 There is also indignation. The raids have entrapped the very immigrant workers who have built, roofed, painted and wired much of the city, he said. Many of these workers have been helping clean and rebuild communities in LA that burned in fires that raged across the region this winter.\par \par \uc2 Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s was nearby when the agents arrived on Friday.\par \par \uc2 For laborers who escaped military juntas and gang violence in their home countries, the masked, armed agents triggered painful flashbacks to cartel kidnappings. \u8220\'20\'1CIt looked like a war zone,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers said they had been blocked from speaking to immigrants who were held in the basement of federal administrative buildings in downtown LA for days, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/11/la-ice-raids-immigration-conditions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 without adequate food or water}}}\uc2 . Some were transferred to detention centers in California\u8217\'20\'19s high desert, or in Texas. At least a handful of people were deported to Mexico almost immediately, according to lawyers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  (DHS) flatly denied that attorneys had been barred from seeing detained clients. \u8220\'20\'1CThese allegations are FALSE,\u8221\'20\'1D spokesperson Tricia McLaughlin said, despite {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/11/la-ice-raids-immigration-conditions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 documented evidence}}}\uc2  that attorneys and even {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/09/democrats-california-new-york-detention-facilities"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 congressional representatives}}}\uc2  were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/RepJimmyGomez/status/1931482451630563343"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 barred}}}\uc2  from speaking to detainees this week.\par \par \uc2 In the days following the raids, Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s and other advocates struggled to track the day laborers who had been arrested. \u8220\'20\'1CIt really does look like a kidnapping,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Agents have picked off people with brown skin seemingly at random, said Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s. \u8220\'20\'1CEven workers with legal status are worried.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18It is a terrible fear\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Eduardo saw another raid unfold at a \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  in Huntington Park, a city where {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://censusreporter.org/profiles/16000US0636056-huntington-park-ca/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nearly}}}\uc2  97% of residents are Latino or Hispanic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey just come and grab you, like grabbing a baby,\u8221\'20\'1D said the 45-year-old day laborer. \u8220\'20\'1CThey don\u8217\'20\'19t ask any questions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He watched the whole thing from inside his flatbed truck. It was so fast \u8211\'20\'13 all of a sudden, a rush of vans, and a flood of armed agents.\par \par \uc2 He got lucky \u8211\'20\'13 they didn\u8217\'20\'19t look his way.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is difficult to describe. It is a terrible fear,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels like a sandbag falling on your body. Your vision starts to darken \u8211\'20\'13 because the American dream is escaping you at that moment. And all you can do is dream of your land, imagine what it would be to go back after so many years.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Eduardo came to the US from Honduras 18 years ago. His older daughter is almost 24, and his younger is nine, and they were worried about him returning to the \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  on Thursday. He was worried, too. \u8220\'20\'1CBut they already came, and they left me alone,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, shrugging. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd I need the work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Only two other laborers and a couple of food vendors decided to take the risk that day. There seemed to be fewer \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  shoppers as well.\par \par \uc2 Across the lot, Carlos, 48, was scanning the half-empty scene. On a typical day, he would make $200, maybe even $300, selling fruit out of the back of his minivan. But since the raids began, he\u8217\'20\'19s scarcely seen a customer. He expects he\u8217\'20\'19ll make maybe $50 this week, if he\u8217\'20\'19s lucky.\par \par \uc2 He was at home when the raid happened, and friends messaged him to warn him not to come. He returned to the \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  the next day, despite the objections from his nine-year-old son, who was terrified he wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t come back. But he\u8217\'20\'19s a single dad, and he has to make rent. \u8220\'20\'1CI told him God watches over us,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Everything is different since the raid\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Across the city, day laborers and street vendors were similarly weighing the dangers of showing up to work against the risks of staying home and losing income.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday in Paramount \u8211\'20\'13 a predominantly Latino suburb of LA, where the presence of federal agents last Saturday triggered a roaring protest \u8211\'20\'13 there were no workers outside the local \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But larger numbers of customers and workers had begun to return to \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  in the Westlake neighborhood \u8211\'20\'13 which was among several locations hit by federal agents in central LA on Friday \u8211\'20\'13 after volunteers showed up this week to patrol the street corners for Ice agents. \u8220\'20\'1CNow we\u8217\'20\'19re ready for them,\u8221\'20\'1D said Diego, a 75-year-old day laborer and sometime flower vendor from Guatemala.\par \par \uc2 Still, everybody was on edge. There was far less work than usual, far fewer customers slowing their cars. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen someone does stop, there\u8217\'20\'19s like a mountain of workers rushing over,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd usually they just want to hire one or two people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even if the lot looks the same as always, said Daniel, 45, it\u8217\'20\'19s not. \u8220\'20\'1CEverything is different since the raid. Nothing is the same,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 He plans to look for work here for as long as he can. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are here because of the luck of the draw,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe do not know what is going to happen from here on.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Note: \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  is not using the full names of any workers in this piece, to protect their privacy and safety\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250616el6g001jl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
