\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Tariff-hit firms should review bonuses or risk backlash, US lawyers warn\b0\par\par\uc2 Kalyeena Makortoff Banking correspondent \par \uc2 688 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 June 2025\par 17:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Partners at \uc2 Cooley\uc2  urge companies to \u8216\'20\'18tread carefully\u8217\'20\'19 and avoid big executive payouts while workers suffer\par \par \uc2 Businesses hit by tariffs should start reviewing their bonus policies and how deeply they may need to cut executive payouts if they hope to avoid a public backlash, lawyers have said.\par \par \uc2 Partners at the Silicon Valley law firm Cooley said that while pay was probably the last thing on bosses\u8217\'20\'19 minds as they scramble to adapt to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/02/us-firms-say-trump-trade-war-is-hitting-production-as-dollar-nears-three-year-low"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s unpredictable tariff policies}}}\uc2 , pay committees should start assessing their options soon.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMany will encounter rising material costs and reduced profit margins, particularly given the significant pressure on the supply chain,\u8221\'20\'1D a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://corpgov.law.harvard.edu/2025/05/20/compensation-arrangement-considerations-in-light-of-2025-tariffs/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 memo by its top lawyers}}}\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTaken together with the recent stock market volatility, companies (both public and private) will need to address the impact of these challenges on their business and, importantly, consider the effects on director and executive compensation programs.\u8221\'20\'1D <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/02/us-firms-say-trump-trade-war-is-hitting-production-as-dollar-nears-three-year-low"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US firms say Trump trade war is hitting production as dollar nears three-year low}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 Cooley\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 whose clients have included Netflix, \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 , and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  before its takeover by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and rebrand as X \u8211\'20\'13 said that without a proper review of pay policies, companies may end up handing big bonuses to bosses while the rest of the workforce suffers from cost cuts and job losses.\par \par \uc2 That may be due to remuneration policies that do not automatically account for the impact of so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cblack swan events\u8221\'20\'1D, such as a dramatic U-turn in US trade policy or a global pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Michael Bergmann, a partner at \uc2 Cooley\uc2 , told the Guardian: \u8220\'20\'1CThere is a meaningful risk that rank-and-file employees are going to suffer here, and that could take the form of layoffs, folks losing their jobs or wages.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The memo said: \u8220\'20\'1CGiven the overall macroeconomic and sociopolitical climate, there is a strong risk that any accommodation for those [tariff] impacts will be viewed as unduly rewarding executives, particularly if rank-and-file employees do not benefit in a proportionate way. Accordingly, compensation committees and boards must tread deliberately and carefully.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cooley lawyers said there was also a possibility that executives might be unduly punished for tariff impacts that were beyond their control, and that this should also be considered by remuneration committees. It is likely that pay packets will start to reflect tariff impacts from early next year.\par \par \uc2 Trump rolled out sweeping tariffs against most countries in April, in a shock move that left companies looking at how they could offset a potential hit to trade and revenues.\par \par \uc2 The US president later paused and then reduced most tariffs, but last week he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/04/trump-tariffs-steel-aluminum"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 refocused attention on steel and aluminium imports}}}\uc2 , doubling tariffs for all countries except the UK to 50%. Tech companies have also suffered as a result of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s trade war with Beijing, which has included an order {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/may/29/tech-shares-climb-after-strong-nvidia-results-despite-warning-over-rise-of-chinese-rivals"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 restricting AI chip exports to China}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While Cooley\u8217\'20\'19s original memo was aimed at US companies, Bergmann said the warning was relevant to others around the world, including in the UK, with the ripple effects also due to hurt businesses even if they do not directly trade with US companies. \u8220\'20\'1CThe company\u8217\'20\'19s nationality is not really the driving question here,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Luke Hildyard\uc2 , the director of the High Pay Centre thinktank, suggested the tariff fallout should prompt a more fundamental review of executives\u8217\'20\'19 multimillion-pound pay packets.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe entire flawed premise of vast executive pay awards is that business success is driven by a tiny number of business leaders, and that companies should pay whatever it takes to attract and retain the best ones,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe tariff furore is only the latest example \u8211\'20\'13 following energy supply challenges in Europe, the pandemic or the supply chain issues in its aftermath \u8211\'20\'13 of business leaders being pretty powerless in the face of wider events. Maybe the most useful lesson here is that they are mortals like the rest of us and more moderate pay awards that reflect that fact would be appropriate.