\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  calls for big cut to US interest rates after jobs report shows hiring slowdown \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 6,538 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 June 2025\par 15:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 US economy added slightly more jobs than expected in May, but March and April\u8217\'20\'19s data was revised lower\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 <a href="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/jun/05/tesla-share-drop-trump-musk-feud">Tesla"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/jun/05/tesla-share-drop-trump-musk-feud">Tesla}}}\uc2  share plunge amid Trump feud wipes $152bn off \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s company</a>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T15:06:10.867Z">4.06pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time to wrap up.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has intensified his call for cuts to US borrowing costs{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/jun/06/uk-house-prices-drop-in-may-markets-us-jobs-report-musk-tesla-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-6842f25d8f08d8006b3736c6#block-6842f25d8f08d8006b3736c6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 , urging the Federal Reserve to lower interest rates by a full percentage point}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Trump pushed for a cut after the latest jobs report, showing that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/jun/06/uk-house-prices-drop-in-may-markets-us-jobs-report-musk-tesla-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-6842de088f08d8006b37358a#block-6842de088f08d8006b37358a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the US added 139,000 new jobs in May}}}\uc2 , more than expected.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/jun/06/uk-house-prices-drop-in-may-markets-us-jobs-report-musk-tesla-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-6842edb68f08edac2d86993f#block-6842edb68f08edac2d86993f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Stocks rallied in New York}}}\uc2  after the jobs report was released.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/jun/06/uk-house-prices-drop-in-may-markets-us-jobs-report-musk-tesla-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-6842b7c98f08d8006b373284#block-6842b7c98f08d8006b373284"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The price of silver has hit a 13-year high}}}\uc2 \u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 \u8230\'20\'26while {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/jun/06/uk-house-prices-drop-in-may-markets-us-jobs-report-musk-tesla-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-6842a0688f0837940802edba#block-6842a0688f0837940802edba"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 food commodity prices have dipped}}}\uc2 \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 \u8230\'20\'26and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/jun/06/uk-house-prices-drop-in-may-markets-us-jobs-report-musk-tesla-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-684286368f088672b8b66461#block-684286368f088672b8b66461"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK house prices fell last month}}}\uc2 . <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jun/06/uk-house-prices-fall-by-more-than-expected-amid-economic-uncertainty"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK house prices fall by more than expected amid economic uncertainty}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T14:54:54.782Z">3.54pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Russia\u8217\'20\'19s central bank\uc2  surprised the markets today, by cutting its key interest rate by a full percentage point.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Russia lowered rates from 21% to 20%, citing easing inflationary pressure.\par \par \uc2 It explained:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCurrent inflationary pressures, including underlying ones, continue to decline. While domestic demand growth is still outstripping the capabilities to expand the supply of goods and services, the Russian economy is gradually returning to a balanced growth path.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At a press conference following the decision Governor \uc2 Elvira Nabiullina\uc2  dismissed a suggestion that the regulator had given in to political pressure to reduce rates.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T14:44:43.963Z">3.44pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has claimed that today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs numbers are \u8220\'20\'1Cgreat\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Posting on Truth Social, the president declares:\par \par \uc2 GREAT JOB NUMBERS, STOCK MARKET UP BIG! AT THE SAME TIME, BILLIONS POURING IN FROM TARIFFS!!!\par \par \uc2 [reminder: Total nonfarm payroll employment in the US increased by 139,000 in May, a little higher than forecast, but lower than the average monthly gain of 149,000 over the prior 12 months.\par \par \uc2 It also wasn\u8217\'20\'19t \u8216\'20\'18great\u8217\'20\'19 news that March and April\u8217\'20\'19s jobs data was revised down, showing that 95,000 fewer jobs were created than previously estimated].\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T13:58:57.209Z">2.58pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Trump calls for big cut to US interest rates\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has taken a break from squabbling with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to call for a hefty cut to US interest rates.\par \par \uc2 In a post on Truth Social, Trump claims that Federal Reserve chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 who he has nicknamed \u8220\'20\'1CToo Late\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 is a \u8220\'20\'1Cdisaster\u8221\'20\'1D for not having cut earlier.\par \par \uc2 Following today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/jun/06/uk-house-prices-drop-in-may-markets-us-jobs-report-musk-tesla-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-6842de088f08d8006b37358a#block-6842de088f08d8006b37358a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 showing a slowdown in hiring}}}\uc2 , the US president argued that the Fed should cut rates by a whole percentage point \u8211\'20\'13 which would be a fairly dramatic reduction.\par \par \uc2 Trump posted:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToo Late\u8221\'20\'1D at the Fed is a disaster! Europe has had 10 rate cuts, we have had none. Despite him, our Country is doing great. Go for a full point, \uc2 Rocket Fuel\uc2 !\par \par \uc2 [Fact check, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ecb.europa.eu/stats/policy_and_exchange_rates/key_ecb_interest_rates/html/index.en.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Europe has had eight cuts}}}\uc2  since the \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2  started easing monetary policy a year ago, with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jun/05/european-central-bank-ecb-interest-rate-cut-economic-growth-eurozone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 latest coming yesterday}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The US Fed hasn\u8217\'20\'19t cut rates {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/dec/18/fed-cuts-interest-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 since last December}}}\uc2  (the month before Trump\u8217\'20\'19s second inauguration), when it lowered its target range to between 4.25% and 4.5%.] <figure class="element element-interactive interactive" data-interactive="https://interactive.guim.co.uk/embed/iframe-wrapper/0.1/boot.js" data-canonical-url="https://interactive.guim.co.uk/datawrapper/embed/EFiyq/2/" data-alt="A chart showing US interest rates"> {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://interactive.guim.co.uk/datawrapper/embed/EFiyq/2/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Interactive}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 In a second post, Trump says a Fed rate cut would lower the interest rates on \uc2 US government\uc2  debt, making borrowing cheaper.\par \par \uc2 He writes:\par \par \uc2 If \u8220\'20\'1CToo Late\u8221\'20\'1D at the Fed would CUT, we would greatly reduce interest rates, long and short, on debt that is coming due. Biden went mostly short term. There is virtually no inflation (anymore), but if it should come back, RAISE \u8220\'20\'1CRATE\u8221\'20\'1D TO COUNTER. Very Simple!!! He is costing our Country a fortune. Borrowing costs should be MUCH LOWER!!!\par \par \uc2 [Factcheck: the US inflation rate dipped to 2.3% per year in April, slightly above the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s 2% target.\par \par \uc2 Also: the fear that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s trade war will be inflationary is making the Fed wary of cutting rates, which is keeping bond yields (interest rates) high]\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-06-06T14:06:02.128Z">at 3.06pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T13:41:57.857Z">2.41pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shares gain 5% in early trading\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 Tesla\uc2  have opened higher, after a torrid session yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s stock has risen by 5.5% in early trading to around $300, recovering around a third of yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s 14% tumble during the escalating clash between \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . <figure class="element element-tweet" data-canonical-url="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/outpostosint/status/1930983059006087480"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/outpostosint/status/1930983059006087480}}}\uc2 ">\par \par \uc2 Trend:\u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shares rise nearly 5% after 14% drop yesterday due to Musk-Trump conflict. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/6b67tLMg9e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/6b67tLMg9e}}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Outpost (@outpostosint) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/outpostosint/status/1930983059006087480?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 6, 2025}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shareholders may be hoping that relations can be patched up. But\u8230\'20\'26 the US president may not be ready to make peace. Trump has told ABC News that he is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot particularly\u8221\'20\'1D interested in talking to Musk right now, claiming the billionaire has \u8220\'20\'1Clost his mind\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 According to the Wall Street Journal, Trump is even considering getting rid of his red {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/tesla"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tesla}}}\uc2  too, which he acquired back in March. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/jun/06/donald-trump-elon-musk-feud-steve-bannon-jeffery-epstein-us-politics-live-news-updates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump reportedly considers getting rid of his Tesla; Musk loses $33bn in net worth amid feud \u8211\'20\'13 live}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T13:35:41.939Z">2.35pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Wall Street opens higher\par \par \uc2 The opening bell on the New York stock market has been rung, and stocks are rising.\par \par \uc2 The main indices are all up in early trading, with the Dow Jones industrial average up 474 points, or +1.1%, at 42,794 points.\par \par \uc2 The broader S&P 500 share index, and the tech-focused Nasdaq , are both up around 1% too.\par \par \uc2 Stocks are gaining despite predictions that today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report will deter the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  from any imminent interest rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 As Scott Helfstein, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.globalxetfs.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Global X}}}\uc2 \u8216\'20\'18s head of investment strategy, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn the ongoing saga of the White House versus the Fed, this jobs report continues to back the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s patience strategy.\par \par \uc2 That said, the White House set the stage for policy uncertainty and economic volatility in the first place. The Fed is likely to remain on hold through the end of summer to see how tariff negotiations proceed and ensure prices are stabilizing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T13:09:12.758Z">2.09pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Investor: US jobs market is in limbo\par \par \uc2 Byron Anderson , head of fixed income at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://email2.zitopartners.com/c/eJwUyj2OKyEMAODTQDmyzY-HguI1uYcNJoleMhMRsivt6Vfbf71mkILsrSLHQpgoJn-rbZce96yoe8E4ek4a--AeNAbKMvy9ElCCDBkDMKVNxzAYwJY45x7QRfi5r_Mlcx0231s7n_5Rb2u93i78c3RxdHnIGDaXHdeHzT_h6OJnvU6xp23fJrPb4SJcPzL7XY6tndvnv181J2JWsKhYqLRswEBIDYopJ2G_qmgAK4l7C4KGDZl3MUVl0Bas-K9KvwEAAP__jABK9Q"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laffer Tengler Investments}}}\uc2  , warns that uncertainty is holding back the US jobs market:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith the amount of uncertainty in the economy, it is not surprising that hiring is slowing. If there is a bright spot, it is that the unemployment rate didn\u8217\'20\'19t increase. The US isn\u8217\'20\'19t hiring, but they aren\u8217\'20\'19t firing either, which will only last so long if we do not see a resolution to the larger issues.\par \par \uc2 Limbo is not a great spot for business and job creation. Who would feel comfortable with the Truther in Chief that can flip the economy on its head with one ill-advised tweet.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T13:06:15.990Z">2.06pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Stephen Brown , deputy chief North America economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  , has spotted signs that the Trump trade war hit the jobs market last month:\par \par \uc2 The contributions to the 139,000 rise in payrolls in May were unusually narrow, with 78,000 of the gain from health care & social assistance and another 48,000 from leisure & hospitality.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the 8,000 drop in manufacturing and 6,500 decline in retail point to some limited negative tariff effects. That said, it was striking that transportation & warehousing employment rebounded by 5,800, despite reports of sharp declines in job openings amid lower imports.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T12:52:51.725Z">1.52pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Wealth Club: US jobs market shrugs off tariffs\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report shows the US labor market has shrugged off the tariff uncertainty that rocked global stock and bond markets in April and May, reports Nicholas Hyett, investment manager at Wealth Club:\par \par \uc2 While the Federal government has continued to shed a small number of jobs, the wider economy has more than made up the difference, with the US adding slightly more jobs than expected in May. Wage growth also came in higher than expected \u8211\'20\'13 suggesting the economy is in rude health.\par \par \uc2 That will be taken as vindication by the Trump administration \u8211\'20\'13 which has been clear that the tariffs are aimed squarely at supporting Main Street rather than pleasing Wall Street. Less positive from the White Houses\u8217\'20\'19 point of view is that a strong economy and rising wages gives the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  less reason to cut interest rates \u8211\'20\'13 pushing yields a touch higher and making the fiscal splurge built into Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1CBig Beautiful Bill\u8221\'20\'1D that bit more expensive.\par \par \uc2 With rate cuts looking less likely, Fed Chair \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  can expect to remain firmly in the President\u8217\'20\'19s firing line once the spat with Musk is over.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 [of course, had Trump not {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/10/trump-tariffs-retreat-analysis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 u-turned on his initial plans for higher tariffs}}}\uc2 , today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs data might be worse\u8230\'20\'26.]\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T12:49:58.742Z">1.49pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 US annual hourly earnings up 3.9%\par \par \uc2 Earnings grew faster than expected across the US economy last month \u8211\'20\'13 a boost to workers, but a potential worry for central bankers.\par \par \uc2 Average hourly earnings for all employees on private nonfarm payrolls rose by 15 cents, or 0.4%, to $36.24 in May, today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report shows. Economists had forecast a smaller rise, of 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 3.9%, ahead of forecasts of a 3.7% rise.\par \par \uc2 April\u8217\'20\'19s annual wage growth data has been revised up too, from 3.8% to 3.9%.\par \par \uc2 Higher wages can lead to stickier inflation, which may deter the Federal Reserve from cutting US interest rates despite mounting pressure from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . <figure class="element element-tweet" data-canonical-url="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LizAnnSonders/status/1930966747999240236"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/LizAnnSonders/status/1930966747999240236}}}\uc2 ">\par \par \uc2 May average hourly earnings +3.9% y/y vs. +3.7% est. & +3.9% prior (rev up from +3.8%) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/RJopqrnDlj"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/RJopqrnDlj}}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Liz Ann Sonders (@LizAnnSonders) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LizAnnSonders/status/1930966747999240236?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 6, 2025}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T12:42:01.593Z">1.42pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 The 139,000 increase in the US non-farm payroll last month is slightly below the average monthly gain of 149,000 recorded over the prior 12 months.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T12:41:14.699Z">1.41pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Digging into the US jobs report, we can see that the labor force participation rate decreased by 0.2 percentage point to 62.4% in May.\par \par \uc2 That suggests a small increase in people neither in work nor looking for a job.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T12:39:32.875Z">1.39pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Where jobs were added, or lost\par \par \uc2 The US healthcare sector added 62,000 jobs in May, higher than the average monthly gain of 44,000 over the prior 12 months, according to today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report. Job gains occurred in hospitals (+30,000), ambulatory health care services (+29,000), and skilled nursing care facilities (+6,000).\par \par \uc2 Employment in leisure and hospitality continued to trend up in May (+48,000), largely in food services and drinking places (+30,000).\par \par \uc2 In May, social assistance employment continued to trend up (+16,000), reflecting continued growth in individual and family services (+16,000).\par \par \uc2 Federal government employment continued to decline in May (-22,000) and is down by 59,000 since January. (Employees on paid leave or receiving ongoing severance pay are counted as employed in the establishment survey, so this may not capture the full impact of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s DOGE cost-cutting drive)\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T12:33:40.840Z">1.33pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 US economy added 139,000 jobs in May\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: Hiring slowed across the US economy last month, and fewer jobs were created than previously thought in March and April too.\par \par \uc2 The US added 139,000 new jobs in May, according to the latest non-farm payroll report, with the US unemployment rate unchanged at 4.2%.\par \par \uc2 Economists had expected the non-farm payroll to rise by 130,000 jobs, so this is slightly higher than forecast.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  reports that \u8220\'20\'1Cemployment continued to trend up in health care, leisure and hospitality, and social assistance. Federal government continued to lose jobs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But, the report also reveals that 95,000 fewer jobs were created in March and April than previously estimated.\par \par \uc2 The change in total nonfarm payroll employment for March was revised down by 65,000, from +185,000 to +120,000, and the change for April was revised down by 30,000, from +177,000 to +147,000, the \uc2 BLS\uc2  says.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-06-06T12:36:11.249Z">at 1.36pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T12:07:35.478Z">1.07pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 US jobs report approaches....\par \par \uc2 Tension is building in the financial markets as investors await the latest US jobs report, due in under half an hour\u8217\'20\'19s time.\par \par \uc2 As flagged in the introduction, economists expect a hiring slowdown \u8211\'20\'13 with non-farm payrolls forecast to increase by around 130,000 for last month, down from 177k in April.\par \par \uc2 But as usual, there\u8217\'20\'19s a wide range of forecasts \u8211\'20\'13 from a low of 75,000 new jobs to a high of 190k. <figure class="element element-tweet" data-canonical-url="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LiveSquawk/status/1930958049339908206"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/LiveSquawk/status/1930958049339908206}}}\uc2 ">\par \par \uc2 US May Payrolls Seen Slowing Sharply, Keeping Fed on Cautious Path\par \par \uc2 Friday\u8217\'20\'19s US non-farm payrolls (NFP) report will conclude a volatile week, during which US President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s import tariffs dominated headlines and drove moves in global equity markets.\par \par \uc2 Economists expect\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/6o9VTq0g1r"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/6o9VTq0g1r}}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 LiveSquawk (@LiveSquawk) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LiveSquawk/status/1930958049339908206?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 6, 2025}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate is expected to remain steady at 4.2%.\par \par \uc2 Kathleen Brooks\uc2 , research director at XTB, explains:\par \par \uc2 The US economy needs to create approximately 200k jobs per month for the unemployment rate to remain stable, likewise, initial jobless claims need to stay below 260k-270k per week for the unemployment rate not to rise. The question is, will job growth slow to such an extent that the unemployment rate rises? The consensus is no, not yet. It is worth considering the economic backdrop, to determine if a downside surprise in the labour market data could materialise on Friday. Consumer sentiment has picked up in recent weeks, although it remains at low levels, business confidence has also picked up from the lows, and the layoff rate, as calculated by the Jolts survey, remains stable.\par \par \uc2 Thus, while we expect a soft May payrolls report, we do not think that it will show the labour market falling off a cliff. The de-escalation in the US/ China trade war may have helped sentiment. There remains a huge amount of uncertainty caused by the US trade tariffs, and if the US economy can generate decent jobs growth in this environment it would suggest an underlying resilience, which could boost stock markets, the dollar and overall risk sentiment.\par \par \uc2 Anything under 100k for May payrolls would be considered weak, and it may also be a sign of worse to come. Thus, Friday\u8217\'20\'19s report is all about the trajectory of the US labour market and what this means for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T12:06:52.160Z">1.06pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 NatWest\uc2  banking app is down\par \par \uc2 UK bank \uc2 NatWest\uc2  has apologised to customers after service issues left people unable to log in to their mobile app.\par \par \uc2 Customers are being urged to use online or telephone banking, or go into a branch, while it works to fix the problem.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for the bank said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are aware that customers are experiencing difficulties accessing the \uc2 NatWest\uc2  mobile banking app this morning.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re really sorry about this and working to fix it as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCustomers can still use online and telephone banking, or visit a branch.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 More than 3,000 outages were reported through services monitoring site Downdetector at about 10am on Friday.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T11:25:16.644Z">12.25pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 UK proposes lifting ban on bitcoin and crypto funds\par \par \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s financial regulator is clearing the way for UK investors to buy crypto assets such as bitcoin though low-cost ETF-style funds.\par \par \uc2 The Financial Conduct Authority is proposing to lift the ban on offering crypto exchange traded notes (cETNs) to retail investors.\par \par \uc2 The change would mean that cETNs could be sold to individual consumers, rather than just professional investors, in the UK, if they\u8217\'20\'19re traded on an \uc2 FCA\uc2 -approved investment exchange.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FCA\uc2  notes that similar products are already available in other countries.\par \par \uc2 David Geale, executive director of payments and digital assets at the \uc2 FCA\uc2  said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis consultation demonstrates our commitment to supporting the growth and competitiveness of the UK\u8217\'20\'19s crypto industry. We want to rebalance our approach to risk and lifting the ban would allow people to make the choice on whether such a high-risk investment is right for them given they could lose all their money.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This feels like a change of position from the regulator, which has previously warned that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/jan/11/bitcoin-be-prepared-to-lose-all-your-money-fca-warns-consumers-risk-productis-cryptoassets"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 crypto investors should be prepared to lose all their money}}}\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T11:02:37.035Z">12.02pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 The UK boss of Tata Steet is urging the \uc2 British government\uc2  to secure its trade deal with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as soon as possible, amid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2025/jun/05/fears-tata-steel-could-be-excluded-from-starmer-trump-tariff-deal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fears it could miss out on tariff-free access to the US.}}}\par \par \uc2 It was reported yesterday that \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2 , which runs the vast Port Talbot steelworks in south Wales, could breach US import rules that require all steel to be \u8220\'20\'1Cmelted and poured\u8221\'20\'1D in the country from which it is imported.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s because \uc2 Tata\uc2  is curently importing steel from its sister companies in India and Europe to be finished in the UK, having shut down its blast furnace at Port Talbot last year as it shifts to a greener electric arc furnace. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2025/jun/05/fears-tata-steel-could-be-excluded-from-starmer-trump-tariff-deal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fears Tata Steel could be excluded from Starmer\u8217\'20\'19s Trump tariff deal}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 Rajesh Nair , CEO of \uc2 Tata Steel UK\uc2  says today:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are grateful for the work the \uc2 UK Government\uc2  has undertaken so far in negotiating this trade agreement with the US administration.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Tata Steel UK\uc2  will need to import steel substrate until Electric Arc Furnace steelmaking is operational in Port Talbot from late 2027 onwards. It is therefore critical for our business that melted and poured in the UK is not a requirement to access the steel quotas in any future trade deal.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven though we are not currently melting steel in the UK, we remain the largest steel producer in the country and our mills continue to transform imported steel coil and slab into high-value, specialist products which are not available from US producers and are therefore essential to our US customers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe urge the government to secure a deal as soon as possible, and we would be happy to provide the \uc2 US Government\uc2  with any needed assurances on the provenance and processing of the steel we supply.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHowever, a good deal with the US will not negate the need for an urgent review of the UK\u8217\'20\'19s own tariff rate quotas for steel to protect us from re-diverted steel flows and global overcapacity\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T10:13:44.030Z">11.13am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Silver price hits 13-year high\par \par \uc2 The price of silver has hit a 13-year high this morning.\par \par \uc2 Silver traded as high as $36.29 per ounce, its highest level since February 2012.\par \par \uc2 Achilleas Georgolopoulos , senior market analyst at Trading Point , suggests anxiety over the US-China dispute over rare earth minerals could be lifting the silver price:\par \par \uc2 Silver is stealing the limelight, as, at the time of writing, it is trading above the $36 level, recording a new 13-year high.\par \par \uc2 Some investors believe that this move could be an indication of a brighter economic outlook, given silver\u8217\'20\'19s multiple industrial uses. While that could be the case, the current upleg could also be driven by hoarding amidst the ongoing rare earth metals dispute.\par \par \uc2 As well as being a precious metal, silver also has industrial uses, in electronics, automobile components and solar panels.\par \par \uc2 Another factor may be some investors shifting out of gold into silver, which has hit a series of record highs in the last few years.\par \par \uc2 Alexander Zumpfe , a senior trader at German gold refiner Heraeus Group , told \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  yesterday that there was \u8220\'20\'1Crenewed interest from momentum-driven investors who are rotating into silver.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Zumpfe explained:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAfter lagging behind gold for several weeks, silver is now catching up,\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The weakness of the dollar has also pushed up the prices of precious metals quoted in the US currency.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T09:08:11.840Z">10.08am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Eurozone growth revised up to 0.6% in Q1 2025\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: the eurozone economy grew faster than previously estimated.\par \par \uc2 Eurozone GDP rose by 0.6% in January-March, new data from eurostat shows, twice as fast as the 0.3% growth previously estimated.\par \par \uc2 The increase has been driven by Ireland , whose economy expanded by a sizzling 9.7% in the last quarter, due to a surge of exports of products, such as pharmaceuticals, to avoid new US tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Ireland was followed by Malta (+2.1%) and Cyprus (+1.3%). The highest decreases were observed in Luxembourg (-1.0%), Slovenia (-0.8%), Denmark and Portugal (both -0.5%).\par \par \uc2 Germany expanded by 0.4%, and France by 0.1%, slower than the UK which expanded by 0.7% in the quarter.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T08:47:20.204Z">9.47am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 The owner of \uc2 Pret A Manger\uc2  is reportedly considering selling a stake in the sandwich chain ahead of a potential stock market flotation.\par \par \uc2 Luxembourg-based JAB Holding \u8211\'20\'13 which bought Pret for \u163\'00\'A31.5 billion in 2018 \u8211\'20\'13 told the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  that while it was not \u8220\'20\'1Ccurrently\u8221\'20\'1D considering a stake sale in Pret, it could look at the move with an initial public offering (IPO) in its sights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs we move closer to a potential IPO, we may evaluate bringing on a pre-IPO investor,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/95aa3684-069c-47fe-8e83-3bec20ebf904"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it told the FT}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It is thought to mark the first time Pret has publicly confirmed IPO plans for Pret, PA Media reports.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T08:30:00.088Z">9.30am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Wall Street is set to open higher in a few hours time, after falling yesterday amid the clashes between \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 share index, which fell 0.5% yesterday, is up around 0.35% in premarket trading.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shares are up over 4% in premarket trading, recovering a portion of yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s 14% tumble.\par \par \uc2 There could be hopes that the two men patch up their disagreement; overnight, hedge-fund billionaire \uc2 Bill Ackman\uc2  urged Trump and Musk to stop fighting and \u8220\'20\'1Cmake peace\u8221\'20\'1D for the benefit of the US. <figure class="element element-tweet" data-canonical-url="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BillAckman/status/1930738907081331040"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/BillAckman/status/1930738907081331040}}}\uc2 ">\par \par \uc2 I support {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/realDonaldTrump?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @realDonaldTrump}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/elonmusk?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @elonmusk}}}\uc2  and they should make peace for the benefit of our great country.\par \par \uc2 We are much stronger together than apart.\u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Bill Ackman\uc2  (@BillAckman) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BillAckman/status/1930738907081331040?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 5, 2025}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 Ion Jauregui, analyst at ActivTrades , says:\par \par \uc2 After a session marked by a sharp decline, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shares rebounded strongly in after-hours trading. The catalyst: a Politico report revealing that President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s advisors have scheduled a phone call with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  for today, Friday, in an effort to ease tensions following a public dispute between the two figures.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-06-06T08:38:34.333Z">at 9.38am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T08:18:35.489Z">9.18am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Global food prices fell in May\par \par \uc2 Global food commodity prices declined in May, driven by cheaper cereal, sugar, and vegetable oil prices.\par \par \uc2 The United Nations\u8217\'20\'19 Food and Agriculture Organization has reported that its food price index, which tracks a basket of food commodities, dipped by 1 point last month, to 127.7 points. That left prices 7.2% higher than a year ago, but almost a third below its peak in March 2022 after Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Cereal prices fell 1.8%, thanks to a record maize harvest in the US, and increasing seasonal availability from ongoing harvests in Argentina and Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Vegetable oil prices fell 3.7%, with palm, rapeseed, soy and sunflower oil prices all down.\par \par \uc2 Sugar fell 2.6%, attributed to \u8220\'20\'1Cweaker global demand for sugar, amid concerns over the uncertain global economic outlook and its potential impact on demand from the beverage and food processing industries\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Meat , though, rose 1.3%, helped by strong global import demand, particularly from China, the Middle East and Europe.\par \par \uc2 Dairy prices rose 0.8%, with international butter prices remaining at \u8220\'20\'1Chistorically high levels\u8221\'20\'1D, due to strong demand from Asia and the Middle East amid tightening milk supplies in Australia.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T07:50:28.156Z">8.50am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Leeds technology firm \uc2 Filtronic\uc2  appears to be caught in the crossfire between \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Filtronic\uc2  , which was founded by Professor David Rhodes, chair of electronic and electrical engineering at \uc2 Leeds University\uc2 , designs and manufacturers RF-to-mmWave components. They convert radio frequency signals to higher-frequency millimeter wave signals, which allow faster communication.\par \par \uc2 It has secured several deals with \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  in the past, including a $20m tie-up in February to supply parts to its Starlink satellite system.\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 Filtronic\uc2  have dropped by 10% this morning, as traders digest Trump\u8217\'20\'19s threat to terminate the governmental subsidies and contracts given to Musk\u8217\'20\'19s businesses.\par \par \uc2 Filtronic\uc2  has been on an excellent run, though. It ended trading last night at a record high, having risen from 11p a decade ago to 132p this morning.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-06-06T07:51:31.920Z">at 8.51am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T07:32:20.476Z">8.32am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 The London stock market has opened higher, heading back towards its recent record high.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 index of blue-chip shares has gained 19 points, or 0.2%, in early trading to 8830 points.\par \par \uc2 Trade war anxiety has eased slightly, after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Xi Jinping held a call yesterday.\par \par \uc2 The Footsie is now less than one hundred points away from its alltime high, 8,908 points, reached in March.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T07:13:52.993Z">8.13am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Disappointing economic data from Germany this morning has suggested that the Trump trade wars have hurt Europe\u8217\'20\'19s largest economy .\par \par \uc2 German exports sank by 1.7% in April, new data from statistics body Destatis shows, a bigger fall than expected. <figure class="element element-tweet" data-canonical-url="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/destatis/status/1930869943135842587"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/destatis/status/1930869943135842587}}}\uc2 ">\par \par \uc2 Im April 2025 sind die deutschen {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Exporte?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Exporte}}}\uc2  gegen\u252\'00\'FCber M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2025 nach vorl\u228\'00\'E4ufigen Ergebnissen um 1,7 % gesunken und die {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Importe?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Importe}}}\uc2  um 3,9 % gestiegen. im Vergleich zum April 2024 sanken die Exporte um 2,1 %, w\u228\'00\'E4hrend die Importe um 3,8 % stiegen. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/QasNyrCtPR"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/QasNyrCtPR}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Au%C3%9Fenhandel?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Au\u223\'00\'DFenhandel}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/5240E57mqZ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/5240E57mqZ}}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Statistisches Bundesamt (@destatis) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/destatis/status/1930869943135842587?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 6, 2025}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 Industrial output also weakened, falling by 1.4% month-on-month in April.\par \par \uc2 ING haven\u8217\'20\'19t given up hope for a cyclical rebound in Germany, yet, anyway. Their global head of macro, \uc2 Carsten Brzeski\uc2  , told clients that the ongoing trade tensions will still weigh on German (and European) industry, adding:\par \par \uc2 The recently increased US tariffs on steel, along with the threat of tariffs on pharmaceuticals, highlight that the risk of escalating trade tensions remains very much alive. Compounding these pressures, the stronger euro effectively acts as an additional tariff.\par \par \uc2 And there are more potential impediments to German industry which have nothing to do with tariffs; water levels in Germany\u8217\'20\'19s rivers are currently at almost unprecedentedly low levels for this time of year. Vessels can currently only transport around 50% of their normal cargo.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T06:52:28.823Z">7.52am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Bosses' bonuses banned at six water companies\par \par \uc2 Bonuses for 10 water company executives in England, including the boss of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/thames-water"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Thames Water}}}\uc2 , will be banned with immediate effect over serious sewage pollution, as part of new powers brought in by the Labour government.\par \par \uc2 The top executives of six water companies who have overseen the most serious pollution events will not receive performance rewards this year, the environment said.\par \par \uc2 The companies \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  , \uc2 Anglian Water\uc2  , \uc2 Southern Water\uc2  , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/unitedutilities"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 United}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/unitedutilities"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/unitedutilities"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Utilities}}}\uc2  , \uc2 Wessex Water\uc2  and \uc2 Yorkshire Water\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 are responsible for the most serious category of sewage pollution into rivers and seas, all of which are, or have been, under criminal investigation by the \uc2 Environment Agency\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Under powers in Labour\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/water"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Water}}}\uc2  (Special Measures) Act 2025, the regulator, \uc2 Ofwat\uc2 , is now able to ban bonuses for water executives where a company fails to meet key standards on environmental and financial performance, or is convicted of a criminal offence. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jun/05/bonuses-banned-for-10-english-water-bosses-over-sewage-pollution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bonuses banned for 10 English water bosses over sewage pollution}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T06:42:55.313Z">7.42am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 House prices: what the experts say\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s some early reaction to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/jun/06/uk-house-prices-drop-in-may-markets-us-jobs-report-musk-tesla-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-684286368f088672b8b66461#block-684286368f088672b8b66461"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the news that UK house prices dipped by 0.4% last month}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tom Bill , head of UK residential research at estate agent Knight Frank :\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDemand was frontloaded this year thanks to April\u8217\'20\'19s stamp duty deadline, which means house prices are coming under downwards pressure as buyers still in the market have a lot to choose from.\par \par \uc2 While activity will eventually pick up, concerns around inflation and the government\u8217\'20\'19s tight financial headroom mean mortgage rates don\u8217\'20\'19t feel poised to drop meaningfully. We expect UK growth of 3.5% in 2025, which suggests the direction of travel for prices will be largely sideways.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Handford, managing director at national estate agent group {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fineandcountry.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fine & Country}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis slight month-on-month dip follows the stamp duty changes introduced in April and comes just ahead of the typically quieter summer period, when many families pause moving plans to focus on holidays and school breaks.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAlthough economic pressures continue to impact personal finances, with inflation at 3.5% and household budgets feeling the squeeze, the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s May rate cut to 4.25% has offered some welcome relief. While mortgage rates remain relatively high, any further easing in borrowing costs could help reignite market activity.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMortgage approvals fell in April as demand naturally cooled after the stamp duty tax break ended, and tighter lending criteria and deposit requirements still pose challenges for many buyers, particularly first-time purchasers. However, steady wage growth is providing some support, even if affordability remains a hurdle. <figure class="element element-tweet" data-canonical-url="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/emmafildes/status/1930870908047987142"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/emmafildes/status/1930870908047987142}}}\uc2 ">\par \par \uc2 In this month\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/HalifaxBank?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @HalifaxBank}}}\uc2  HPI for May 2025 house prices took two steps back after taking two steps forward in April. Falling 0.4% to \u163\'00\'A3296,648. This game of cat and mouse looks set to continue throughout the year, with rates determining the outcome. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/1RKSpp4OAS"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/1RKSpp4OAS}}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Emma Fildes (@emmafildes) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/emmafildes/status/1930870908047987142?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 6, 2025}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 Matt Swannell , chief economic advisor to the \uc2 EY\uc2  ITEM Club :\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAfter a strong start to 2025, the housing market lost momentum as March\u8217\'20\'19s change in stamp duty thresholds came into view and passed. Having spiked in March, housing transactions slowed sharply in April after homebuyers had rushed to complete transactions in the nick of time. This soft patch probably has a little further to go. Mortgage approvals, which lead house purchases by a couple of months, have sunk through the first four months of this year. Earlier changes to stamp duty thresholds in 2021 also led to a temporary drop-off in housing activity.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe think that the current weakness will prove temporary and that the conditions are in place for a modest pickup in the housing market later in 2025. Further interest rate cuts and real pay gains will support demand. But with house prices remaining high, affordability challenges and ongoing economic uncertainty will temper activity.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T06:32:58.076Z">7.32am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Northern Ireland continues to lead annual price growth in the UK\par \par \uc2 Halifax\u8217\'20\'19s report also shows that house prices are rising faster in Northern Ireland, Wales and Scotland than in England.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the details:\par \par \uc2 Northern Ireland once again recorded the fastest pace of annual property price inflation, up by +8.6% over the past year. The typical home now costs \u163\'00\'A3209,388, though prices remain well below the UK average.\par \par \uc2 Wales and Scotland also posted strong annual growth of +4.8% in May. Average prices now stand at \u163\'00\'A3230,405 and \u163\'00\'A3214,864 respectively.\par \par \uc2 Among the English regions, the North West and Yorkshire and the Humber lead the way, both showing annual house price growth of +3.7%. Average property values in these areas are now \u163\'00\'A3240,823 and \u163\'00\'A3213,983 respectively.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, London continues to see more subdued growth, with prices rising by just +1.2% year-onyear. However, the capital remains by far the most expensive part of the UK housing market, with the average home now priced at \u163\'00\'A3542,017.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T06:23:39.391Z">7.23am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 UK house prices dip in May\par \par \uc2 British house prices fell by more than expected in May, new figures from mortgage lender Halifax showed on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Halifax said house prices fell by 0.4% in May, more than reversing a 0.3% increase in April. Economists had only expected a fall of 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 According to Halifax , the average property price was \u163\'00\'A3296,648 last month, down from \u163\'00\'A3297,798 in April.\par \par \uc2 On an annual basis, house prices were 2.5% higher on the year \u8211\'20\'13 again less than expected.\par \par \uc2 Amanda Bryden , head of mortgages at Halifax , says the broader picture is that the housing market that has remained largely stable in 2025, with average prices down by just -0.2% since the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 Bryden adds:\par \par \uc2 The market appears to have absorbed the temporary surge in activity over spring, which was driven by the changes to stamp duty.\par \par \uc2 Affordability remains a challenge, with house prices still high relative to incomes. However, lower mortgage rates and steady wage growth have helped support buyer confidence.\par \par \uc2 The outlook will depend on the pace of cuts to interest rates, as well as the strength of future income growth and broader inflation trends. Despite ongoing pressure on household finances and a stilluncertain economic backdrop, the housing market has shown resilience \u8211\'20\'13 a story we expect to continue in the months ahead.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-06-06T07:37:33.868Z">at 8.37am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-06-06T06:23:14.551Z">7.23am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Markets brace for US jobs report, after explosive Trump-Musk row\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 Investors will have one eye on the US jobs market today, and the other on the spectacular blow-up between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  overnight.\par \par \uc2 The latest US employment report is expected to show a slowdown in hiring across the US in May.\par \par \uc2 Economists forecast that the US non-farm payroll will have risen by around 130,000 in May, down from the 177,000 increase recorded in April, with the unemployment rate sticking at 4.2%. <figure class="element element-tweet" data-canonical-url="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MikeZaccardi/status/1930568463191314576"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/MikeZaccardi/status/1930568463191314576}}}\uc2 ">\par \par \uc2 Median NFP estimate: 126k {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/imJpaGE3pH"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/imJpaGE3pH}}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Mike Zaccardi, CFA, CMT (@MikeZaccardi) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MikeZaccardi/status/1930568463191314576?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 5, 2025}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 A weak payrolls report could fuel fears that the US economy is slowing, as Trump\u8217\'20\'19s trade wars hit activity. But it could also intensify the pressure on the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  to lower interest rates, something the US president has been demanding for months.\par \par \uc2 Tony Sycamore , market analyst at IG , explains:\par \par \uc2 The US unemployment rate has hovered between 4.0% and 4.2% over the past year, and a job in the unemployment rate to 4.3% or higher will heighten economic slowdown fears. The US rates market is pricing in an 85% chance of a 25bp Fed rate cut in September, with a cumulative 55bp in cuts expected by year-end.\par \par \uc2 So the markets could be volatile at 1.30pm UK time, when the non-farm payroll data lands.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of volatility\u8230\'20\'26 the Trump-Musk relationship exploded dramatically on Thursday, with the president and the world\u8217\'20\'19s richest person slinging accusations at each other. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jun/05/trump-elon-musk-fallout"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Impeachment, Epstein and bitter acrimony: Trump and Musk joust in astonishing social media duel}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 Tesla\uc2  slumped over 14%, wiping over $150bn off the company\u8217\'20\'19s value, as Trump threatened to terminate Musk\u8217\'20\'19s governmental subsidies and contracts, and accused the billionaire of going \u8220\'20\'1CCRAZY!\u8221\'20\'1D over the removal of electric car subsidies.\par \par \uc2 From the other corner, Musk called for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s impeachment, claimed the president appeared in the files into convicted sex offender \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 , and briefly threatened to decommission \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Dragon spacecraft.\par \par \uc2 It all added up to another bruising day for shareholders in \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , whose value had already been hit by the backlack against Musk\u8217\'20\'19s role in the Trump Administration. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/jun/05/tesla-share-drop-trump-musk-feud"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tesla share plunge amid Trump feud wipes $152bn off Elon Musk\u8217\'20\'19s company}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 Some traders will have been betting on further falls in \uc2 Tesla\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s share price, as \uc2 Chris Weston\uc2  , head of research at Pepperstone , explains:\par \par \uc2 The selling in \uc2 Tesla\uc2  stock on the day has been wholly impressive with 285m shares traded on the day \u8211\'20\'13 the most since Jan 2023 \u8211\'20\'13 with a \u8216\'20\'18sell first, ask questions later\u8217\'20\'19 mentality sweeping through the shareholder base.\par \par \uc2 In the options space, over 4m put options traded hands, 4x the 20-day average.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 7am BST: Halifax house price index\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9am BST: \uc2 UN FAO\uc2  food price index\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am BST: Eurozone GDP report for Q1 2025 (3rd estimate)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 11.30am BST: Bank of Russia interest rate decision\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.30pm BST: US non-farm payroll report\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-06-06T08:00:35.967Z">at 9.00am BST</time> \par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250606el66000xk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
